CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to prescribe, in accordance with the Standards and Recommended Practices adopted in the Annexes to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, measures for the safe and efficient air navigation of aircraft, light aircraft and ultra-light vehicles, and matters concerning the duties and other obligations of the State, aviation business operators, aviation personnel and other persons. <Amended on August 27, 2019>

Article 2 (Definitions)
The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on August 27, 2019; May 18, 2021; April 18, 2023; March 19, 2024; May 27, 2025; December 30, 2025>
1. "Aircraft" means any machine that can derive support in the atmosphere from the reactions of the air other than the reactions of the air against the earth’s surface. It includes the following machines that meet the criteria prescribed by Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, including maximum take-off weight and seating capacity, and any other machine prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act:
a. Aeroplane;
b. Helicopter;
c. Airship;
d. Glider;
2. "Light aircraft" means aeroplanes, helicopters, gyroplanes, powered parachutes, etc., meeting the standards prescribed by Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the maximum takeoff weight and the number of seats, which are machines capable of flying by capitalizing on the reaction of the air, other than aircraft;
3. "Ultra-light vehicle" means a device, other than an aircraft or light aircraft, that can derive support in the atmosphere from reactions of the air and that meets the criteria prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, including empty weight and seating capacity, such as a powered flying device, hang-glider, paraglider, balloon or unmanned aircraft;
4. "Aircraft of State agencies, etc." means an aircraft owned or leased by the State, a local government, or a public institution prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act from among public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as a "State agency, etc."), and used to perform any of the following duties; provided, that military, police or customs aircraft are excluded:
a. Search and rescue in response to disasters, accidents or similar events;
b. Suppression and prevention of wildfire;
c. Rescue and emergency medical services, including the transport of emergency patients;
d. Other duties necessary to ensure public security and to maintain public order;
5. "Aviation service" means any of the following services:
a. The operation of an aircraft, including the operation of its radio equipment, but excluding flight practice under Article 46;
b. The provision of air traffic control services;
c. Flight dispatch duties;
d. Determining whether an aircraft, engine, or propeller (hereinafter collectively referred to as "aircraft, engine, or propeller"), appliance, or part that has undergone maintenance, repair, or alteration (hereinafter referred to as "maintenance, etc.") is airworthy, and verifying maintenance performed on a light aircraft or its appliances or parts;
5-2. "Air traffic control service" means any of the following services using wire or radio communications or other means to prevent collisions between aircraft (including light aircraft; hereinafter the same shall apply in this subparagraph) or between aircraft and obstructions, and to expedite and maintain an orderly flow of air traffic; provided, that air traffic control training under Article 47 is excluded:
a. Aerodrome control service: A service of controlling the movement of aircraft operating on the manoeuvring area (meaning taxiways and runways) of an aerodrome or airport and in the surrounding airspace, and issuing instructions or guidance/advice relating to air traffic and safety, or providing information, to such aircraft;
b. Approach control service: A service of controlling the movement of aircraft departing from or arriving at an aerodrome or airport, and issuing instructions or guidance/advice relating to air traffic and safety, or providing information, to such aircraft;
c. Area control service: A service of controlling the movement of aircraft flying in a control area (excluding a control area in which approach control service is provided), and issuing instructions or guidance/advice relating to air traffic and safety, or providing information, to such aircraft;
d. Services prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, including services supporting those specified in items (a) through (c);
6. "Accident" means an occurrence associated with the operation of an aircraft, from the time any person boards the aircraft with the intention of flight until all such persons have disembarked [or, in the case of an aircraft operated without a person on board by remote control or other means (hereinafter referred to as an "unmanned aircraft"), from the time the aircraft moves with the intention of flight until the flight ends and its engine is shut down], that falls under any of the following and is prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
a. Death, serious injury or disappearance of a person;
b. Damage or structural failure of the aircraft;
c. The aircraft is missing or completely inaccessible;
7. "Light aircraft accident" means any of the following occurrences prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, which takes place from the moment the engine of the light aircraft starts for the purpose of flight until the moment its engine stops because the flight has ended:
a. Death, serious injury or disappearance of a person caused by the light aircraft;
b. Crash, collision or fire involving the light aircraft;
c. The light aircraft is missing or completely inaccessible;
8.  "Ultra-light vehicle accident" means an occurrence that takes place from the time an ultra-light vehicle takes off (including taking off from water; hereinafter the same shall apply) with the intention of flight until it lands (including landing on water; hereinafter the same shall apply), that falls under any of the following and is prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
a. Death, serious injury or disappearance of a person caused by the ultra-light vehicle;
b. Crash, collision or fire involving the ultra-light vehicle;
c. The ultra-light vehicle is missing or completely inaccessible;
9. "Serious incident" means an occurrence prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act that seriously endangered aviation safety and involved circumstances indicating that there was a high probability of an accident;
10. "Incident" means an occurrence, other than an accident or serious incident, associated with the operation of an aircraft that affects or could affect the safety of operation;
10-2."Hazard" means a circumstance, condition, physical or human factor, or other factor with the potential to cause an accident, serious incident or incident, or to increase the likelihood thereof;
10-3. "Safety risk" means the predicted probability and severity of the consequences or outcomes of a hazard that could compromise aviation safety;
10-4. "Safety data" means any of the following data used to maintain or improve aviation safety or for other related purposes:
a. Reports on failures, defects, or malfunctions which have occurred to aircraft, engine, or propeller under Article 33;
b. Flight data and the results of analyzing such data under Article 58(4);
c. Radar data and the results of analyzing such data under Article 58(5);
d. Data reported under Article 59 or 61;
e. Results of an investigation under Article 60 of this Act and Article 19 of the Aviation and Railway Accident Investigation Act;
f. Data collected in the course of aviation safety activities under Article 132 and reports on the results of such activities;
g. Information on meteorological services prescribed in Article 12 of the Weather Act;
h. Data and other material collected and managed for aviation safety management by an airport operator defined in subparagraph 34 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act (hereinafter referred to as an "airport operator");
i. Information managed by a system established under the subparagraphs of Article 6(1) of the Aviation Business Act;
j. Information, statistics and other data collected in the performance of duties under Article 68(4) of the Aviation Business Act;
k. Data shared with the Republic of Korea for aviation safety by international organizations, foreign governments or other entities;
l. Other data prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
10-5. "Safety information" means safety data processed, organized or analysed for use in safety management;
11. "Flight information region (FIR)" means airspace whose name and vertical and horizontal limits are designated and published by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in accordance with the Convention on International Civil Aviation and its Annexes, for the provision of information necessary for the safe and efficient flight of aircraft, light aircraft and ultra-light vehicles and for search and rescue;
12. "Territorial airspace" means the portion of the atmosphere controlled by the Republic of Korea above its territory, and its inland waters and territorial waters under the Territorial Sea and Contiguous Zone Act;
13. "Airway" means a route through airspace, delineated on the surface of the Earth, that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has designated as suitable for the air navigation of aircraft, light aircraft or ultra-light vehicles;
14. "Aviation personnel" means a person holding a personnel licence under Article 34(1);
15. "Flight simulation training device (FSTD)" means a device prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act that replicates or simulates the flight deck of an aircraft;
16. "Flight crew member" means a person who holds a personnel licence specified in any of subparagraphs 1 through 6 of Article 35 and performs aviation duties on board an aircraft;
17. "Cabin crew member" means a person who performs duties on board an aircraft for passenger safety, including the emergency evacuation of passengers;
18. "Instrument flight" means a flight conducted by determining the attitude, altitude, position and direction of flight of an aircraft solely by reference to instruments installed in the aircraft;
19. "Instrument flight rules (IFR)" means rules under which a person conducting an instrument flight operates in accordance with the sequence and timing of movement, take-off and landing and the method of flight instructed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 84(1) or by a person holding an air traffic services certificate under Article 85(1) (hereinafter referred to as an "air traffic services certificate");
20. "Fatigue risk management system (FRMS)" means a system that continuously monitors and manages fatigue-related safety risks, based on operational experience and scientific principles and knowledge, so that flight crew members and cabin crew members can perform their duties at adequate levels of alertness;
21. "Aerodrome" means an aerodrome referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act;
22. "Airport" means an airport under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act;
23. "Airport facilities" means airport facilities under subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act;
24. "Air navigation facilities" means the air navigation facilities referred to in subparagraph 15 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act;
24-2. "Air traffic management (ATM)" means the integrated management of air traffic and airspace in a safe and efficient manner, comprising the following functions:
a. Designationof airspace and any other related activities under Article 78;
b. Air traffic services (ATS) under Article 83;
c. Air traffic flow management (ATFM) under Article 83-2;
24-3. "Air traffic data" means the following data necessary for air traffic management:
a. Data, such as aircraft flight information, that are mutually shared and exchanged with foreign governments or international organizations for air traffic management;
b. Data generated and managed for airspace management by air traffic control units or other entities;
c. Radar data and the results of analyzing such data under Article 58(5);
d. Flight plans to be prepared and submitted by persons who intend to operate aircraft under subparagraph 4 of Article 67(2) and data related thereto;
e. Data necessary for air traffic flow management under Article 83-2;
f. Data necessary to provide aeronautical information and aeronautical charts under Article 89;
g. Data generated and used in facilities that are related to the take-off and landing of aircraft, among airport facilities, and that are prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act such as runways;
h. Data, including aircraft position, generated and used by radio navigation aids defined in subparagraph 17 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act;
i. Data produced by airport operators for the management of aircraft operations, including aircraft taxi times and air traffic statistics;
j. Data on slots under Article 18 of the Aviation Business Act;
k. Aeronautical meteorological data, including aeronautical meteorological forecasts and warnings under Article 14-2 of the Weather Act.
25. "Control zone" means airspace over and around an aerodrome or airport that is designated and published by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the safety of air traffic;
26. "Control area" means airspace extending upwards from 200 meters above the surface of the earth or water that is designated and published by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the safety of air traffic;
26-2. "Air traffic control unit" means a facility prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as an aerodrome control tower, approach control unit or area control center, that is established to provide air traffic control service;
27. "Air transport service" means air transport services referred to in subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
28. "Air transport operator" means an air transport operator referred to in subparagraph 8 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
29. "Aerial work" means aircraft use business referred to in subparagraph 15 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
30. "Aerial work operator" means an aircraft use business entity referred to in subparagraph 16 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
31. "Aircraft maintenance service provider" means an aircraft maintenance service provider referred to in subparagraph 18 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
32. "Ultra-light vehicle use business" means ultra-light vehicle use business under subparagraph 23 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
33. "Ultra-light vehicle use business entity" means an ultra-light vehicle use business entity referred to in subparagraph 24 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act;
34. "Airfield" means an airfield referred to in subparagraph 19 of Article 2 of the Airport Facilities Act.

Article 3 (Special provisions applicable to military aircraft)
(1) This Act shall not apply to military aircraft and a person engaged in aviation duties related thereto.
(2) This Act shall not apply to aircraft used for customs services or police services and a person engaged in aviation duties related thereto; provided, for the prevention of accidents, such as a mid-air collision, Articles 51 and 67, subparagraph 5 of Article 68, and Articles 79 and 84(1) shall apply thereto.
(3) Paragraph (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to aircraft used by the United States of America and a person engaged in aviation duties related thereto pursuant to Article 4 of the Mutual Defense Treaty between the United States and the Republic of Korea.

Article 4 (Special provisions applicable to aircraft of State agencies)
(1) This Act (excluding Articles 66, 69 through 73, and 132) shall apply to aircraft of State agencies and a person engaged in aviation duties related thereto.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), Articles 53 and 67, subparagraphs 1 through 3 of Article 68, and subparagraph 7 of Article 77(1), and Articles 79 and 84(1) shall not apply where aircraft of State agencies are urgently operated (including training) for search and rescue due to disasters, accidents, etc., fire suppression, evacuation of patients with acute illnesses or injuries that require immediate medical attention, and other public purposes prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where Articles 59, 61, and 62(5) and (6) apply to aircraft of State agencies the "Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport" shall be construed as the "head of a competent administrative agency." In such cases, the head of a competent administrative agency shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport that the head has received reports pursuant to Articles 59, 61, and 62(5) and (6).

Article 5 (Transfer of certain functions and duties in respect of leased aircraft)
Where an aircraft registered in a foreign State is leased to and operated by an operator of the Republic of Korea, or an aircraft registered in the Republic of Korea is leased to and operated by an operator of a foreign State, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall prescribe and publish provisions governing the transfer of State functions and duties relating to the operation of the leased aircraft, in accordance with the Convention on International Civil Aviation.

Article 6 (Establishment of the master plan for aviation safety policies)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish master plans for national aviation safety policies (hereinafter referred to as "master plan for aviation safety policies") every five years.
(2) A master plan for aviation safety policies shall include the following:
1. Objectives and strategies of aviation safety policies;
2. Matters concerning accident prevention and safe operation of aircraft, light aircraft, and ultra-light vehicles;
3. Matters concerning a certification system of the manufacture, maintenance, and safety of an aircraft, light aircraft, and ultra-light vehicles;
4. Matters concerning the management of flight information regions and airways and the improvement of an air traffic system;
5. Matters concerning training and management of qualification of aviation personnel;
6. Any other requirements to improve aviation safety.
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to establish or modify a master plan for aviation safety policies, the Minister may request the heads of the relevant administrative agencies to provide necessary cooperation.
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport establishes or modifies a master plan for aviation safety policies, the Minister shall give public notice of the details thereof in the Official Gazette and inform the heads of the relevant administrative agencies whom the Minister has requested to provide cooperation pursuant to paragraph (3) of the details thereof.
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish annual action plans to implement a master plan for aviation safety policies.

Article 6-2 (Aviation Safety Day)
(1) In order to raise public awareness of aviation safety, enhance social interest therein, and promote a safety culture in aviation, the State shall designate December 29 of each year as Aviation Safety Day
(2) The State and local governments may conduct events and other projects consistent with the purpose of Aviation Safety Day.
(3) Requirements for the conduct of events and other projects related to Aviation Safety Day under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 27, 2025]

CHAPTER II AIRCRAFT REGISTRATION

Article 7 (Aircraft registration)
(1) Any person who owns or leases an aircraft and thereby has the right to use the aircraft (hereinafter referred to as "owner") shall file for registration of the aircraft with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, the same shall not apply to aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(2) Where a domestic air transport operator or an international air transport operator who obtains an air operator certificate (AOC) under Article 90(1) intends to file for registration of an aircraft pursuant to paragraph (1), the air transport operator shall have necessary maintenance personnel to ensure the safe operation of the relevant aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on June 9, 2020>

Article 8 (Acquisition of nationality of aircraft)
An aircraft registered pursuant to Article 7 shall acquire the nationality of the Republic of Korea and shall have the subsequent rights and obligations.

Article 9 (Effect of aircraft registration on ownership)
(1) The acquisition, loss, or modification of ownership of an aircraft shall come into effect upon registration.
(2) The right to lease an aircraft shall come into effect against a third party upon registration.

Article 10 (Restrictions on aircraft registration)
(1) No aircraft owned or leased by any of the following persons shall be registered; provided, the same shall not apply to an aircraft which the citizens or corporations of the Republic of Korea have the right to lease and use: <Amended on December 7, 2021>
1. A person other than a citizen of the Republic of Korea;
2. A foreign government or foreign public organization;
3. A foreign corporation or organization;
4. A corporation, at least 1/2 of shares or equity stakes of which are owned by a person falling under any of subparagraphs 1 through 3 or whose business is substantially controlled by such person (limited to the registration of an aircraft for purposes of aviation business defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act);
5. A corporation whose representative in its corporation registration certificate is a foreigner or in which foreigners account for at least 1/2 of the number of its executive officers in the corporation registration certificate.
(2) Notwithstanding the proviso of paragraph (1), no aircraft with the foreign nationality shall be registered.

Article 11 (Particulars to be recorded in the aircraft register)
(1) Where an aircraft has been registered pursuant to Article 7, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall record the following in the original register:
1. The type of the aircraft;
2. The manufacturer of the aircraft;
3. The manufacturing number of the aircraft;
4. A place where the aircraft is parked;
5. The name or title, address, and nationality of the owner, lessor, or lessee;
6. The date of registration;
7. Registration mark.
(2) Except as provided in paragraph (1), any other requirements for the registration of an aircraft shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 12 (Issuance of aircraft registration certificates)
When an aircraft has been registered pursuant to Article 7, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue an aircraft registration certificate to the person who registered the aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 13 (Changes in aircraft registration)
Where any changes are made to the particulars of registration referred to in subparagraphs 4 or 5 of Article 11(1), the owner shall file an application for changes in registration with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport within 15 days from the date on which those particulars change, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 14 (Transfer of aircraft registration)
A person who intends to transfer or take over ownership of an aircraft or the right to lease the aircraft registered shall file an application for registration of transfer with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport within 15 days from the date a reason for the transfer or takeover arises, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 15 (De-registration of aircraft)
(1) Where a registered aircraft falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the owner shall apply to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for de-registration, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, within 15 days from the date the relevant ground arises:
1. Where the aircraft is destroyed or dismantled (excluding any disassembly for maintenance, transportation, or storage);
2. Where the whereabouts of the aircraft is unknown for at least one month (in cases of an accident, two months);
3. Where the owner transfers or leases the aircraft to a person falling under any subparagraph of Article 10(1) (in the case of a lease, only where the aircraft acquires foreign nationality);
4. Where the right to use the aircraft lapses due to the expiration of the period of lease.
(2) Where the owner fails to apply for de-registration of an aircraft pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall notify the owner to apply for de-registration for a specified period of at least seven days.
(3) Where the owner fails to apply for de-registration of an aircraft even after being notified under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall de-register the aircraft by authority and notify the owner and other interested parties thereof.
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has registered the cancellation of an aircraft pursuant to paragraph (1) or (3), the Minister shall issue a certificate of cancellation of registration, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on January 9, 2024>

Article 16 (Certified copies, extracts of the aircraft register)
(1) Any person may request the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to issue a certified copy or certified extract of the original register of an aircraft or to permit that person to peruse the original register of the aircraft.
(2) In receipt of a request pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue the relevant material or permit any person to peruse the original register of the aircraft unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.

Article 17 (Attachment and protection of the aircraft identification plate)
(1) Upon registration of an aircraft, the owner shall attach an identification plate on the aircraft in accordance with the type, location, method, etc. prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) No person shall destroy the identification plate attached on an aircraft pursuant to paragraph (1).

Article 18 (Display of nationality, registration marks and owner identification)
(1) No person shall operate an aircraft on which the nationality, registration marks, and the name or title of the owner are not displayed; provided, the same shall not apply to aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as newly manufactured aircraft.
(2) Requirements for displaying nationality and other markings and for the composition of a registration mark under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

CHAPTER III AIRWORTHINESS

Article 19 (Airworthiness Standards)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall determine and publicly notify Airworthiness Standards including the following (hereinafter referred to as "Airworthiness Standards") in order to ensure the safety of aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts:
1. Airworthiness criteria for aircraft, engine, propeller;
2. Environmental standards for aircraft, engine, propeller(including Fuel venting and Exhaust Emission Requirements and Noise certification);
3. Standards for continuing airworthiness of aircraft, engine, propeller;
4. Identification and Registration Marking for aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts;
5. Certification Procedures for aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts.

Article 20 (Type Certification)
(1) A person who intends to have the design of an aircraft, engine, or propeller certified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, file an application for certification under either subparagraph of paragraph (2). The same shall also apply where any certified matter is changed. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(2) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall examine the application to ascertain whether the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller conforms to the Airworthiness Standards for aircraft, engine, or propeller and shall grant the certification under the following subparagraphs: <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
1. Type certification: If the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller is designed according to the Airworthiness Standards;
2. Restricted type certification: If the applicant proves that either of the following aircraft is designed according to the Airworthiness Standards related to the services of the relevant aircraft and that the aircraft can be operated safely to the extent of operation proposed by the applicant;
a. An aircraft used for any of the specific services prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as wildfire suppression, and search and rescue (excluding the aircraft prescribed in item b);
b. An aircraft manufactured with type certification under subparagraph 1 of Article 4(5) of the Military Aircraft Airworthiness Certification Act which is modified to provide specific services prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as wildfire suppression, and search and rescue.
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants type certification under subparagraph 1 of paragraph (2) (hereinafter referred to as "type certification") or the restricted type certification under subparagraph 2 of paragraph (2) (hereinafter referred to as "restricted type certification"), the Minister shall issue a type certificate or a restricted type certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(4) Any person who intends to transfer or acquire a type certificate or a restricted type certificate shall report the transfer thereof to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and shall file an application for reissuance of the relevant certificate with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(5) Any person who intends to obtain supplemental type certification to modify the design of an aircraft, engine, or propeller for which type certification, restricted type certification, or Type Certificate Validation under Article 21 has already been granted (hereinafter referred to as "supplemental type certification") shall file an application for supplemental type certification with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants supplemental type certification, the Minister shall issue a supplemental type certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
(7) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct surveillance activities, on a regular or occasional basis, to determine whether the holder of a type certificate, restricted type certificate, or supplemental type certificate maintains the certified matters, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(8) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport finds, as a result of surveillance activities under paragraph (7), any matter that may endanger safe operation of aircraft, the Minister may order the holder of a type certificate, restricted type certificate, or supplemental type certificate to take corrective actions as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(9) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, in any of the following cases,revoke type certification, restricted type certification, or supplemental type certification of the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller, or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, the Minister shall cancel type certification, restricted type certification, or supplemental type certification, in cases falling under subparagraph 1: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder obtained the type certificate, restricted type certificate, or supplemental type certificate by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where an aircraft, engine, or propeller ceases to conform to the Airworthiness Standards applicable as at the time type certification, restricted type certification, or supplemental type certification was granted;
3. Where the holder fails to comply with an order to take corrective actions under paragraph (8);
4. Where the holder falls under any of the following items, in violation of Article 58(2):
a. Where the holder fails to establish an aviation safety management system under Article 58(2) (hereinafter referred to as "safety management system") before the holder begins services;
b. Where the holder establishes and maintains a safety management system without obtaining approval;
c. Where the holder establishes and maintains a safety management system differently from the details thereof approved;
d. Where the holder changes any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act without obtaining approval;
[Title Amended on Dec. 26, 2017]

Article 21 (Type Certificate Validation)
(1) When the holder of a type certificate issued by a foreign government for the design of an aircraft, engine, or propeller intends to obtain approval of compliance with Airworthiness Standards for such aircraft, engine, or propeller  (hereinafter referred to as "Type Certificate Validation"), the holder shall file an application for Type Certificate Validation for each type of aircraft, engine, or propeller with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, in cases of an aircraft referred to in either of the following subparagraphs, such application may include the engines and propellers mounted thereon: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
1. An aeroplane with a maximum takeoff weight not exceeding 5,700 kilograms;
2. A helicopter with a maximum takeoff weight not exceeding 3,175 kilograms.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), an aircraft referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) and the engines and propellers mounted on the aircraft shall be deemed to have obtained Type Certificate Validation under paragraph (1) if the aircraft obtained type certification from a country with whom the Republic of Korea has concluded a Bilateral Aviation Safety Agreement on airworthiness of aircraft, engine, or propeller. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants Type Certificate Validation, the Minister shall inspect whether the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller (excluding aircraft deemed to have obtained Type Certificate Validation under paragraph (2) and the engines and propellers mounted on such aircraft) comply with the Airworthiness Standards; provided, however, that an aircraft, engine, or propeller type-certified by a country that has concluded a Bilateral Aviation Safety Agreement on the airworthiness of aircraft, engine, or propeller with the Republic of Korea may be partially exempted from such inspection, as stipulated in the relevant agreement. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller comply with Airworthiness Standards upon inspection under paragraph (3), the Minister shall issue a written Type Certificate Validation, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(5) Where an aircraft, engine, or propeller which has obtained type certification or Type Certificate Validation obtains supplemental type certification for its design from a foreign government, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may grant Supplemental Type Certificate Validation (hereinafter referred to as "Supplemental Type Certificate Validation"), as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(6) When granting Supplemental Type Certificate Validation, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall inspect whether the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller  comply with the Airworthiness Standards and, if the Minister finds them compliant, shall issue a Supplemental Type Certificate Validation certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, however, that for an aircraft, engine, or propeller granted supplemental type certification by a country that has concluded a Bilateral Aviation Safety Agreement on the airworthiness of aircraft, engine, or propeller with the Republic of Korea, part of the inspection may be omitted as stipulated in the relevant agreement. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(7) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the Type Certificate Validation or the Supplemental Type Certificate Validation of the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller, or may issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke the Type Certificate Validation or the Supplemental Type Certificate Validation: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
1. Where the Type Certificate Validation or Supplemental Type Certificate Validation has been granted by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the aircraft, engine, or propeller fail to comply with Airworthiness Standards applicable as at the time the Type Certificate Validation or Supplemental Type Certificate Validation is granted.

Article 22 (Production Certification)
(1) Where any of the following persons intends to be certified as equipped with technology, facilities, human resources, a quality management system, etc. that enable the person to produce aircraft, engine, or propeller conforming to the design authorized according to type certification, restricted type certification, or supplemental type certification (hereinafter referred to as "production certification"), the applicant shall file an application for production certification with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; May 12, 2026>
1. The holder of a type certificate;
2. The holder of a restricted type certificate;
3. The holder of a supplemental type certificate;
4. A person authorized to use type certification, restricted type certification, or supplemental type certification through a contract with a certificate holder referred to in any of subparagraphs 1 through 3.
(2) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall inspect whether the person who intends to produce aircraft, engine, or propeller is equipped with technology, facilities, human resources, quality management systems, etc. that enable the person to produce aircraft, engine, or propeller conforming to the design authorized according to type certification, restricted type certification, or supplemental type certification. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; May 12, 2026>
(3) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants production certification under paragraph (1), the Minister shall issue a production certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. In such cases, the production certificate shall not be transferred to or acquired by another person. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
(4) Where any reason prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act arises, such as the relocation or expansion of facilities or a change in the quality management system, which could affect the airworthiness of aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts, the holder of a production certificate shall report such event to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct surveillance activities, on a regular or occasional basis, to determine whether the holder of a production certificate maintains the certified matters, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport finds, as a result of surveillance activities under paragraph (5), any matter that may endanger safe operation of aircraft, the Minister may order the holder of a production certificate to take corrective actions as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(7) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, in any of the following cases, revoke production certification or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke the production certification: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder obtained the production certificate by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the aircraft, engine, or propeller fail to comply with Airworthiness Standards applicable as at the time production certification is granted;
3. Where the holder fails to comply with an order to take corrective actions, etc. under paragraph (6);
4. Where the holder falls under any of the following items, in violation of Article 58(2):
a. Where the holder fails to establish a safety management system before the holder begins services;
b. Where the holder establishes and maintains a safety management system without obtaining approval;
c. Where the holder establishes and maintains a safety management system differently from the details thereof approved;
d. Where the holder changes any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act without obtaining approval;

Article 23 (Airworthiness certificate and continuing airworthiness)
(1) A person who intends to obtain certification that an aircraft is airworthy (hereinafter referred to as "airworthiness certificate") shall file an application for airworthiness certificate with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) No aircraft other than aircraft with the nationality of the Republic of Korea shall be certified for airworthiness; provided, the same shall not apply to aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) No person shall operate an aircraft without either of the following certificates: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
1. Standard airworthiness certificate: Certification granted where the relevant aircraft is deemed produced in conformity with the design authorized according to type certification or Type Certificate Validation and capable of safe operation;
2. Special airworthiness certificate: Certification granted where the relevant aircraft is deemed capable of safe operation upon review of the extent of operation proposed by the manufacturer or the owner of the aircraft in any case prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as where the relevant aircraft has been granted restricted type certification or for purposes of research and development of aircraft.
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants an airworthiness certificate falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (3), the Minister shall, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, inspect the conditions and flight performance of the relevant aircraft during the design and production process and after completion and shall designate the operating limitations of the relevant aircraft; provided, if an aircraft falls under any of the following, such inspection, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, may be partially omitted: <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
1. An aircraft granted type certification, restricted type certification, or Type Certificate Validation;
2. An aircraft produced by the holder of a production certificate;
3. An aircraft imported with approval of airworthiness from a foreign government exporting aircraft.
(5) Airworthiness certificate shall remain valid for one year; provided, the period of validity may be extended or shortened in consideration of the type and age of aircraft and the ability of the owner (including a person entrusted under Article 32(2)) to maintain airworthiness, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; Mar. 19, 2024>
(6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that an aircraft is airworthy upon inspection under paragraph (4), the Minister shall issue an airworthiness certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
(7) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the airworthiness certificate of the relevant aircraft or order the suspension of its validity for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the airworthiness certificate in cases falling under subparagraph 1: <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
1. Where airworthiness certificate has been granted by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the aircraft fails to comply with Airworthiness Standards applicable as at the time airworthiness certificate is granted.
(8) The owner who intends to operate an aircraft shall maintain airworthiness of the aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
(9) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct surveillance activities at any time to ascertain whether the owner maintains the airworthiness of the relevant aircraft pursuant to paragraph (8). In addition, the Minister may issue Airworthiness Directives or order maintenance of aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts, to the owner to maintain the airworthiness of the relevant aircraft. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>

Article 24 (Airworthiness approval)
(1) Any person who intends to provide an aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts which are manufactured, operated, or maintained in the Republic of Korea, to other persons may obtain Airworthiness Approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that the relevant aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts meet the Airworthiness Standards or the Standards for Technical Standard Order Authorization referred to in Article 27(1) and that the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller is in a condition for safe operation, the Minister shall grant Airworthiness Approval referred to in paragraph (1).
(3) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the airworthiness approval under paragraph (2) or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the airworthiness approval, in cases falling under subparagraph 1:
1. Where the airworthiness approval has been granted by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts fail to meet the Airworthiness Standards or the standards for Technical Standard Order Authorization referred to in Article 27(1) applicable as at the time the approval of airworthiness is granted.

Article 25 (Noise Certification)
(1) Where the owner of an aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act obtains an airworthiness certificate, or where the noise level of the aircraft changes due to repair, modification or other work, the owner shall obtain certification of compliance with the noise standards under subparagraph 2 of Article 19 (hereinafter referred to as "Noise Certification") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) No person shall operate an aircraft that has failed to obtain Noise Certification or has failed to meet Airworthiness Standards; provided, however, that this paragraph shall not apply where the person operating the aircraft is the holder of flight permission granted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke Noise Certification or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke Noise Certification:
1. Where the holder obtained Noise Certification by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the aircraft fails to meet the Airworthiness Standards applicable as at the time the Noise Certification is granted.

Article 26 (Order to comply with amended Airworthiness Standards)
Where, as a result of an amendment of the provisions in the Airworthiness Standards, an aircraft for which a type certificate has been issued fails to meet the amended standard, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require the holder of the type certificate or the owner of the aircraft to comply with the amended Airworthiness Standards. In such cases, the certificate holder or the owner of the aircraft shall comply with that requirement.

Article 27 (Technical Standard Order Authorization)
(1) A person who intends to design and manufacture components (excluding cases where components are designed and manufactured for experimental or research and development purposes; hereinafter referred to as "Technical Standard Order articles") determined and  notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in order to ensure airworthiness of aircraft, engine, or propeller shall obtain Technical Standard Order Authorization for the design and manufacture of the relevant articles (hereinafter referred to as "Technical Standard Order Authorization") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in accordance with standards for Technical Standard Order Authorization (hereinafter referred to as "standards for Technical Standard Order Authorization") determined and notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, Technical Standard Order articles prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, which have obtained type approval from a country that has entered into the Bilateral Aviation Safety Agreement concerning Technical Standard Order Authorization with the Republic of Korea, shall be deemed to have obtained Technical Standard Order Authorization.
(2) When granting Technical Standard Order Authorization, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall inspect whether the design and manufacture of the Technical Standard Order articles conform to the standards for Technical Standard Order Authorization and, if the Minister finds them compliant, shall issue a Technical Standard Order Authorization certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) No person shall manufacture or sell Technical Standard Order articles that have failed to obtain Technical Standard Order Authorization, or use such articles on an aircraft, engine, or propeller
(4) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the relevant Technical Standard Order Authorization or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the Technical Standard Order Authorization:
1. Where Technical Standard Order Authorization has been granted by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where Technical Standard Order articles fail to meet standards for Technical Standard Order Authorization applicable as at the time the Technical Standard Order Authorization is granted.

Article 28 (Parts Manufacturer Approval)
(1) Any person who intends to manufacture components or parts to be used on aircraft, engine, or propeller shall obtain certification (hereinafter referred to as "Parts Manufacturer Approval") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport with regard to whether the applicant has human resources, facilities, technology, inspection systems, etc. necessary for manufacturing components or parts in compliance with Airworthiness Standards, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, the same shall not apply where the applicant intends to manufacture any of the following components or parts:
1. components or parts of the same kinds manufactured by a manufacturer of components or parts installed as at the time type certification or supplemental type certification, or Type Certificate Validation or Supplemental Type Certificate Validation is granted;
2. Technical Standard Order articles manufactured after obtaining Technical Standard Order Authorization;
3. Other components or parts prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) When granting Parts Manufacturer Approval, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue a Parts Manufacturer Approval certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, where the Minister deems that components or parts manufactured by a parts manufacturer meet the Airworthiness Standards after inspecting whether the parts manufacturer can manufacture components or parts in compliance with Airworthiness Standards.
(3) No person shall manufacture or sell components or parts that have failed to obtain Parts Manufacturer Approval or use such components or parts on aircraft, engine, or propeller.
(4) The holder of a Parts Manufacturer Approval issued by a country that has entered into the Bilateral Aviation Safety Agreement with the Republic of Korea shall be deemed to have obtained Parts Manufacturer Approval.
(5) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke Parts Manufacturer Approval or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke Parts Manufacturer Approval:
1. Where Parts Manufacturer Approval has been granted by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where components or parts fail to meet the Airworthiness Standards applicable as at the time Parts Manufacturer Approval is granted.

Article 29 (Imposition of penalty surcharges)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport orders the suspension of the effect of type certification, restricted type certification, supplemental type certification, production certification, Technical Standard Order Authorization, or Parts Manufacturer Approval in accordance with Article 20(9), 22(7), 27(4), or 28(5), and such suspension is likely to cause serious inconvenience to users, etc. of aircraft or undermine the public interest, the Minister may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 100 million won in lieu of the suspension of the effect of such certification or approval. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; May 12, 2026>
(2) Specific criteria and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges under paragraph (1), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person liable to pay a penalty surcharge in accordance with paragraph (1) fails to make payment by the deadline, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected.

Article 30 (Approval of repair and modification)
(1) Where the owner of an aircraft that has obtained an airworthiness certificate intends to repair or modify the relevant aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts to the extent prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, the owner, etc. shall obtain approval regarding whether such repair or modification meets Airworthiness Standards (hereinafter referred to as "approval of repair or modification") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) No owner shall operate an aircraft, engine, or propeller that has failed to obtain approval of repair or modification, or use components or parts that have failed to obtain approval of repair or modification on aircraft, engine, or propeller.
(3) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), in any of the following cases, where the repair or modification meets Airworthiness Standards, approval of repair or modification shall be deemed granted:
1. Where the holder of a Technical Standard Order Authorization repairs or modifies Technical Standard Order articles manufactured by the holder;
2. Where the holder of a Parts Manufacturer Approval repairs or modifies components or parts manufactured by the holder;
3. Where the holder of a maintenance organization approval under Article 97(1) repairs or modifies aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts.

Article 31 (Inspections for aircraft, product and organization approvals)
(1) When granting a certification or approval under Articles 20 through 25, Article 27, Article 28, or Article 30, or approved maintenance organization (AMO) certification under Article 97, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall, in advance and as determined by the Minister, inspect the relevant aircraft, engine, or propeller, and appliances, or inspect the organization, facilities, human resources, etc. for manufacturing or maintaining them.
(2) In order to conduct an inspection referred to in paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall appoint or commission persons who inspect aircraft, engine, or propeller, and components (hereinafter referred to as "inspector") from among any of the following persons:
1. Holders of an aircraft maintenance technician licence under subparagraph 8 of Article 35;
2. Persons who have obtained qualification of engineer or higher qualification in the field of aviation under the National Technical Qualifications Act;
3. Persons who have been engaged in the design, manufacture, maintenance, or quality assurance of aircraft for at least three years after having acquired a bachelor's degree or higher degree in the fields related to aviation technologies;
4. Persons who have been engaged in the design, manufacture, maintenance, or quality assurance of aircraft of State agencies, etc., for at least five years.
(3) Where inspectors who are not public officials of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport conduct inspections referred to in paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may pay an allowance to such inspectors within budgetary limits.

Article 32 (Maintenance release)
(1) Where the owner has performed maintenance, etc. on an aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts (excluding minor maintenance prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act and repair or modification under Article 30(1); hereafter in this Article the same shall apply), the owner shall not operate the aircraft, engine, propeller, or use the components or parts on the aircraft, engine, or propeller unless a maintenance release for the relevant aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts has been issued, in accordance with the methods prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, by the holder of an aircraft maintenance technician licence under subparagraph 8 of Article 35 who meets the qualification requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, however, that where maintenance, etc. is performed in a region outside the Republic of Korea in which it is difficult to obtain a maintenance release, the owner may operate the aircraft, engine, propeller, or use the components or parts on the aircraft, engine, or propeller if a maintenance release has been issued by a person who meets the qualification requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the owner intends to entrust maintenance, etc. on an aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts to a third person, the owner, etc. shall entrust maintenance, etc. to the holder of a maintenance organization approval under Article 97(1) or a person who has manufactured the aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts.

Article 33 (Mandatory reporting of failures, defects and malfunctions of aircraft, products and parts)
(1) Where the holder of a type certificate, supplemental type certificate, production certificate, Technical Standard Order (TSO) Authorization, or Parts Manufacturer Approval becomes aware that a failure, defect, or malfunction prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act has occurred, due to a defect in its design or manufacture, in an aircraft, engine, propeller, component, or part that the holder manufactured or for which the holder obtained certification or approval, the holder shall report the occurrence to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where an owner prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, such as an air transport operator or aerial work operator, or the holder of a maintenance organization approval under Article 97(1), becomes aware that a failure, defect, or malfunction prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act has occurred during the operation or maintenance of an aircraft, the owner or approval holder shall report the occurrence to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

CHAPTER IV PERSONNEL LICENSING

Article 34 (Personnel Licensing)
(1) A person who intends to be engaged in aviation services shall obtain certification of qualification of aviation personnel (hereinafter referred to as "certification of qualification") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, however, that the same shall not apply to the operation of an unmanned aircraft among aviation services.
(2) Any of the following persons shall not be eligible to obtain a licence: <Amended on May 27, 2025>
1. Persons below the ages classified as follows:
a. Qualification of a private pilot: 17 years of age (where the licence of a private pilot is limited to a glider under Article 37, 16 years of age);
b. Qualification of a commercial pilot, multi-crew pilot, flight navigator, flight engineer, student air traffic controller, or aircraft maintenance technician: 18 years of age;
c. Qualifications for airline transport pilots, air traffic controllers, and flight dispatchers: 21 years of age.
2. A person subject to the revocation of a licence under Article 43(1), in whose case two years have not passed from the date of revocation (limited to cases where the person obtains a new certification of qualification after revocation of the former licence).
(3) Notwithstanding paragraphs (1) and (2), a member of the armed forces who controls aircraft at an air operation base governed by the Protection of Military Bases and Installations Act may perform air traffic control services after obtaining a licence from the Minister of National Defense.

Article 34-2 (Validity and renewal of personnel licences)
(1) The validity period of a licence shall be five years from the date the licence is issued under Article 38(5).
(2) A person who holds a licence and intends to maintain its validity beyond the period specified in paragraph (1) shall renew the licence every five years before its expiry date, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, that a person who has undergone an assessment under Article 38-2 or 63 shall be deemed to have renewed the licence.
(3) Where a person who intends to renew a licence under paragraph (2) is unable to renew it within the prescribed period due to military service or any other reason prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, the person may defer the renewal, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Where a person who holds a licence neither renews the licence under paragraph (2) nor defers its renewal under paragraph (3), the licence shall be suspended from the day following its expiry date.
(5) A person whose licence has been suspended under paragraph (4) shall renew the licence, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in order to restore its validity.
(6) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and operate an integrated career management system, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, in order to systematically check and manage the work history, education and training record, etc. of persons seeking to renew their qualifications.
(7) To establish and operate the integrated career management system under paragraph (6), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, request air traffic business operators under subparagraph 35 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act, aviation education and training institutions, or other entities to provide necessary data or information. A person receiving such a request shall comply unless there is a justifiable reason.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 35 (Type of Licenses)
Aviation personnel licences shall be of the following types: <Amended on May 27, 2025>
1. Airline transport pilot;
2. Commercial pilot;
3. Private pilot;
4. Multi-crew pilot;
5. Flight navigator;
6. Flight engineer;
7. Student air traffic controller;
7-2. Air traffic controller;
8. Aircraft maintenance technician;
9. Flight dispatcher.

Article 36 (Privileges)
(1) The scope of duties for each type of licence shall be as specified in the Appendix.
(2) No person who holds a licence shall engage in duties outside the scope authorized by the type of licence held by that person.
(3) Where a person falls under any of the following, paragraphs (1) and (2) shall not apply: <Amended on May 27, 2025>
1. Where a person boards and pilots an aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act (including handling its airframe and powerplants while on board; hereinafter the same shall apply);
2. Where the licence holder boards an aircraft of a new category, class, or type and conducts a test flight, etc., and has obtained permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
3. Where a person who has been granted a licence as a student air traffic controller performs air traffic control services under the supervision of an air traffic controller in order to obtain a licence as an air traffic controller or a rating on a licence.

Article 37 (Ratings)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may grant ratings to aviation personnel certificates according to the following classifications; provided, however, that a licence as an air traffic controller shall be rated under subparagraph 3: <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019; May 27, 2025>
1. In cases of qualification of an airline transport pilot, commercial pilot, private pilot, multi-crew pilot, or flight engineer: The category, class, or type of aircraft;
2. In cases of qualification of an aircraft maintenance technician: The category of aircraft or light aircraft and field of maintenance.
3. In the case of a certification as an air traffic controller: The type of air traffic control services, method of performance, and air traffic control facilities.
(2) Aviation personnel who have been granted a rating under paragraph (1) shall not engage in aviation services outside the scope of the rating with respect to the category, class, or type of aircraft or light aircraft, the field of maintenance, or the type of air traffic control service, method of performance, or air traffic control facility. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019; May 27, 2025>
(3) Details necessary for the limitation on a licence under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 38 (Examinations and exemptions)
(1) A person who intends to obtain a licence other than that of an air traffic controller shall successfully complete the written and practical examinations administered by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport regarding the knowledge and abilities necessary to engage in aviation services, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on May 27, 2025>
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants a rating by category, class, or type of aircraft or light aircraft under Article 37 (including instrument rating and flight instructor rating under Article 44), the Minister shall examine the applicant’s flight experience, maintenance experience, and other relevant experience involving aircraft or light aircraft. In such cases, the initial rating by category and class of aircraft or light aircraft may be assessed by means of a practical examination. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
(3) A person who intends to obtain a licence as an air traffic controller or a rating on a licence shall pass an assessment conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport regarding air traffic control skills and job knowledge necessary to engage in the services, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; in such cases, a person who intends to undergo the assessment shall meet all of the requirements of the following subparagraphs: <Added on May 27, 2025>
1. To hold a licence as a student air traffic controller under Articles 34 and 35;
2. To complete an education and training course for training air traffic controllers conducted by a specialized training institution under Article 48(1) or conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 83(4);
3. To have work experience meeting or exceeding the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, obtained under the supervision of an air traffic controller at the air traffic control facilities corresponding to the licence rating sought;
(4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may fully or partially exempt any of the following persons from examinations and assessments under paragraphs (1) through (3), as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019; Jun. 10, 2022; May 27, 2025>
1. A person who has obtained a licence from a foreign government;
2. Deleted; <May 12, 2026>
3. A person who has practical experience, including flight experience or maintenance experience involving aircraft or light aircraft;
4. A person who holds qualification in the field of aviation technology under the National Technical Qualifications Act;
5. A person who has completed a training course regarding an aircraft provided by the manufacturer of the aircraft;
6. A person who intends to perform the duties of an air traffic controller at a newly established air traffic control facility and whom the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to exempt, in whole or in part, from examinations and assessments.
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue a licence to a person who has passed the examinations and assessments under paragraphs (1) through (3). <Amended on May 27, 2025>

Article 38-2 (Competency checks for air traffic controllers)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall regularly assess whether an air traffic controller who has received a rating on their licence under subparagraph 3 of Article 37(1) possesses the required skills, including records of periodic training and duty performance, and may, where deemed particularly necessary, conduct such assessments at any time.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall suspend the effect of the rating where an air traffic controller who has been granted a rating on certification fails to undergo the assessment under paragraph (1) or fails to pass such assessment.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall cancel the suspension of validity where a person whose validity of rating has been suspended under paragraph (2) passes the assessment under paragraph (1).
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary, the Minister may have a person who has been granted air traffic services certification conduct the assessment under paragraph (1) for air traffic controllers engaged at air traffic control facilities operated by that person, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) An air traffic controller who passes the assessment under paragraph (4) shall be deemed to have passed the assessment under paragraph (1). In such cases, paragraphs (2) and (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the suspension of validity of a rating and the cancellation thereof.
(6) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (5), any other requirements for the methods, standards, cycles, etc. of competency assessments for air traffic controllers shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Added on May 27, 2025]

Article 39 (Practical examinations using flight simulation training devices)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may implement a practical examination referred to in Article 38(1) by using a flight simulation training device designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in lieu of an aircraft pursuant to Article 39-2(3). <Amended on May 18, 2021>
(2) Experience acquired using a flight simulation training device designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport pursuant to Article 39-2(3) shall be deemed flight experience in an aircraft under the former part of Article 38(2). <Amended on May 18, 2021>
(3) Any other requirements for recognizing experience acquired using a flight simulation training device under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on May 18, 2021>
[Title Amended on May 18, 2021]

Article 39-2 (Designation of flight simulation training devices)
(1) Where a person prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as an air transport operator, intends to obtain the designation of a flight simulation training device, the applicant shall file an application for designation with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to modifications to the designated matters or the extension of the period of validity of designation under paragraph (4).
(2) Upon receipt of an application referred to in paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall inspect whether the performance standards and other specifications of the relevant flight simulation training device comply with the standards for designation determined and notified by the Minister (hereafter in this Article referred to as "the standards for designation of flight simulation training devices"); provided, however, that part of the inspection may be omitted for a device whose designation is to be extended.
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that the findings of inspection under paragraph (2) indicate that a simulation training device meets the standards for designation as a flight simulation training device, the Minister shall issue a certificate of designation after determining the grade, scope of operation, etc. of the relevant flight simulation training device.
(4) The period of validity of designation of a flight simulation training device shall be one year; provided, however, that where a quality management system is established and operated for the relevant flight simulation training device, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, the period of validity may be extended by two years.
(5) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the designation of a flight simulation training device or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the designation in cases falling under subparagraph 1:
1. Where the designation holder has obtained such designation by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where a simulation training device no longer meets the standards for designation as a flight simulation training device which it has met at the time of designation.
(6) An application for designation under paragraph (1), the partial omission of inspections under the proviso of paragraph (2), the issuance of a certificate of designation under paragraph (3), the standards and procedures for making dispositions under paragraph (5), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Added on May 18, 2021]

Article 39-3 (Prohibition of lending or borrowing personnel licences and misuse of names)
(1) A holder of an aviation personnel licence shall not permit another person to perform aviation duties using the holder’s name, or lend the licence issued under Article 38(5) (hereinafter referred to as an "aviation personnel licence") to another person. <Amended on May 27, 2025>
(2) No person shall use any other person's name to perform aviation duties, or borrow any other person's aviation personnel licence.
(3) No person shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (1) or (2).
[This Article Added on May 18, 2021]

Article 39-4 (Carriage of personnel licenses and Medical Assessments)
(1) Aviation personnel shall carry their licenses while performing aviation duties.
(2) Aviation personnel falling under subparagraphs 1 through 4, 7, and 7-2 of Article 35 shall, while performing aviation duties, carry not only their licences but also the Medical Assessments issued under Article 40(3) and (4).
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 40 (Medical Assessment)
(1) Any of the following persons shall obtain a Medical Assessment for each kind of licence from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on May 27, 2025>
1. flight crew members;
2. A person who performs air traffic control services after obtaining a licence under subparagraph 7 or 7-2 of Article 35.
(2) Any other requirements for the standards and methods for and the period of validity, etc. of Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person who intends to obtain a Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under paragraph (1) meets standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue a Medical Assessment.
(4) Even though a person who intends to obtain a Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under paragraph (1) fails to fully meet the standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may issue a Medical Assessment by placing limitations on the scope or period of validity of the relevant aviation duties as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, if deemed necessary in consideration of the experience and competence of the person who undergoes an aviation medical examination. <Amended on January 18, 2022>
(5) A person issued with a Medical Assessment with limitations placed on the scope or period of validity of the relevant aviation duties pursuant to paragraph (4) shall observe the scope or period of validity. <Added on January 18, 2022>
(6) A person dissatisfied with a decision on a Medical Assessment for each kind of licence referred to in paragraph (1) may file an objection with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on January 18, 2022>
(7) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport makes a decision on an objection under paragraph (6), the Minister shall notify the applicant of the details thereof without delay. <Amended on January 18, 2022>

Article 41 (Orders to undergo aviation medical examinations)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order flight crew, student air traffic controllers, and air traffic controllers for whom the validity period of a Medical Assessment has not expired to undergo an aviation medical examination under Article 40, where the Minister deems it particularly necessary. <Amended on May 27, 2025>

Article 41-2 (Health promotion)
(1) To reduce risk factors affecting aviation safety, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and a person who has obtained air traffic services certification shall annually formulate and implement health promotion activity plans for student air traffic controllers and air traffic controllers under their jurisdiction, with the aim of promoting and maintaining their health. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) To reduce risk factors affecting aviation safety, an air transport operator, an aircraft use business entity, or the owner of an aircraft for international flight operation shall annually formulate and implement health promotion activity plans for flight crew under their jurisdiction, with the aim of promoting and maintaining their health.
(3) Any other requirements for the details of health promotion activity plans, the formulation and implementation thereof under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Added on Jan. 18, 2022]

Article 42 (Restrictions on aviation services)
(1) Flight crew members, student air traffic controllers, and air traffic controllers who fail to meet standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under Article 40(2) shall not be engaged in aviation services (including flight practice under Article 46 and air traffic control practice under Article 47; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) even though their Medical Assessments remain valid. <Amended on Jan. 18, 2022; May 12, 2026>
(2) Where flight crew members, student air traffic controllers, and air traffic controllers who have obtained Medical Assessments under Article 40(1) experience a deterioration in their physical or mental condition as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, they shall file a report thereon with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, accompanied by a written opinion of an aviation medical examiner designated in accordance with Article 49(1). <Added on Jan. 18, 2022; May 12, 2026>
(3) Upon receipt of a report under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall verify, without delay, whether the physical or mental state of the person who has filed the report meets standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence and notify the relevant person of the results thereof. <Added on Jan. 18, 2022>
(4) No person who has reported the deterioration of the person's physical or mental state pursuant to paragraph (2) shall be engaged in aviation services until the person is notified of the results under paragraph (3). <Added on Jan. 18, 2022>
(5) Detailed standards for deterioration in physical or mental condition, the deadline and method for reporting, and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on Jan. 18, 2022>

Article 42-2 (Air traffic control aptitude test)
(1) A person who intends to obtain a licence as a student air traffic controller under Article 38(1) shall pass an aptitude test administered by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "air traffic control aptitude test") to determine whether the person possesses the aptitude suitable for air traffic control services.
(2) A person who failed the immediately preceding air traffic control aptitude test and for whom 3 months have not passed since the date of such test may not take an air traffic control aptitude test.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust business affairs regarding the air traffic control aptitude test to the Korea Transportation Safety Authority established under the Korea Transportation Safety Authority Act (hereinafter referred to as "Korea Transportation Safety Authority") or aviation-related institutions or organizations.
(4) If the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport entrusts business affairs regarding the air traffic control aptitude test under paragraph (3), the Minister may subsidize the expenses necessary therefor.
(5) Any other requirements for the passing standards, testing methods, procedures, etc. of the air traffic control aptitude test shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 27, 2025]

Article 43 (Revocation of Licence, Medical Assessment, or passing of air traffic control aptitude test)
(1) Where aviation personnel fall under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke their licence or the rating on that licence (hereafter in this Article referred to as "licence, etc.") or issue an order to suspend the effect of such certification of qualification, etc. for a specified period not exceeding 1 year; provided, however, that where they fall under subparagraph 1, 6-2, 6-3, 15, or 31, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the relevant licence, etc.: <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019; Jun. 9, 2020; Dec. 8, 2020; May 18, 2021; Dec. 7, 2021; Jan. 18, 2022; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder has obtained a licence or rating by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where a person is sentenced to a fine or heavier punishment for violating this Act;
3. Where the holder causes casualties or damage to property because the holder causes an accident by intent or gross negligence in the course of performing aviation duties as aviation personnel;
4. Where aviation personnel who check the maintenance, etc. pursuant to the main clause of Article 32(1) fail to check airworthiness in accordance with the methods prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
5. Where the holder is engaged in services outside the scope of services based on the kinds of licence, in violation of Article 36(2);
6. Where aviation personnel who have been granted a rating on their licence engage in aviation services outside the rated category, class, or type of aircraft or light aircraft, the rated field of maintenance, or the rated type, method of performance, or facility of air traffic control services, in violation of Article 37(2);
6-2. Where the holder permits another person to use the holder's name to perform aviation duties or lends the holder's licence in violation of Article 39-3(1);
6-3. Where the holder arranges or facilitates any of the following acts, in violation of Article 39-3(3):
a. Permitting another person to use the holder's name to perform aviation services or lending the holder's aviation personnel certificate;
b. Using another person's name to perform aviation services or borrowing any other person's aviation personnel licence;
6-4. Where a person fails to carry an aviation personnel licence while performing aviation duties, in violation of Article 39-4(1);
7. Where a person engages in aviation duties (including flight practice under Article 46; hereinafter the same shall apply in subparagraphs 13, 14, and 16 of this paragraph) without obtaining a Medical Assessment, in violation of Article 40(1);
8. Where flight crew members, student air traffic controllers, and air traffic controllers who fail to meet standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under Article 40(2) are engaged in aviation services, in violation of Article 42(1);
8-2. Where the holder fails to report the deterioration of the holder's physical or mental state, in violation of Article 42(2);
8-3. Where the holder performs aviation duties before being notified of the results under Article 42(3), in violation of Article 42(4);
9. Where the holder conducts IFR flight or flies an aircraft by reference to instruments without obtaining an instrument rating, in violation of Article 44(1);
10. Where the holder conducts flight training without obtaining a flight instructor rating, in violation of Article 44(2);
11. Where the holder is engaged in duties falling under any subparagraph of Article 45(1) without obtaining certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency, in violation of Article 45(1);
11-2. Where an instructor at an approved training organization under Article 48(1), or a person enrolled in a training course, falsely enters flight time in an aircraft flight log carried under Article 52(1) (hereinafter referred to as an "aircraft flight log");
12. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft falling under any subparagraph of Article 55 or is engaged in duties, such as IFR flight, night flight, or flight training under Article 44(2) without flight experience prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 55;
13. Where the holder is engaged in aviation duties while being unable to normally perform aviation duties under the influence of alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1);
14. Where the holder consumes or uses any alcoholic beverage, etc. referred to in Article 57(1) while being engaged in aviation duties, in violation of Article 57(2);
15. Where the holder fails to comply with a request to undergo a breath test to determine whether the holder has consumed or used any alcohol, etc. referred to in Article 57(1), in violation of Article 57(3);
15-2. Where the holder smokes on an aircraft, in violation of Article 57-2;
16. Where the holder causes a serious incident, incident, or hazard referred to in Article 61(1) by intent or gross negligence when performing aviation duties;
17. Where the holder fails to perform the duties as a pilot in command referred to in Article 62(2) or (4) through (6);
18. Where a pilot operates an aircraft without obtaining recognition or undergoing review of qualification for the operation thereof, in violation of Article 63;
19. Where the pilot in command takes off an aircraft or modifies a flight plan without approval from a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher, in violation of Article 65(2);
20. Where the holder performs the takeoff from or landing at an area other than takeoff and landing areas, in violation of Article 66;
21. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft without complying with flight rules, in violation of Article 67(1);
21-2. Where a person fails to immediately implement a measure referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 67-2(2) (including a measure taken by the Minister of National Defense under Article 10(1) of the Act on the Operation of Military Aircraft, engine, propeller), in violation of Article 67-2(3);
22. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft or performs an act falling under any subparagraph of Article 68, in violation of that Article;
23. Where the holder transports dangerous goods without permission, in violation of Article 70(1);
24. Deleted <May 12, 2026>
25. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft or performs the duties without conforming to the Flight Safety Regulations referred to in Article 77(1), in violation of Article 77(2);
26. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft in non-controlled airspace or warning airspace without adhering to the flight methods and procedures determined and publicly announced by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 79(1);
27. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft in regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces) without obtaining permission from or adhering to the flight methods and procedures determined by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 79(2);
28. Where the holder fails to adhere to the timing and procedures for moving, takeoff, and landing, and the method of controlling the flight of an aircraft instructed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a person who has obtained air traffic services certification, in violation of Article 84(1);
29. Where the holder controls the flight of an aircraft or performs the duties without conforming to operation standards, in violation of Article 90(4) (including cases where it applies mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 96(1));
30. Where the holder performs duties without complying with the operations manual or maintenance control manual, in violation of the latter part of Article 93(7) (including cases where it is applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(2));
30-2. Where aviation personnel who confirm the maintenance of light aircraft or the components or parts thereof pursuant to the main clause of Article 108(4) fail to confirm such maintenance in accordance with the method prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
31. Where the holder is engaged in aviation duties during the period of suspension, in violation of an order to suspend the licence, etc., under Article 38-2 or this Article.
(2) Where the effect of a qualification is suspended under paragraph (1), suspension of an airline transport pilot qualification shall also include the person’s multi-crew pilot, commercial pilot, and private pilot qualifications, and suspension of a commercial pilot qualification shall also include the person’s private pilot qualification. <Added on Jan. 18, 2022>
(3) Where aviation personnel fall under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke their Medical Assessments or issue an order to suspend the effect of such Medical Assessments for a specified period not exceeding 1 year; provided, however, that where they fall under subparagraph 1, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke their Medical Assessments: <Amended on Jan. 18, 2022; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder has obtained a Medical Assessment by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder falls under any of subparagraphs 13 through 15 of paragraph (1);
2-2. Where the holder fails to carry a Medical Assessment while performing aviation services, in violation of Article 39-4(2);
3. Where the holder is deemed unfit to perform aviation services because the holder fails to meet the standards for Medical Assessment for the relevant category referred to in Article 40(2);
4. Where the holder is engaged in aviation duties (including flight practice under Article 46) without observing the limited scope of aviation duties, in violation of Article 40(5);
5. Where the holder fails to comply with an Order to Undergo an Aviation Medical Examination under Article 41;
6. Where the holder performs aviation duties, in violation of Article 42(1);
7. Deleted <May 12, 2026>
(4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the passing of an air traffic control aptitude test where a student air traffic controller or a person who intends to obtain a licence as a student air traffic controller has passed the air traffic control aptitude test by fraud or other improper means. <Added on May 27, 2025>
(5) Where a person who applies for or takes an examination or assessment for a licence, etc., an aviation medical examination, or an air traffic control aptitude test commits any fraudulent act in such examination or assessment, the relevant examination or assessment shall be suspended or invalidated. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(6) A person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall not apply for or take an examination or assessment for a licence, etc., an aviation medical examination, or an air traffic control aptitude test under this Act for a period prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, not exceeding two years from the date of the relevant disposition, taking into account the details and severity of the violation. <Added on May 12, 2026>
1. A person whose licence, etc. has been revoked under paragraph (1), whose Medical Assessment has been revoked under paragraph (3), or whose passing of an air traffic control aptitude test has been revoked under paragraph (4);
2. A person whose examination, assessment, or inspection has been suspended or invalidated because of a fraudulent act under paragraph (5).
(7) The standards and procedures for dispositions under paragraphs (1), (3), and (4), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on January 18, 2022; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
[Title Amended on May 27, 2025][Effective Date: May 28, 2028] Article 43 (limited to matters concerning air traffic control aptitude tests)

Article 44 (Instrument rating and flight instructor rating)
(1) A person who holds an airline transport pilot licence (only when operating a helicopter), commercial pilot licence, private pilot licence or multi-crew pilot licence shall obtain an instrument rating from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in order to conduct any of the following flights using a category of aircraft that the person is authorized to operate:
1. Instrument flight;
2. Flight conducted under instrument flight rules (IFR);
(2) A person who intends to provide flight training for persons who undergo the following flight practice shall obtain a flight instructor rating separately for each category and each class of aircraft from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act in consideration of flight hours: <Amended on October 24, 2017; May 12, 2026>
1. Flight practice conducted aboard an aircraft (excluding an aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act under Article 36(3)) by a person who does not hold a licence under subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 35;
2. Flight practice conducted by a person holding any licence referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 35 on board an aircraft other than a category or class for which the certification is rated under Article 37;
3. Flight practice conducted on board an aircraft by a person who intends to obtain an instrument rating or flight instructor rating.
(3) Any other requirements for a flight instructor rating under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Article 38 and Article 43(1), (5), and (6) shall apply mutatis mutandis to examinations for the instrument rating under paragraph (1) and the flight instructor rating under paragraph (2), and to the revocation thereof and other related matters. <Amended on Jan. 18, 2022; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>

Article 45 (Certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency)
(1) A person who intends to engage in services falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall obtain certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on May 27, 2025>
1. Controlling the flight of an aircraft operating between at least two countries;
2. Air traffic control services for an aircraft operating between two or more countries;
3. Radio communications on an aircraft operating between at least two countries among aeronautical communications referred to in Article 53 of the Airport Facilities Act.
(2) Any other requirements for the implementation of an examination for certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency (hereinafter referred to as "certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency") referred to in paragraph (1), ratings of certification, acceptance criteria by rating, the period of validity by rating, etc. shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person who intends to obtain certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency meets acceptance criteria by rating under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue a certificate of Aviation English Language Proficiency, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Notwithstanding paragraph (3), where a person who intends to obtain certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency, who performs air traffic control services after obtaining a licence from the Minister of National Defense pursuant to Article 34(3), meets acceptance criteria by rating under paragraph (2), the Minister of National Defense may issue a certificate of Aviation English Language Proficiency.
(5) A person who has obtained certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency from a foreign government shall be exempt from an examination for certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency referred to in paragraph (2) within the period of validity by relevant rating.
(6) Subparagraph 1 of Article 43(1) and paragraphs (5) and (6) of that Article shall apply mutatis mutandis to the revocation, etc. of certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency. In such cases, "licence, etc." shall be construed as "certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency." <Amended on Jan. 18, 2022; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>

Article 46 (Flight practice)
(1) Article 36(1) and (2), Article 37(2), and Article 44(1) shall not apply to any of the following acts of piloting for flight practice. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
1. Flight practice conducted aboard an aircraft of a class or type other than that covered by a rating (limited to an aircraft of the category covered by the rating) by a person who holds a licence under subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 35 and a Medical Assessment under Article 40, under the supervision of a person who holds the licence and Medical Assessment required to pilot that aircraft (including a person designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport as possessing the knowledge and ability required to pilot that aircraft);
2. Flight practice referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 44(2), for which permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and which is conducted under the supervision of the holder of a flight instructor rating;
3. Flight practice under subparagraph 2 or 3 of Article 44(2), conducted under the supervision of a person holding flight instructor rating.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives an application for permission for flight practice under subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1), in which case the Minister deems that an applicant has the ability necessary to undergo flight practice, the Minister shall permit the flight practice, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Permission under subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1) shall be granted by issuing a flight practice permit to an applicant.
(4) Articles 40 through 42 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Medical Assessment of a person who has obtained permission under subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1), an order to undergo an aviation medical examination, etc. <Amended on Jan. 18, 2022>
(5) Where a person who has received a flight practice permit referred to in paragraph (3) undergoes flight practice, the permit holder shall carry a flight practice permit and a Medical Assessment.

Article 47 (Air traffic control practice)
(1) Where a person who has not obtained a licence as a student air traffic controller under subparagraph 7 of Article 35 intends to undergo air traffic control practice (hereinafter referred to as "air traffic control practice"), the person shall obtain permission for such practice from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and undergo such practice under the supervision of a person who meets the qualification requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives an application for permission for air traffic control practice referred to in paragraph (1), and deems that the applicant has the ability necessary to undergo air traffic control practice, the Minister shall permit the applicant to undergo air traffic control practice, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Permission for air traffic control practice referred to in paragraph (1) shall be granted by issuing an air traffic control practice permit to an applicant.
(4) Articles 40 through 42 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Medical Assessment of a person who has obtained permission for air traffic control practice under paragraph (1), an order to undergo an aviation medical examination, etc. <Amended on Jan. 18, 2022>
(5) Where a person who has received an air traffic control practice permit under paragraph (3) undergoes air traffic control practice, the permit holder shall carry an air traffic control practice permit and a Medical Assessment.

Article 47-2 (Revocation or suspension of practice permits and Medical Assessments for unlicensed trainees)
(1) Where a person who conducts flight practice under subparagraph 2 of Article 46(1) or air traffic control practice under Article 47(1) (hereafter in this Article referred to as "flight practice, etc.") falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke permission for flight practice under Article 46(2) or permission for air traffic control practice under Article 47(2) (hereafter in this Article, referred to as "permission for practice") or issue an order to suspend the effect of such permission for practice for a specified period not exceeding one year; provided, however, that in cases falling under subparagraphs 1, 11, and 14, the relevant permission for practice shall be revoked:
1. Where a person has obtained permission for practice by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where a person is sentenced to a fine or heavier punishment for violating this Act;
3. Where a person conducting flight practice, etc. causes an accident resulting in casualties or damage to property by intent or gross negligence in the course of flight practice, etc.;
4. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. without obtaining a Medical Assessment, in violation of Article 40(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
5. Where a person who fails to meet standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under Article 40(2) conducts flight practice, etc., in violation of Article 42(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
6. Where a person fails to report the deterioration of the person's physical or mental state, in violation of Article 42(2) as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
7. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. before being notified of the results under Article 42(3), in violation of Article 42(4), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
8. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. without carrying an aircraft flight practice permit or an air traffic control practice permit in violation of Article 46(5) or 47(5);
9. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. while being unable to normally conduct flight practice, etc. under the influence of alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1);
10. Where a person consumes or uses any alcohol, etc. referred to in Article 57(1) while conducting flight practice, etc. in violation of Article 57(2);
11. Where a person fails to comply with a request to undergo a breath test to determine whether the person has consumed or used any alcohol, etc. referred to in Article 57(1), in violation of Article 57(3);
12. Where a person smokes inside an aircraft, in violation of Article 57-2;
13. Where a person causes a serious incident, incident, or hazard referred to in Article 61(1) by intent or gross negligence in the course of flight practice, etc.;
14. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. during the period of suspension, in violation of an order to suspend the permission for practice under this Article.
(2) Where a person conducting flight practice, etc. falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the person's Medical Assessment or issue an order to suspend the effect of such Medical Assessment for a specified period not exceeding one year; provided, however, that where the person falls under subparagraph 1, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the Medical Assessment:
1. Where the person has obtained a Medical Assessment by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where a person falls under any of subparagraphs 9 through 11 of paragraph (1);
3. Where a person is deemed unfit for flight practice, etc. due to failure to meet standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence referred to in Article 40(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
4. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. without observing the limited scope of aviation services, in violation of Article 40(5), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
5. Where a person fails to comply with an order to undergo an aviation medical examination under Article 41, as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Articles 46(4) and 47(4);
6. Where a person who fails to meet standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under Article 40(2) conducts flight practice, etc., in violation of Article 42(1), which is applied mutatis mutandis in Article 46(4) and 47(4);
7. Where a person conducts flight practice, etc. without carrying a Medical Assessment, in violation of Article 46(5) or Article 47(5).
(3) Where a person undergoing a review for permission for practice or an aviation medical examination commits a fraudulent act during the review or examination, the relevant review or examination shall be suspended or invalidated. Such person shall not undergo another review for permission for practice or aviation medical examination under this Act for two years from the date of the relevant disposition.
(4) The criteria and procedures for dispositions under paragraphs (1) and (2), and any other requirements, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on January 18, 2022]

Article 48 (Approval of training organizations)
(1) Any person or entity falling under either of the following subparagraphs may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, obtain approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to operate a training organization for aviation personnel or cabin crew (hereinafter referred to as an "approved training organization"); provided, however, that a person or entity falling under subparagraph 1 that intends to train aviation personnel under subparagraphs 1 through 4, 7, 7-2, and 8 of Article 35 [excluding cases where the holder of an air operator certificate under Article 90(1) (including as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(1)) or a maintenance organization approval under Article 97(1) directly trains its own aviation personnel in accordance with that certificate or approval] shall obtain such approval: <Amended on May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. A person or entity that intends to train individuals to become qualified aviation personnel (including training for ratings under Article 37(1));
2. A person or entity that intends to train individuals to become cabin crew members.
(2) Any person or entity intending to obtain approval under paragraph (1) shall have an education and training system that complies with the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act (hereinafter referred to as "training organization approval standards") regarding curricula, teaching methods, human resources, facilities, equipment, etc.
(3) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport approves a training organization, the Minister shall issue to the approval holder a training organization approval certificate together with the approved Training Manual. The Training Manual shall set out the training programmes, the number and qualifications of instructors, the methods of evaluation, and any other requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) If the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport determines that it is necessary to ensure safety in conducting educational and training programs, the Minister may order an amendment to the approved Training Manual under paragraph (3), ex officio or at the request of the holder of a training organization approval.
(5) The holder of a training organization approval shall comply with the Training Manual approved under paragraph (3), including any amendments made under paragraph (4).
(6) The holder of a training organization approval shall continuously maintain its education and training system in accordance with the approved Training Manual and shall undergo an inspection conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport whenever that system is changed, including when a new training programme is introduced.
(7) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct regular or ad hoc inspections, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, to determine whether the holder of a training organization approval maintains its education and training system. If an inspection finds that the system is not continuously maintained in accordance with the Training Manual approved under paragraph (3), including any amendments made under paragraph (4), the Minister may order the holder to take corrective or other measures. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(8) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may fully or partially subsidize approved training organizations, within the budget, for expenses incurred by them in training aviation personnel necessary for air transport services.
(9) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and operate a system to provide the public with information on aviation education and training and to systematically manage aviation education and training institutions, including approved training organizations (hereinafter referred to as the "Integrated Aviation Education and Training Management System").
(10) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request air transportation service providers defined in subparagraph 35 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act, aviation education and training institutions, and other relevant persons to provide data or information necessary to establish and operate the Integrated Aviation Education and Training Management System. A person receiving such a request shall comply unless there is good cause not to do so.
(11) The holder of a training organization approval shall submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, through the Integrated Aviation Education and Training Management System, a list of persons who have commenced a training course and a list of persons who have completed it, within 10 days after the commencement date and the completion date of such course, respectively. <Added on May 12, 2026>
[This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 48-2 (Validity and renewal of training organization approvals)
(1) The validity period of a training organization approval under Article 48(1) shall run from the date on which the approval is granted for the period specified in each of the following subparagraphs; where the Training Manual is amended under paragraph (4) of that Article, the validity period shall be the remainder of the previous validity period:
1. An approved training organization located in the Republic of Korea: Five years;
2. An approved training organization located outside the Republic of Korea: Two years.
(2) The holder of a training organization approval who intends to maintain the approval beyond the validity period under paragraph (1) shall renew the approval, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, before its expiration date at each interval specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
(3) Where the holder of a training organization approval fails to renew the approval under paragraph (2), the effect of such approval shall be suspended from the day following its expiration date.
(4) The holder of a training organization approval suspended under paragraph (3) shall renew the approval, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in order to restore its validity.
(5) Where the holder of a training organization approval suspended under paragraph (3) fails to renew the approval under paragraph (4) within 12 months from the date of suspension, the approval shall cease to have effect on the day following the expiration of that period.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]
[Former Article 48-2 moved to Article 48-4 <May 12, 2026>]

Article 48-3 (Prohibition of lending training organization approval certificates)
(1) The holder of a training organization approval shall not permit another person or entity to conduct the activities of an approved training organization using the holder’s name, or lend the approval certificate to another person or entity.
(2) No person or entity shall conduct the activities of an approved training organization using the name of an approval holder, or borrow the holder's approval certificate.
(3) No person or entity shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (1) or (2).
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]
[Former Article 48-3 moved to Article 48-5 <May 12, 2026>]

Article 48-4 (Revocation of training organization approvals and suspension of activities)
(1) Where the holder of a training organization approval falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the approval or order suspension of the training organization’s activities for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided that the Minister shall revoke the approval in cases falling under subparagraph 1, 4-3, 4-4, or 8. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder obtained the approval by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder breaches the training organization approval standards, without any justification;
3. Where the holder fails to comply with the approved Training Manual, without just grounds, in violation of Article 48(5);
3-2. Where the holder fails to comply with an order to take corrective measures, etc. under the latter part of Article 48(7);
4. Where the holder fails to comply with a request from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to provide data or information under Article 48(10), without just grounds;
4-2. Where the holder fails to submit, within the prescribed period, or falsely submits, a list of persons who commenced or completed a training course, in violation of Article 48(11);
4-3. Where the holder conducts the activities of the training organization during a period in which the approval is suspended under Article 48-2(3);
4-4. Where the holder permits another person or entity to conduct the activities of the approved training organization using the holder’s name, or lends the approval certificate to another person or entity, in violation of Article 48-3(1);
5. Where the holder fails to establish a training course for more than two years after the approval is granted;
5-2. Where an instructor at an approved training organization, or a person enrolled in a training course, falsely enters flight time in an aircraft flight log; provided that this shall not apply where the approval holder has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant services to prevent the violation;
6. Where the holder has caused an accident by intent or gross negligence or where the holder's failure to fulfill the duty of due care in managing and supervising aviation personnel results in an accident;
7. Where the holder's violation of Article 58(2) falls under any of the following cases:
a. Where the holder fails to implement a safety management system before the holder begins services;
b. Where the holder maintains a safety management system without approval;
c. Where the holder maintains a safety management system differently from the details thereof approved;
d. Where the holder changes, without approval, any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
8. Where the holder continues business operations during business suspension under the main clause of this paragraph.
(2) Detailed criteria and procedures for making dispositions under paragraph (1) and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on October 24, 2017]
[Moved from Article 48-2 <May 12, 2026>]

Article 48-5 (Penalty surcharges on approved training organizations)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport is required to order suspension of the activities of the holder of a training organization approval because the holder falls under any of subparagraphs 2, 3, 3-2, 4, 4-2, 5, 5-2, 6, or 7 of Article 48-4(1), but such suspension is likely to cause serious inconvenience to users of the approved training organization or other persons, or to harm the public interest, the Minister may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding one billion won in lieu of the suspension. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) Specific standards and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges referred to in paragraph (1), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person liable to pay a penalty surcharge pursuant to paragraph (1) fails to make payment by the deadline, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected.
[This Article Newly Inserted on October 24, 2017]
[Moved from Article 48-3 <May 12, 2026>]

Article 49 (Designation of medical examiners)
(1) To ensure the efficient and professional performance of duties relating to Medical Assessment for each kind of licence under Article 40, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, designate medical specialists who have received professional training in aviation medicine (hereinafter referred to as "aviation medical examiners") and authorize them to perform duties relating to Medical Assessment under Article 40 on behalf of the Minister.
(2) Criteria for designation of a medical examiner, such as the record of completion of training and professional experience, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Aviation medical examiners shall receive professional training regularly conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 50 (Revocation or suspension of medical examiner designations)
(1) Where an aviation medical examiner falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the designation thereof or issue an order to suspend the effect of the designation thereof for a specified period not exceeding one year; provided, however, that where the medical examiner falls under any of subparagraphs 1, 3, 4 or 6 through 8, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the designation thereof:
1. Where the medical examiner has been designated as a medical examiner by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the medical examiner negligently performs services prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the issuance of Medical Assessments referred to in Article 40;
3. Where the medical examiner performs services concerning Medical Assessment referred to in Article 40 during the period of suspension of the effect of designation as the medical examiner referred to in this Article;
4. Where the medical examiner fails to meet the criteria for designation referred to in Article 49(2);
5. Where the medical examiner fails to receive professional training referred to in Article 49(3);
6. Where the medical examiner issues a wrong Medical Assessment by intent or gross negligence;
7. Where the medical examiner's medical license is revoked under Article 65 of the Medical Service Act or suspended under Article 66 of that Act;
8. Where the medical examiner requests the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to revoke the designation.
(2) No person who receives a disposition to revoke the designation of a medical examiner pursuant to paragraph (1) shall file an application for the designation of a medical examiner under this Act for two years from the date of such disposition. <Added on January 18, 2022>
(3) The standards and procedures for dispositions under paragraph (1), and any other requirements, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on January 18, 2022>

CHAPTER V OPERATION OF AIRCRAFT

Article 51 (Installation and operation of aircraft radio equipment)
A person who intends to operate an aircraft or the owner thereof, etc. shall install and operate radio equipment prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as an emergency locator transmitter (ELT) and secondary surveillance radar transponder in the relevant aircraft.

Article 52 (Aircraft instruments, equipment and documents to be carried and operated)
(1) A person who intends to operate an aircraft or the owner thereof, etc. shall install flight instruments and equipment or load documents, first aid kits, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "flight instruments, etc.") necessary for the safe flight of the aircraft in the relevant aircraft and operate those instruments. In such cases, equipment necessary for accident prevention and safe flight may be additionally installed in an aeroplane whose maximum takeoff weight exceeds 600 kilograms but does not exceed 5,700 kilograms. <Amended on January 17, 2017>
(2) The aircraft on which flight instruments, etc. are required to be installed or carried under paragraph (1), the types of such instruments, equipment and other items, the standards for their installation and carriage, their methods of operation, and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 53 (Aircraft fuel requirements)
Neither a person who intends to operate an aircraft nor the owner thereof, etc. shall operate the aircraft without loading the amount of fuel prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act on the aircraft.

Article 54 (Lights to be displayed by aircraft)
A person who operates an aircraft or who, at night (meaning the period from sunset to sunrise; hereinafter the same shall apply), parks or moors an aircraft at an aerodrome shall display lights to indicate the position of the aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 55 (Recent experience requirements for flight crew members)
A flight crew member who intends to operate any of the following aircraft, to conduct an IFR flight or night flight, or to be engaged in providing flight training under Article 44(2) shall have flight experience (including flight experience acquired using a flight simulation training device) prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on May 18, 2021>
1. Aircraft used for air transport services or aerial work;
2. Aircraft meeting the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the weight of aircraft and the number of passenger seats, which are used for international flight operations (hereinafter referred to as "aircraft for international flight operations").

Article 56 (Fatigue management for crew members)
(1) An air transport operator, an aerial work operator, or the owner of an aircraft for international flight operation shall manage the fatigue of flight crew and cabin crew (hereinafter referred to as "aircrew") and flight dispatchers belonging thereto by at least one of the following methods: <Amended on Dec. 8, 2020>
1. Complying with standards to restrict flight time, flight duty period, duty period, etc. (hereafter in this Article referred to as "flight time, etc.") of aircrew or working hours of flight dispatchers, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
2. Building and operating a fatigue risk management system.
(2) An air transport operator, an aerial work operator, or the owner of an aircraft for international flight operation intending to build and operate a fatigue risk management system shall operate the fatigue risk management system after obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. The same shall also apply to change of any important matters prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act among the matters approved.
(3) Where an air transport operator, an aerial work operator, or the owner of an aircraft for international flight operation manages the fatigue of aircrew or flight dispatchers pursuant to subparagraph 1 of paragraph (1), the operator or owner shall retain, for at least 15 months, records of the flight time, etc. of aircrew and the working hours of flight dispatchers. <Amended on Dec. 8, 2020>
[Title Amended on Dec. 8, 2020]

Article 57 (Restrictions on alcohol and other psychoactive substances)
(1) Neither aviation personnel (including a person who undergoes flight practice referred to in Article 46 and a person who undergoes air traffic control practice referred to in Article 47; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) nor cabin crew members shall be engaged in aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members where they are unable to normally perform aviation duties (including flight practice referred to in Article 46 and air traffic control practice referred to in Article 47; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) or the duties of cabin crew members under the influence of alcohol referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Liquor Tax Act, narcotics referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Narcotics Control Act, or hallucinogens, etc. referred to in Article 22(1) of the Chemical Substances Control Act (hereinafter referred to as "alcohol, etc.").
(2) Neither aviation personnel nor cabin crew members shall consume or use alcohol, etc., while being engaged in aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members.
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary for aviation safety and hazard prevention or has any reasonable cause to deem that aviation personnel or cabin crew members were engaged in aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members, in violation of paragraph (1) or (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may conduct a test to determine whether the aviation personnel or cabin crew members consumed or used any alcohol, etc., by using a breathalyzer test, etc., and the aviation personnel or cabin crew members shall comply with such test. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(4) Where the aviation personnel or the cabin crew members have an objection to the outcomes of the test under paragraph (3), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may conduct another test to determine whether they consumed or used alcohol, etc., by collecting blood or conducting a urine test upon obtaining their consent.
(5) The criteria for determination that aviation personnel or cabin crew members are unable to normally perform aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members under the influence of alcohol, etc. shall be as follows:
1. Where a blood alcohol content is at least 0.02 percent due to consumption of any beverage containing alcohol;
2. Where the person has used narcotics referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Narcotics Control Act;
3. Where the person has used any hallucinogen referred to in Article 22(1) of the Chemical Substances Control Act.
(6) Kinds of alcohol, etc., detailed procedures necessary for the measurement thereof, and any other requirements for the management, etc. of records of measurement shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act pursuant to paragraphs (1) through (5).

Article 57-2 (Prohibition of smoking on board aircraft)
Aviation personnel (including persons who undergo flight practice under Article 46) and cabin crew members shall not smoke on aircraft while being engaged in aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members.
[This Article Newly Inserted on December 8, 2020]

Article 58 (State safety programme)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and notify a state safety programme including the following: <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019; May 12, 2026>
1. Aviation safety policies, safety objectives, organizational framework, and available resources;
2. Aviation safety risk management, including hazard identification, risk assessment and mitigation;
3. Aviation safety assurance, including measurement and monitoring of safety performance;
4. Aviation safety promotion, including communication of aviation safety policies and objectives and development of a positive safety culture;
5. Deleted; <Aug. 27, 2019>
6. Deleted. <Aug. 27, 2019>
(2) Any person or entity falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall establish a safety management system to prevent aircraft accidents and ensure flight safety in accordance with the state safety programme under paragraph (1) before commencing production, training, operation, business, etc., and shall maintain the system after obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to changes of any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, however, that this shall not apply where a foreign service provider has obtained recognition on its safety management system, established in accordance with the regulation of its origin state, from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017; Aug. 27, 2019; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. The holder of a type certificate, restricted type certificate, or production certificate; provided, however, that this shall not apply where the holder of a supplemental type certificate or a person or entity authorized to use that certificate has obtained a production certificate;
2. The holder of a training organization approval under the proviso to Article 48(1) who meets all of the following requirements:
a. The holder has a number of aircraft, aviation personnel, etc., meeting or exceeding the scale prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act;
b. The holder operates a training course using aircraft employed in actual operations;
3. The holder of an air traffic services certificate;
4. The holder of an air operator certificate under Article 90(1) (including as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(1)) who falls under either of the following items:
a. An air transport operator;
b. An aerial work operator whose number of aircraft, engine, or propeller meets or exceeds the scale prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
5. The holder of a maintenance organization approval under Article 97(1) whose number of aircraft maintenance technicians, scope of maintenance work to be performed, etc. meet or exceed the scale prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act;
6. The holder of an airport operating certificate under Article 38(1) of the Airport Facilities Act;
7. Deleted <May 12, 2026>
8. A person or entity that intends to operate aircraft for international flight operations;
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and maintain a safety management system in accordance with the state safety programme under paragraph (1) in order to perform the Minister's duties under paragraphs (1) through (4) of Article 83. <Amended on May 27, 2025>
(4) An air transport operator prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, among the air transport operators under subparagraph 4 of paragraph (2) shall prepare a flight data analysis programme including the following matters when establishing safety management system: <Added on Aug. 27, 2019; May 12, 2026>
1. Procedures for installing and operating equipment prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to collect flight data;
2. Matters concerning the protection and utilization of flight data and its analysis;
3. Any other requirements notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as the preservation of flight data and its quality management.
(5) A safety management system established by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or by the holder of an air traffic services certificate required to establish such a system under subparagraph 3 of paragraph (2) shall include the following matters where radar is to be used in providing air traffic control services under Article 83(1): <Added on August 27, 2019>
1. Procedures for the installation and operation of equipment to collect radar data;
2. Matters concerning the protection and utilization of radar data and its analysis.
(6) Any person or entity operating a safety management system under paragraph (2) or (3), including the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, shall use the aviation safety data collected for operating the system and the results of analysing those data only for the purposes of preventing aircraft accidents and ensuring aviation safety. No such person or entity shall, on the grounds of those analysis results, dismiss, transfer, discipline or otherwise treat unfairly a related person, or take any other adverse action against that person in relation to their status or treatment; provided, however, that this shall not apply where laws or regulations have been violated intentionally or through gross negligence, or a criminal act has been confirmed.
(7) Any person or entity that holds or intends to obtain two or more types of certificates or approvals under subparagraphs 1 through 6 of paragraph (2) (hereinafter referred to as "safety certification") may, considering the scope and interrelationship of the relevant services, integrate the individual safety management systems (hereinafter referred to as an "integrated safety management system") and maintain it after obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall apply to changes of an important approved matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(8) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct surveillance activities on the maintenance and operation of safety management systems and integrated safety management systems (hereinafter collectively referred to as "aviation safety management systems") on a regular or occasional basis, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. In such cases, surveillance activities may be conducted in conjunction with the surveillance activities for safety certifications prescribed in this Act and the Airport Facilities Act.
(9) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport finds any matters that may endanger aviation safety as a result of surveillance activities under paragraph (8), the Minister may order the holder of the relevant safety certification to take corrective action. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(10) Where safety certification expires, is suspended, revoked, or surrendered, the approval of the respective safety management systems shall also be deemed to have expired, been suspended, revoked, or surrendered. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(11) In addition to the matters provided in paragraphs (1) through (10), the following matters shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on August 27, 2019; May 12, 2026>
1. Requirements for establishing a state safety programme under paragraph (1);
2. The contents to be included in a safety management system under paragraph (2), the criteria for approval of the system and changes thereto, and the requirements for establishing and operating the system;
2-2. Criteria for recognition of the safety management system of a foreign service provider under the proviso to paragraph (2);
3. The contents to be included in a safety management system for the duties under paragraph (3), and the requirements for establishing and operating the system;
4. Criteria for approval of an integrated safety management system and changes thereto, and requirements for establishing and operating the system;
5. Requirements for conducting regular and occasional surveillance activities on aviation safety management systems and taking corrective action under paragraphs (8) and (9).
[Title Amended on August 27, 2019]

Article 58-2 (Revocation or suspension of approval for safety management systems)
(1) Where a person or entity that has obtained approval for a safety management system or an integrated safety management system falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke such approval or issue an order to suspend its effect for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke such approval:
1. Where the person or entity obtained the approval by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the person or entity falls under either of the following items, in violation of Article 58(2) or (7):
a. Where the person or entity operates the safety management systems differently from the approved details thereof;
b. Where the person or entity changes, without obtaining approval, any important matter concerning the safety management systems prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
3. Where the person or entity fails to comply with an order to take corrective actions, etc. under Article 58(9).
(2) Any other requirements for detailed standards, procedures, etc., for making dispositions under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 59 (Mandatory safety reporting)
(1) A relevant person, such as aviation personnel, who has caused any aircraft accident, serious incident, or incident prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act (hereinafter referred to as "incident subject to mandatory reporting") or has become aware of the occurrence of any aircraft accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting shall report the fact to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, a report submitted to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport on the occurrence of a failure, defect, or malfunction pursuant to Article 33 shall be deemed a report submitted under this Article. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall not provide any information received through a report under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "mandatory aviation safety report") to a third party or disclose it to the public; provided, this shall not apply in any of the following cases: <Added on August 27, 2019; May 12, 2026>
1. Where safety data and safety information may be provided in accordance with this Act;
2. Where necessary for performing the accident investigation duties referred to in subparagraph 7 of Article 2(1) of the Aviation and Railway Accident Investigation Act.
(3) No person or entity shall dismiss, transfer, discipline or otherwise treat unfairly a person who has submitted a mandatory aviation safety report, or take any other adverse action against that person in relation to their status or treatment, on the grounds of that report. <Added on Aug. 27, 2019>
(4) The scope of aviation personnel and other related persons under paragraph (1), the information to be included in a mandatory aviation safety report, and the timing, methods and procedures for submitting such a report shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on August 27, 2019>

Article 60 (Safety investigation)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives a report pursuant to Article 59(1), 120(2), or 129(3) or becomes aware of the occurrence of an aircraft accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting without receiving such a report, the Minister may conduct an investigation to ascertain the facts concerning the occurrence, determine whether this Act has been violated, and verify other relevant matters. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019; Jun. 9, 2020>
(2) Where an incident subject to mandatory reporting has been reported pursuant to Articles 33 and 59(1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may refrain from taking administrative enforcement action under this Act and the Airport Facilities Act, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act; provided that this shall not apply where an investigation under paragraph (1) establishes that the incident was caused intentionally or through gross negligence. <Added on Aug. 27, 2019; May 12, 2026>
(3) Article 132(2) and (4) through (9) shall apply mutatis mutandis to procedures, methods, etc. for conducting fact-finding investigations referred to in paragraph (1). <Amended on August 27, 2019>
(4) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), any other requirements for conducting fact-finding investigations shall be determined by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on August 27, 2019>

Article 61 (Voluntary safety reporting)
(1) Where any person causes or becomes aware of any incident other than an incident subject to mandatory reporting under Article 59(1) (hereinafter referred to as "incident subject to voluntary reporting") or becomes aware of, or suspects the occurrence of, an aviation safety hazard, the person may report such fact to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall not provide any information received through a report under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "voluntary aviation safety report") to a third party or disclose it to the public, except as provided in this Act. <Amended on August 27, 2019>
(3) No person or entity shall dismiss, transfer, discipline or otherwise treat unfairly a person who has submitted a voluntary aviation safety report, or take any other adverse action against that person in relation to their status or treatment, on the grounds of that report.
(4) Where a person who has caused an incident subject to voluntary reporting or a hazard submits a voluntary aviation safety report within 10 days of its occurrence, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall not take enforcement action against that person under this Act or the Airport Facilities Act, unless the person caused the incident or hazard intentionally or through gross negligence. <Amended on August 27, 2019; June 9, 2020>
(5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), the information to be included in a voluntary aviation safety report and the methods and procedures for submitting such a report shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 61-2 (Safety data collection and processing system)
(1) To promote aviation safety, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and operate a system for electronically processing the collection, storage, integration, analysis, etc. of safety data and safety information (hereinafter referred to as an "integrated safety data collection and analysis system"). <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) To establish and operate the integrated safety data collection and analysis system, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request a relevant administrative agency, a public institution under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, or a holder of a safety management system approval under Article 58(2) to submit safety data and safety information, if the relevant agency, institution or approval holder has concluded an agreement with the Minister on the provision of such data and information. Any agency, institution or approval holder receiving such a request shall comply with it unless there are justifiable grounds for not doing so. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(3) Where necessary, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the operation of the integrated safety data collection and analysis system to a relevant specialized institution, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and publish standards for the operation of the integrated safety data collection and analysis system, including the following: <Amended on May 12, 2026>
1. Procedures for collecting, storing and analysing safety data and safety information;
2. Methods and procedures for sharing analysis results with institutions that provide safety data and safety information;
3. Any other requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act as necessary for operating the integrated safety data collection and analysis system.
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may publish the results of analysing the safety data and safety information under paragraph (1) in publications. The content and methods of publication shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>
[This Article Newly Inserted on August 27, 2019]

Article 61-3 (Protection of personal information contained in safety data and safety information)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a specialized institution entrusted with the operation of the integrated safety data collection and analysis system under Article 61-2(3) shall use the safety data and safety information collected, stored and analysed under Article 61-2(1) only for the purposes of maintaining and improving aviation safety. In doing so, the Minister or the specialized institution shall establish and implement measures to ensure the protection of personal information as defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Personal Information Protection Act. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
[This Article Newly Inserted on August 27, 2019]

Article 62 (Authority and responsibilities of the pilot-in-command)
(1) A person responsible for safe flight of an aircraft (hereinafter referred to as "pilot in command") shall direct and supervise the crew members of the aircraft.
(2) No pilot in command shall take off an aircraft unless the pilot in command confirms that preparation necessary for the flight of an aircraft has been completed, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where the pilot in command deems that any hazard has occurred or is likely to occur to the aircraft or passengers aboard, the pilot in command may order passengers aboard the aircraft to abide by the method of evacuation and any other safety requirements.
(4) Where any hazard occurs to the aircraft during flight, the pilot in command shall rescue passengers and prepare measures necessary to prevent any hazard to people or articles on the ground or waters and shall not leave the aircraft unless the pilot in command has evacuated passengers and other persons aboard from the aircraft.
(5) In cases of any accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting, the pilot in command shall report the fact to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, where the pilot in command is unable to report the fact, the owner of the aircraft shall report it. <Amended on August 27, 2019>
(6) Where the pilot in command becomes aware that any accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting takes place in other aircraft, the pilot in command shall report the fact to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, the same shall not apply where the pilot in command becomes aware of the fact through radio equipment. <Amended on August 27, 2019>
(7) Where any interested party, such as aviation personnel, files reports pursuant to Article 59(1), the main clause of paragraph (5) and the main clause of paragraph (6) shall not apply.

Article 63 (Flight crew qualifications and proficiency checks)
(1) The pilot-in-command of any of the following aircraft shall obtain operational qualification from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport based on an assessment of knowledge and skills. Any other pilot of such aircraft shall obtain operational qualification from the Minister based on an assessment of skills:
1. Aircraft used for air transport operation;
2. Aircraft used for operation prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act among aircraft used for aerial work;
3. Aircraft for international flight operations.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct regular proficiency checks to determine whether persons who have obtained operational qualification under paragraph (1) possess the required knowledge or skills, as applicable. Where the Minister considers it particularly necessary, the Minister may conduct additional proficiency checks at any time.
(3) Where a person who has obtained operational qualification under paragraph (1) fails to undergo or pass a proficiency check under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke that person’s operational qualification.
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport considers it necessary, the Minister may authorize an air transport operator or aerial work operator designated as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to grant operational qualification under paragraph (1) to, or conduct proficiency checks under paragraph (2) for, its pilots-in-command or other pilots.
(5) A pilot-in-command or other pilot who has obtained operational qualification or passed a proficiency check under paragraph (4) shall be deemed to have obtained operational qualification under paragraph (1) and undergone a proficiency check under paragraph (2). In such cases, paragraph (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis.
(6) Notwithstanding paragraph (4), where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport considers it necessary, the Minister may conduct a proficiency check under paragraph (2) for a pilot-in-command or other pilot specified in the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(7) The pilot-in-command of an aircraft used for air transport operations shall meet the experience requirements for the areas, routes and airports in which or to which the pilot intends to operate, limited to those specified in the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(8) Any other requirements for the operational qualifications, proficiency checks and experience requirements referred to in paragraphs (1) through (7) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 64 (Flight crew proficiency checks using flight simulation training devices)
Where aspects of the assessment for granting operational qualification or conducting a proficiency check under Article 63, including emergency procedures, are difficult to assess using an aircraft, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may assess those aspects using a flight simulation training device designated by the Minister under Article 39-2(3). <Amended on May 18, 2021>
[Title Amended on May 18, 2021]

Article 65 (Flight operations officers/flight dispatchers)
(1) An air transport operator and the owner of aircraft for international flight operations shall employ flight operations officers/flight dispatchers, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) The pilot-in-command of an aircraft operated by a person or entity required to employ a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher under paragraph (1) shall obtain approval from the flight operations officer/flight dispatcher before commencing a flight or amending the flight plan.
(3) A person or entity required to employ a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher under paragraph (1) shall provide that officer/dispatcher with the training necessary to acquire the knowledge and experience required to perform the relevant duties properly, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 66 (Take-off and landing at aerodromes)
(1) No person shall take off or land an aircraft, other than a glider or airship, at a place that is not an aerodrome or at an aerodrome that does not meet the standards required for that aircraft. However, this prohibition shall not apply in any of the following cases:
1. Where permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport due to unavoidable circumstances, such as a safety-related emergency;
2. Where the take-off or landing is conducted in accordance with the operations specifications issued by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 90(2).
(2) Detailed criteria and procedures necessary for permission under subparagraph 1 of paragraph (1) and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 67 (Rules of the air)
(1) A person or entity that intends to operate an aircraft shall conduct flight operations in accordance with the standards, procedures, methods and other requirements concerning flight (hereinafter referred to as the "rules of the air") prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act in accordance with the Convention on International Civil Aviation and its Annexes. <Amended on December 30, 2025>
(2) The rules of the air shall be classified as follows:
1. Rules concerning general matters, such as flight procedures to protect property and human life;
2. Visual flight rules (VFR);
3. Instrument flight rules (IFR);
4. Rules concerning the preparation, submission, receipt, notification, etc. of flight plans;
5. Rules concerning any other requirements for safe flight.
(3) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where the standards, procedures, methods, etc. under the Convention on International Civil Aviation and its Annexes differ from the rules of the air, flight over the high seas shall be conducted in accordance with the Convention on International Civil Aviation and its Annexes. <Added on December 30, 2025>

Article 67-2 (Interception of aircraft and prohibition of the use of weapons against aircraft)
(1) No person shall use weapons against an aircraft that is in flight in accordance with this Act.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may take the following measures to identify an aircraft in flight or, where necessary, guide it safely. In taking such measures, the lives of persons on board and the safety of the aircraft’s operation shall not be endangered.
1. Approaching and identifying an aircraft;
2. Guiding or directing its flight path;
3. Directing it to land at a designated aerodrome;
4. Other measures necessary for the safe operation of the aircraft.
(3) The pilot-in-command of an aircraft subject to any measure under the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) (including a measure taken by the Minister of National Defense under Article 10(1) of the Act on the Operation of Military Aircraft) shall immediately comply with that measure in accordance with the procedures and methods prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, however, that where the aircraft is flying in the territorial airspace of a foreign State, the pilot-in-command shall comply with the measure in accordance with the procedures and methods prescribed by that State.
[This Article Added on May 12, 2026]

Article 68 (Prohibited flight operations and activities)
For the protection of life and property, a person who intends to operate an aircraft shall not conduct any of the following flights or acts; provided, however, that this shall not apply where permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
1. Flight below the lowest safe altitude prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
2. Dropping an object or aerial application;
3. Parachute descent;
4. Acrobatic flight, such as flying the aircraft overturned or flying the aircraft on its side in a region prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
5. Flight of a remotely piloted aircraft;
6. Other flights or acts prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, that jeopardize or are likely to jeopardize the lives and property of people.

Article 69 (Designation of aircraft for emergency duties)
(1) An owner who intends to use an aircraft for emergency duties prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the evacuation of emergency patients requiring immediate medical attention, shall obtain designation of the aircraft from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(2) Article 66, and subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 68 shall not apply where the owner of an aircraft designated under paragraph (1) by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "aircraft for emergency duties") operates the aircraft to perform emergency duties under paragraph (1).
(3) Matters regarding the designation, procedures for operation, etc. of an aircraft for emergency duties shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Where the owner of an aircraft for emergency duties falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke designation as the aircraft for emergency duties; provided, where the owner falls under subparagraph 1, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke designation as an aircraft for emergency duties:
1. Where the aircraft has been designated as an aircraft for emergency duties by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the aircraft fails to comply with the procedures for the operation thereof under paragraph (3).
(5) No person in whose case designation as an aircraft for emergency duties is revoked pursuant to paragraph (4) shall obtain designation as an aircraft for emergency duties within two years from the date such designation is revoked.

Article 70 (Transport of dangerous goods by air)
(1) A person or entity that intends to transport by aircraft highly explosive or flammable articles or other dangerous goods prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act (hereinafter referred to as "dangerous goods") shall obtain permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the holder of an air operator certificate (AOC) under Article 90(1) meets the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, including those concerning the communication of information on dangerous goods loaded on board, the holder shall be deemed to have obtained permission under paragraph (1).
(3) A person or entity that packs, loads, stores, transports or otherwise handles dangerous goods transported by aircraft (hereinafter referred to as a "dangerous goods handler", and such activities as "handling of dangerous goods") shall comply with the procedures and methods for handling dangerous goods determined and notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the prevention of aviation hazards and the safety of human life.

Article 71 (Inspection of packagings and receptacles for dangerous goods)
(1) A person or entity that intends to manufacture or import packagings and receptacles for the transport of dangerous goods for sale shall have those packagings and receptacles inspected for safety by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall prescribe and notify matters regarding methods of inspection, acceptance criteria, etc. of packagings and receptacles referred to in paragraph (1).
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate an agency that professionally performs inspection services concerning receptacles and packagings of dangerous goods (hereinafter referred to as "inspection agency for packagings and receptacles") and require the inspection agency for packagings and receptacles to conduct inspections referred to in paragraph (1).
(4) The criteria for designation of an inspection agency for packagings and receptacles, including requirements for inspection personnel and equipment, and requirements for its operation shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) Where an inspection agency for packagings and receptacles falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke its designation or order the suspension of all or part of its activities for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided that, where it falls under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke its designation: <Amended on Jan. 17, 2017>
1. Where it has been designated as the inspection agency for packagings and receptacles by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the inspection agency for packagings and receptacles conducts inspections referred to in paragraph (1) in violation of the inspection methods, acceptance criteria, etc. for packagings and receptacles referred to in paragraph (2);
3. Where it fails to meet the criteria for designation referred to in paragraph (4).
(6) Detailed criteria for the enforcement action under paragraph (5) and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 72 (Dangerous goods training)
(1) A dangerous goods handler subject to training under paragraph (2) shall undergo training on the handling of dangerous goods provided by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided that this requirement shall not apply where training on the handling of dangerous goods has been completed at a training institution recognized by international organizations, such as the International Civil Aviation Organization, and the International Air Transport Association.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall prescribe and publish the specific categories of dangerous goods handlers required to undergo training under paragraph (1), the training content and any other requirements.
(3) To provide the training under paragraph (1) efficiently, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may approve a training organization specializing in the handling of dangerous goods (hereinafter referred to as an "approved dangerous goods training organization") and require the holder of that approval to provide training for dangerous goods handlers.
(4) The criteria for approval of an approved dangerous goods training organization, including requirements for training personnel, facilities and equipment, and requirements for its operation shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) Where the holder of an approval under paragraph (3) falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke its approval or order the suspension of all or part of its activities for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided that, where it falls under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke its approval:
1. Where it has obtained approval by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where it fails to meet the criteria for approval under paragraph (4).
(6) Detailed criteria for the enforcement action under paragraph (5) and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 73 (Restrictions on the use of portable electronic devices)
To prevent adverse effects, including electromagnetic interference, on the navigation and communication equipment of aircraft in operation, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may restrict the use of portable electronic devices carried by passengers, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 74 (Approval of extended diversion time operations (EDTO))
(1) An air transport operator shall obtain approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, if the operator intends to operate an aeroplane with two or more engines prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act on a route containing a point from which the time required to fly, at the cruising speed specified in the relevant following subparagraph, to and land at the nearest airport exceeds the time prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
1. An aeroplane with two engines: Cruising airspeed when one engine is not in operation;
2. An aeroplane with at least three engines: Cruising airspeed when all engines are in operation.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to grant approval referred to in paragraph (1), the Minister shall confirm whether the aircraft meets Flight Safety Regulations notified pursuant to Article 77(1).

Article 75 (Approval for operations in RVSM and other specified airspace)
(1) An owner who intends to operate an aircraft in any of the following airspaces shall obtain approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, that this shall not apply in cases prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, including the operation of an aircraft for search and rescue in the airspace referred to in subparagraph 1:
1. Airspace in which a reduced vertical separation minimum is applied (hereinafter referred to as "Reduced Vertical Separation Minimum (RVSM) airspace");
2. Airspace permitted solely for the operation of aircraft that have specific navigation performance (hereinafter referred to as "performance-based navigation (PBN)-required airspace");
3. Other airspace prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to manage airspace in an efficient manner.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to grant approval referred to in paragraph (1), the Minister shall confirm whether the aircraft meets Flight Safety Regulations notified pursuant to Article 77(1).

Article 76 (Required crew complement and training)
(1) A person who intends to operate an aircraft shall have the necessary crew members on board for the safety of flight in the aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Deleted <May 12, 2026>
(3) An air transport operator or aerial work operator shall provide education and training necessary to perform the relevant services for the aircrew aboard the aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 77 (Flight safety regulations for aircraft operations)
(1) To ensure the safe operation of aircraft, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and notify Flight Safety Regulations containing the following matters, within the scope prescribed by this Act, the Convention on International Civil Aviation and its Annexes:
1. Licence;
2. Aviation training institutions;
3. Aircraft registration and display of the registration mark;
4. Aircraft airworthiness;
5. Approved Maintenance Organization (AMO) Certification Standards;
6. Aircraft instruments and equipment;
7. Operation of aircraft;
8. Operating certification of air transport operation and the management thereof;
9. Any other requirements for safe flight, which are prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Owners and aviation personnel shall abide by the Flight Safety Regulations referred to in paragraph (1).

CHAPTER VI AIR TRAFFIC MANAGEMENT, Aeronautical Information Management and Aeronautical Charts

Article 77-2 (Establishment and implementation of the national air navigation plan)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and implement a plan for national air navigation (hereinafter referred to as "national air navigation plan") for air traffic management in accordance with the global air navigation plan of the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO).
(2) A national air navigation plan shall include the following:
1. Objectives of and strategies for air traffic policies;
2. Matters regarding information on, operation of, and technology for air traffic;
3. Matters regarding evaluation of the efficiency, safety, etc. of air traffic management;
4. Any other requirements to enhance the safety, economics, and efficiency of air traffic.
(3) To establish and implement a national air navigation plan, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may organize and operate a consultative body composed of relevant administrative agencies, public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, airport operators, air transport operators, aircraft ground handling service providers referred to in subparagraph 20 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act, and related specialized institutions.
(4) Requirements for the composition and operation of the consultative body for pursuing a national air navigation plan under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on April 18, 2023]

Article 78 (Designation of airspace and establishment of instrument flight procedures)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to manage airspace in a systematic and efficient manner, the Minister may designate and announce a flight information region divided into the following regions of airspace:
1. Controlled airspace: Airspace including control zones and control areas, in which a pilot is required to receive instructions on the order, timing, method, etc. of controlling the flight of an aircraft for the safety of air traffic from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a person who has obtained air traffic services certification pursuant to Article 84(1);
2. Uncontrolled airspace: Airspace other than controlled airspace, in which it is necessary to provide flight advisory service and flight information to a pilot of an aircraft;
3. Regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces): Airspace in which it is necessary to prohibit or restrict the flight of an aircraft for the safety of air traffic;
4. Warning airspace: Airspace in which special attention, caution, identification, etc. is required when a pilot of an aircraft controls the flight of the aircraft.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary, the Minister may subdivide, designate and notify the airspace under paragraph (1), as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and announce standard departure and arrival procedures, approach procedures, airways, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "instrument flight procedures (IFPs)") for aircraft using airspace designated and announced pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2). <Added on March 19, 2024>
(4) A person who has obtained air traffic services certification may establish and announce instrument flight procedures after obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, airways shall be excluded. <Added on March 19, 2024>
(5) Any other requirements for the standards and procedures for establishing and designating airspace under paragraphs (1) through (4), the standards for establishing instrument flight procedures, and the procedures for announcing and approving such procedures shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on March 19, 2024>
[Title Amended on March 19, 2024]

Article 79 (Flight procedures and restrictions in designated airspace)
(1) A person who intends to operate an aircraft in uncontrolled airspace or warning airspace referred to in Article 78(1) shall adhere to the methods and procedures for flying aircraft determined and announced by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport with respect to such airspace or area.
(2) No person who intends to operate an aircraft shall fly the aircraft in regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces) referred to in Article 78(1); provided, however, that this prohibition shall not apply where the person has obtained permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, and flies the aircraft in accordance with the methods and procedures prescribed by the Minister for that airspace.

Article 80 (Establishment of Airspace Committee)
(1) The Airspace Committee shall be established under the authority of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to deliberate on requirements for the establishment and management of airspace referred to in Article 78.
(2) In addition to the matters provided for in paragraph (1), matters regarding the composition, operation, function, etc. of the Airspace Committee shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 81 (Inter-agency cooperation on air traffic safety and airspace management)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall mutually cooperate with the heads of relevant administrative agencies concerning the following in order to ensure air traffic safety. In such cases, the Minister and such heads shall consider national security: <Amended on April 18, 2023>
1. Matters concerning air traffic control;
2. Matters concerning efficient airspace management;
3. Matters concerning air traffic flow management under Article 83-2;
4. Any other requirements for air traffic safety.
(2) Detailed requirements for requesting cooperation under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 82 (Airspace management in wartime and declared united defense situations)
Wartime-related Acts and the United Defense Act shall apply to airspace management in wartime and at the time of proclamation of a united defense incident under the United Defense Act.

Article 83 (Provision of air traffic services)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate may provide air traffic control service to aircraft, light aircraft or other vehicles at an aerodrome or airport or within a control zone or control area.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate may provide advice and information related to the operation of aircraft or light aircraft, such as operational conditions of the aerodrome, airport, and navigation aids, to pilots, related agencies, etc. for the safe and efficient operation of aircraft or light aircraft in a flight information region.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate may provide information on an aircraft or light aircraft that requires search and rescue in a flight information region to pilots, related agencies, etc. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(4) To provide air traffic control services under paragraph (1) in a systematic manner, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall provide the training necessary to develop qualified air traffic controllers within the Ministry and maintain their proficiency. In such cases, the Minister shall establish a training system, including subjects, methods, personnel, facilities and equipment, equivalent to the criteria for approval of training organizations, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 27, 2025>
(5) Requirements concerning the areas, recipients, content and procedures for the provision of services by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate under paragraphs (1) through (3) (hereinafter referred to as "air traffic services"), and any other requirements, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on May 27, 2025>

Article 83-2 (Air traffic flow management)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall manage air traffic flow to ensure that the safe and efficient operation of aircraft is maintained by adjusting air traffic volumes to achieve a balance between the air traffic capacity and traffic volume and by easing air traffic congestion in advance.
(2) Air traffic services providers, air transport operators, airport operators, aircraft ground handling service providers defined in subparagraph 20 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act, and any other person or entity involved in aircraft operations shall fully cooperate in air traffic flow management prescribed in paragraph (1).
(3) Any other requirements for air traffic flow management under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on April 18, 2023]

Article 83-3 (Air traffic data collection, analysis and evaluation system)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and operate a system to collect, analyse, and evaluate air traffic data (hereinafter referred to as "air traffic data system") to support the safe, economical, and efficient operation of aircraft.
(2) To establish and operate the air traffic data system, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request a relevant administrative agency or a public institution under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions to submit air traffic data. Any agency or institution receiving such a request shall comply with it unless there are justifiable grounds for not doing so.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the operation of the air traffic data system to an institution specializing in air traffic data, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) For the operation of the air traffic data system, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and publish standards for its operation that include the following:
1. Procedures for collecting, storing and analysing air traffic data;
2. Methods and procedures for sharing the results of analysis with institutions that provide air traffic data;
3. Any other requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act as necessary for the operation of the air traffic data system.
[This Article Newly Inserted on April 18, 2023]

Article 84 (Compliance with air traffic control instructions)
(1) A person or entity that intends to move, take off, land or fly an aircraft at an aerodrome or airport or within a control zone or control area shall comply with instructions issued by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate concerning the sequence and timing of aircraft movements, take-offs and landings, and the manner in which flights are conducted.
(2) A person or entity that operates a vehicle, carries out maintenance or repairs, or performs other activities on the movement area of an aerodrome or airport shall comply with the instructions of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate to ensure air traffic safety.

Article 85 (Certification and continuing oversight of air traffic services providers)
(1) Where a person or entity other than the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to provide air traffic services, that person or entity shall have a system capable of supporting the provision of air traffic services (hereinafter referred to as an "air traffic services provision system") and shall obtain an air traffic services certificate from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall determine and notify the requirements for personnel, facilities, and equipment necessary for air traffic services certification, the requirements concerning the air traffic services manual, air traffic services certification procedures, etc. (hereinafter referred to as the "Air Traffic Services Certification Standards").
(3) When granting air traffic services certification, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall inspect whether the applicant meets the Air Traffic Services Certification Standards and, if the Minister finds that the applicant meets those Standards, the Minister shall issue an air traffic services certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) The holder of an air traffic services certificate shall maintain the air traffic services provision system in place when the certificate was issued and shall comply with the Air Traffic Services Certification Standards.
(5) Where the holder of an air traffic services certificate intends to make changes to an air traffic services provision system, the holder shall report the proposed changes to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, however, that where the holder intends to change important matters prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the air traffic services manual referred to in paragraph (2), the holder shall obtain approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(6) If a report of a change under the main clause of paragraph (5) contains no defect in the particulars stated in the report or its attached documents and meets the formal requirements prescribed by Acts, regulations or other rules, the reporting obligation shall be deemed fulfilled when the report reaches the receiving authority. <Added on August 9, 2017>
(7) Where the Air Traffic Services Certification Standards are amended and the air traffic services provision system of a certificate holder no longer complies with those Standards, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the holder to comply with the amended Standards. <Amended on August 9, 2017>
(8) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may conduct surveillance activities on a regular basis or at any time to determine whether the holder of an air traffic services certificate continues to maintain its air traffic services provision system. <Amended on August 9, 2017>
(9) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport discovers any matters that may endanger air traffic safety upon conducting surveillance activities under paragraph (8), the Minister may order corrective actions, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on August 9, 2017>

Article 85-2 (Validity period and renewal of air traffic services certification)
(1) The validity period of air traffic services certification shall be five years from the date certification is obtained under Article 85(1); where the air traffic services provision system is amended under paragraph (5) of that Article, the validity period shall be the remainder of the previous validity period.
(2) The holder of an air traffic services certificate who intends to continue providing air traffic services beyond the validity period under paragraph (1) shall renew such certification every five years before its expiration date, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where the holder of an air traffic services certificate fails to renew it under paragraph (2), the effect of such certification shall be suspended from the day following its expiration date.
(4) Where air traffic services certification has been suspended under paragraph (3), the holder shall renew that certification, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in order to restore its legal effect.
(5) Where air traffic services certification has been suspended under paragraph (3) and the holder fails to renew it under paragraph (4) within 12 months from the date of suspension, the certification shall cease to have legal effect on the day following the expiration of that period.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 85-3 (Prohibition of lending air traffic services certificates)
(1) The holder of an air traffic services certificate shall not permit another person or entity to provide air traffic services using the holder’s name, or lend the air traffic services certificate to another person or entity.
(2) No person or entity shall provide air traffic services using the name of the holder of an air traffic services certificate, or borrow the holder's certificate.
(3) No person or entity shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (1) or (2).
[This Article Added on May 12, 2026]

Article 86 (Revocation of air traffic services certification or suspension of air traffic services)
(1) Where the holder of an air traffic services certificate falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke such certification or order suspension of the provision of air traffic services for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the certification in cases falling under subparagraph 1, 6-2, 6-3, or 8. <Amended on August 9, 2017; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder obtained the air traffic services certificate by fraud or other improper means;
1-2. Where the holder conducts an assessment under Article 38-2(4) by improper means;
2. Where the holder falls under any of the following, in violation of Article 58(2):
a. Where the holder fails to establish a safety management system before providing air traffic services;
b. Where the holder operates a safety management system without approval;
c. Where the holder operates a safety management system in a manner inconsistent with the approval granted;
d. Where the holder changes, without approval, any important matters prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
3. Where the holder fails to maintain an air traffic services provision system, or provides air traffic services without complying with the Air Traffic Services Certification Standards, in violation of Article 85(4);
4. Where the holder alters an air traffic services provision system without reporting or approval, in violation of Article 85(5);
5. Where the holder fails to comply with an order to conform to the amended Air Traffic Services Certification Standards, in violation of Article 85(7);
6. Where the holder fails to comply with an order to take corrective action under Article 85(9);
6-2. Where the holder provides air traffic services during a period in which the effect of air traffic services certification is suspended under Article 85-2(3);
6-3. Where the holder has another person or entity provide air traffic services using the holder’s name, or lends an air traffic services certificate to another person or entity, in violation of Article 85-3(1);
7. Where the holder causes an aircraft accident intentionally or through gross negligence, or an aircraft accident occurs because the holder fails to exercise due care in managing and supervising aviation personnel under its supervision;
8. Where the holder provides air traffic services during suspension of air traffic services provision under this Article.
(2) Detailed criteria for the enforcement action under paragraph (1) and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 87 (Penalty surcharges on air traffic services certificate holders)
(1) Where the holder of an air traffic services certificate falls under subparagraph 1-2, any of subparagraphs 2 through 6, subparagraph 6-2, subparagraph 6-3, or subparagraph 7 of Article 86(1), and suspension of the provision of air traffic services is required, but such suspension is likely to cause serious inconvenience to aerodrome users or other persons or to harm the public interest, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 100 million won in lieu of the suspension. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) Specific standards and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges referred to in paragraph (1), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person or entity liable to pay a penalty surcharge referred to in paragraph (1) fails to make payment by the deadline, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected.

Article 88 (Establishment and implementation of an aircraft search and rescue support plan)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and implement an aircraft search and rescue support plan setting out the roles of the relevant administrative agencies, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, to support those agencies in searching for aircraft or rescuing persons in the event of aircraft distress.

Article 89 (Provision of aeronautical information)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall provide information necessary to ensure the safety, regularity and efficiency of aircraft operation (hereinafter referred to as "aeronautical information") to those who conduct flights in a flight information region.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall publish a chart in which information necessary for aircraft operation is provided, such as airways, air navigation facilities, aerodrome, airports, and control zones (hereinafter referred to as "aeronautical chart").
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may collect data necessary for the production of aeronautical information and aeronautical charts (hereafter referred to as "aeronautical data" in this Article) from airport operators, relevant administrative agencies, and other persons prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act (hereafter referred to as "provider of aeronautical data" in this Article). In such cases, the provider of aeronautical data shall provide the aeronautical data requested by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(4) Among the aeronautical information and charts referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide, for a fee, those prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, such aeronautical information and charts shall be provided without any cost to the institutions prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as relevant administrative agencies. <Added on Apr. 18, 2023; May 12, 2026>
(5) The details of aeronautical information or charts under paragraphs (1) through (4), methods of the provision thereof, units of measurement, the collection of aeronautical data, and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on Apr. 18, 2023; May 12, 2026>

Article 89-2 (Standardization of air traffic services and related activities)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall perform the following duties to ensure the safe and efficient establishment and publication of instrument flight procedures under Article 78(3), provision of air traffic services under Article 83, and provision of aeronautical information under Article 89(1) (hereafter in this Article collectively referred to as "air traffic services and related activities"):
1. Standardization of air traffic services and related activities;
2. Supply and demand, and education and training of personnel related to air traffic services and related activities;
3. Installation, improvement, and maintenance and repair of facilities and equipment related to air traffic services and related activities;
4. Verification and assessment of air traffic services and related activities;
5. Collection and analysis of statistics related to air traffic services and related activities;
6. Any other duties prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Any other requirements for the standardization and assessment of air traffic services and related activities under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on December 30, 2025]

CHAPTER VII SAFETY PROVISIONS FOR AIR OPERATORS AND MAINTENANCE ORGANIZATIONS

SECTION 1 SAFE OPERATION OF AIR TRANSPORT OPERATORS

Article 90 (Certification and continuing surveillance of air transport operators)
(1) A person or entity that is an air transport operator shall obtain an air operator certificate (AOC) after undergoing inspection of a safe operation system, such as human resources, equipment, facilities, assistance in flight operations and assistance in maintenance management, by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in accordance with the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act before that person or entity begins flight operations.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants an air operator certificate (AOC) under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as an "AOC"), the Minister shall issue the AOC to the holder together with operations specifications specifying the operating conditions and limitations prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act with respect to the routes and airports to be operated, methods of aircraft maintenance and other matters.
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to ensure the safe operation of aircraft, the Minister may modify operations specifications referred to in paragraph (2) ex officio or at the request of the AOC holder.
(4) The holder of an AOC or aviation personnel belonging to the holder shall comply with the operations specifications referred to in paragraph (2).
(5) The holder of an AOC shall maintain the safe operation system in place when the initial AOC was obtained. If the safe operation system is changed for any of the following reasons, the holder shall have the system inspected by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on June 10, 2022>
1. Where a new type of aircraft that is not registered in the operations specifications issued under paragraph (2) is introduced;
2. Where the holder of an AOC whose validity is suspended under paragraph (9) intends to resume the operation;
3. Where an additional route  is opened;
4. Where air transport services are transferred or taken over under Article 21 of the Aviation Business Act;
5. Where businesses are merged under Article 22 of the Aviation Business Act.
(6) In order to ensure the safe operation of aircraft, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct surveillance activities, on a regular or occasional basis, to determine whether the holder of an AOC maintains a safe operation system, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(7) Where, during an inspection under paragraph (6), any of the following occurs and emergency action is required, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, require the AOC holder to suspend the operation of an aircraft or a route, or require aviation personnel to suspend their duties:
1. Where any matters that may affect airworthiness of aircraft are identified;
2. Where it is identified that aviation personnel related to the operation of aircraft fail to meet the requirements necessary for them to be engaged in the relevant duties pursuant to this Act, such as education and training or operational qualification;
3. Where the holder fails to comply with the standards prescribed by this Act for safe operation of aircraft, such as standards of flight times or flight rules;
4. Where the condition of an airport or runway, or other relevant circumstances, could endanger the safe operation of aircraft;
5. Where the Minister deems that any other conditions may affect the safe operation system.
(8) Where the grounds for a suspension under paragraph (7) cease to exist, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall lift the suspension without delay.
(9) When the holder of an AOC suspends the operation for more than 60 consecutive days, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue an order to suspend the validity of the air operator certificate (AOC) for the safe operation of aircraft and the safety of passengers. <Added on June 10, 2022>
(10) Where an inspection under paragraph (5) finds that safe operation of aircraft is possible, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue an order to lift the suspension of the air operator certificate under paragraph (9) for the holder. <Added on Jun. 10, 2022>

Article 90-2 (Periodic review of the continued validity of air operator certificates)
(1) The holder of an air operator certificate under Article 90(1) shall apply to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for an inspection to verify whether the holder’s safe operating system is maintained (hereinafter referred to as a "validity inspection"). The application shall be made no later than three months before the fifth anniversary of the date on which the air operator certificate was issued (where a validity inspection has been conducted under paragraph (3), referring to the date on which the holder was notified of the inspection results under that paragraph).
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where the holder of an air operator certificate undergoes an inspection conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under subparagraphs 4 or 5 of Article 90(5) and is notified of compliance, the holder shall apply for a validity inspection no later than three months before the fifth anniversary of the date of such notification.
(3) Where the holder of an air operator certificate applies for a validity inspection under paragraph (1) or (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct the validity inspection and notify the holder of the inspection results.
(4) Application procedures, the content and methods of inspections, notification of inspection results, and any other requirements for validity inspections under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 90-3 (Prohibition of lending or borrowing air operator certificates and misuse of names)
(1) The holder of an air operator certificate shall not permit another person or entity to conduct operations under the certificate using the holder’s name, or lend the air operator certificate to another person or entity.
(2) No person or entity shall perform services under an air operator certificate in the name of the certificate holder, or borrow the certificate.
(3) No person or entity shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (1) or (2).
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 90-4 (Establishment and operation of a risk-based surveillance system)
(1) To efficiently conduct inspections under Article 90(6) or validity inspections, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may analyze safety risks using the following data and prioritize the subjects and scope of inspections based on the results thereof:
1. Results of identifying hazards and assessing safety risks under subparagraph 2 of Article 58(1);
2. Reports, document submissions, and inspection results regarding services under Article 132(1) through (3);
3. Results of inspections concerning air operator certificates and related safety management systems;
4. Any other data deemed necessary by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for safety risk analysis.
(2) In order to perform the duties under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and operate a system that analyzes safety risk through electronic means (hereinafter referred to as "risk-based safety oversight system").
(3) If deemed necessary, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the establishment and operation of a risk-based safety oversight system to a relevant specialized agency prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Any other requirements for the safety risk analysis under paragraph (1), the use of its results, and the establishment, operation, and maintenance of the risk-based safety oversight system shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 91 (Revocation of air operator certificates or suspension of aircraft operations)
(1) Where the holder of an air operator certificate falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the certificate or order suspension of aircraft operations for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, the Minister shall revoke the certificate in cases falling under subparagraph 1, 39, 39-2, 39-3, 39-5, or 49. <Amended on December 26, 2017; August 27, 2019; June 9, 2020; December 8, 2020; January 18, 2022; June 10, 2022; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder has obtained the air operator certificate (AOC) by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder operates an aircraft on which the nationality, registration mark, and the name or title of the owner are not displayed, in violation of Article 18(1);
3. Where the holder operates an aircraft which airworthiness certificate is not granted to, in violation of Article 23(3);
4. Where the holder operates an aircraft, or uses an aircraft, engine, propeller, component, or part, without complying with an Airworthiness Directive (AD) concerning maintenance of the aircraft, engine, propeller, component, or part, or an order for any other inspection, maintenance, issued under Article 23(9) to maintain the airworthiness of the aircraft;
5. Where the holder operates an aircraft that has failed to obtain noise certification or that has failed to meet Korea Airworthiness Standards, in violation of Article 25(2);
6. Where the holder fails to comply with a request to conform to the amended Korea Airworthiness Standards, in violation of Article 26;
7. Where the holder uses Technical Standard Order (TSO) articles without Technical Standard Order (TSO) Authorization on aircraft, engine, or propeller, in violation of Article 27(3);
8. Where the holder uses components or parts that have failed to obtain parts manufacturer certification on aircraft, engine, propeller, or components, in violation of Article 28(3);
9. Where the holder operates an aircraft, engine, or propeller without approval of repair or modification, or uses components or parts that have failed to obtain approval of repair or modification on such aircraft, engine, or propeller, in violation of Article 30(2);
10. Where the holder operates an aircraft, engine, or propeller that has undergone maintenance checks or uses components or parts on the aircraft, engine, or propeller, without obtaining confirmation of airworthiness on such aircraft, engine, or propeller, components or parts, in violation of Article 32(1);
11. Where the holder has a flight crew member who fails to meet the standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence referred to in Article 40(2) be engaged in aviation duties, in violation of Article 42(1);
12. Where the holder operates an aircraft in which radio equipment prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act has not been installed or an aircraft in which installed radio equipment is inoperative, in violation of Article 51;
13. Where the holder operates an aircraft without installing or carrying flight instruments, etc., or fails to adhere to the method of the operation thereof, in violation of Article 52;
14. Where the holder operates an aircraft without loading an amount of fuel prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 53;
15. Where the holder fails to indicate the position of an aircraft with a beacon, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, when the holder operates the aircraft or parks or holds the aircraft in an airport at night, in violation of Article 54;
16. Where the holder assigns a flight crew member who does not have flight experience prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to operate an aircraft, to conduct an IFR flight or night flight, or to be engaged in flight instruction, in violation of Article 55;
17. Where the holder fails to manage fatigue of crew members or flight operations officers/flight dispatchers under the holder’s supervision, in violation of Article 56(1);
18. Where the holder operates a fatigue risk management system or changes important matters without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 56(2);
19. Where the holder permits aviation personnel or cabin crew members to be engaged in aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members under a condition that they are unable to normally perform aviation duties or the duties of cabin crew members under the influence of alcohol, etc. in violation of Article 57(1);
20. Where the holder falls under any of the following, in violation of Article 58(2):
a. Where the holder fails to build a safety management system before starting business;
b. Where the holder operates a safety management system without approval;
c. Where the holder operates a safety management system differently from the details thereof approved;
d. Where the holder changes, without approval, any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
21. Where the holder fails to report an accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting that has occurred, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of the proviso of Article 62(5);
22. Where the holder grants operational qualification or conducts an examination of qualification of the pilot in command or other pilots belonging to the holder by fraudulent means when granting operational qualification or conducting an examination of qualification pursuant to Article 63(4);
23. Where the holder assigns a pilot in command who does not meet the experience requirements for the area, route, and airport of intended operation to operate an aircraft, in violation of Article 63(7);
24. Where the holder fails to employ a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher, in violation of Article 65(1);
25. Where the holder assigns a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher to be engaged in the relevant duties without providing education and training necessary to perform the relevant duties, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 65(3);
26. Where the holder permits flight crew to perform the takeoff from or landing at an area other than the aircraft takeoff or landing area, in violation of Article 66;
27. Where the holder permits a person to control the flight of an aircraft or perform an act falling under any subparagraph of Article 68, in violation of that Article;
28. Where the holder transports dangerous goods by using an aircraft without obtaining permission, in violation of Article 70(1);
29. Where the holder handles dangerous goods without complying with the procedures and methods for handling dangerous goods notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 70(3);
30. Where the holder assigns a person who has failed to undergo training on dangerous goods handling to handle dangerous goods, in violation of Article 72(1);
31. Where the holder operates an aeroplane without obtaining approval, in violation of Article 74(1);
32. Where the holder operates an aircraft in airspace falling under any subparagraph of Article 75(1) without obtaining approval, in violation of Article 75(1);
33. Where the holder operates an aircraft without a flight crew member on board necessary for the flight safety, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 76(1);
34. Where the holder fails to provide education and training necessary to perform the relevant duties to crew members on board an aircraft, in violation of Article 76(3);
35. Where the holder operates an aircraft or performs duties without complying with the Flight Safety Regulations under Article 77(1), in violation of Article 77(2);
36. Where the person or entity commenced aircraft operations without obtaining an air operator certificate, in violation of Article 90(1);
37. Where the holder fails to comply with operations specifications, in violation of Article 90(4);
38. Where the holder fails to maintain a safe operation system or operates an aircraft without undergoing an inspection of the changed safe operation system, in violation of Article 90(5);
39. Where the holder operates an aircraft without complying with the suspension of the operation of an aircraft or airway, in violation of Article 90(7);
39-2. Where the holder operates an aircraft during the suspension of the validity of the air operator certificate (AOC) prescribed in Article 90(9);
39-3. Where the holder operates an aircraft without undergoing a validity inspection, in violation of Article 90-2(1);
39-4. Where a validity inspection conducted under Article 90-2(3) finds that the safe operating system is not properly maintained;
39-5. Where the holder has another person or entity perform services under an air operator certificate using the holder’s name, or lends the air operator certificate to another person or entity, in violation of Article 90-3(1);
40. Where the holder prepares an operations manual or maintenance control manual, or changes any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, without obtaining authorization from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of the main clause of Article 93(1) or the proviso of paragraph (2) of that Article;
41. Where the holder amends the operations manual or maintenance control manual without reporting the amendment to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of the main clause of Article 93(2);
42. Where the holder fails to provide relevant personnel with the operations manual or maintenance control manual authorized under the main clause of Article 93(1) or the proviso of paragraph (2) of that Article, or reported under the main clause of paragraph (2) of that Article, in violation of the former part of Article 93(7);
43. Where the holder operates or maintains an aircraft without complying with the operations manual or maintenance control manual authorized under the main clause of Article 93(1) or the proviso of paragraph (2) of that Article, or reported under the main clause of paragraph (2) of that Article, in violation of the latter part of Article 93(7);
44. Where the holder fails to comply with a safety improvement directive for the safety of air transport under any subparagraph of Article 94;
45. Where the holder fails to report duties (only applicable to duties to perform aviation safety activities) or to submit documents pursuant to Article 132(1), or files false reports or submits false documents;
46. Where the holder refuses, obstructs, or evades access to aircraft, engine, propeller, or inspection (only applicable to inspection to perform aviation safety activities) of books, documents, etc., under Article 132(2);
47. Where the holder fails to answer questions (only applicable to questions to perform aviation safety activities) about relevant persons referred to in Article 132(2) or gives a false answer;
48. Where the holder causes an accident or serious incident by intent or gross negligence or by neglecting the holder’s duty to pay considerable attention to the selection of and supervision over aviation personnel;
49. Where the holder operates an aircraft during the period of suspension of the operation of aircraft under this Article.
(2) Any other requirements for detailed standards, procedures, etc., for making dispositions under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 92 (Imposition of penalty surcharges on air transport operators)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport is required to order the suspension of aircraft operations because the holder of an air operator certificate falls under any of subparagraphs 2 through 38, 39-4, or 40 through 48 of Article 91(1), but such suspension is likely to cause severe inconvenience to aircraft users, etc. or to harm the public interest, the Minister may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 10 billion won in lieu of the suspension of aircraft operations. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) Specific standards and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges referred to in paragraph (1), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person or entity liable to pay a penalty surcharge referred to in paragraph (1) fails to make payment by the deadline, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected.

Article 93 (Operations manuals and maintenance control manuals of air transport operators)
(1) A person or entity that is an air transport operator shall formulate an operations manual and a maintenance control manual and obtain authorization from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport before the operation of aircraft, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided however, that the same shall not apply where that person or entity obtains the air operator certificate (AOC) which includes an operations manual and a maintenance control manual.
(2) Where a person or entity that is an air transport operator intends to amend an operations manual or maintenance control manual authorized under the main clause of paragraph (1), that person or entity shall report the amendment to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided however, that authorization from the Minister shall be obtained to change any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the minimum equipment list or a crew training programme.
(3) When granting authorization under the main clause of paragraph (1) or the proviso of paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall confirm whether the operations manual, maintenance control manual or any amendment thereto complies with the Flight Safety Regulations under Article 77(1).
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants authorization pursuant to the main clause of paragraph (1) or the proviso of paragraph (2), the Minister may add conditions or a deadline or change conditions or the deadline; provided however, that such conditions or deadline shall be the minimum necessary to promote public interest or grant authorization, and shall not impose an unfair burden on the relevant person or entity that is an air transport operator.
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall notify the reporter of whether to accept the relevant report within 10 days from the date of receipt of the report under the main clause of paragraph (2). <Added on June 9, 2020>
(6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport fails to notify the reporter of whether to accept the report or of an extension of the processing period pursuant to the statutes or regulations governing processing of civil petitions within the period prescribed in paragraph (5), the report shall be deemed accepted on the day after the end of such period (referring to the relevant processing period, where the processing period is extended or re-extended pursuant to the statutes or regulations governing processing of civil petitions). <Added on June 9, 2020>
(7) A person or entity that is an air transport operator shall provide personnel engaged in aircraft operations or maintenance with the operations manual or maintenance control manual authorized by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under the main clause of paragraph (1) or the proviso of paragraph (2), or filed with the Minister under the main clause of paragraph (2). That person or entity and such personnel shall comply with such applicable manual. <Amended on June 9, 2020>

Article 94 (Safety improvement orders for air transport operators)
Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary for the safety of air transport, the Minister may issue to a person or entity that is an air transport operator any of the following safety improvement directives:
1. Improvement of aircraft and other facilities;
2. Requirements for implementing an international treaty concerning aviation;
3. Any other requirements necessary to eliminate elements interfering with safe operation of aircraft.

SECTION 2 SAFE OPERATION OF AERIAL WORK OPERATOR

Article 95 (Revocation of aerial work operator certificate or suspension of aircraft operations)
(1) Where the holder of an air operator certificate under Article 90, as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(1), falls under any subparagraph of Article 91(1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke air operator certification or issue an order to suspend the operation of aircraft for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that in cases falling under subparagraphs 1, 39, 39-2, 39-3, 39-5, or 49 of Article 91(1), the Minister shall revoke air operator certification. <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022; May 12, 2026>
(2) Where a person or entity that is an aerial work operator and does not hold an air operator certificate under Article 90, as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(1), falls under any of subparagraphs 2 through 22, 26 through 30, and 32 through 48 of Article 91(1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may issue an order to suspend the operation of aircraft for a specified period not exceeding six months.
(3) Detailed standards and procedures for making dispositions under paragraphs (1) and (2), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on January 17, 2017>
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport is required to issue an order to suspend the operation of aircraft under paragraph (1) or (2), in which case the suspension of the operation thereof causes serious inconvenience to users, etc. of aircraft or harms public interest, the Minister may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 300 million won in lieu of such suspension. <Amended on January 17, 2017>
(5) Specific criteria and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges under paragraph (4), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on January 17, 2017>
(6) Where a person or entity liable to pay a penalty surcharge referred to in paragraph (4) fails to make payment by the deadline, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected. <Amended on January 17, 2017>

Article 96 (Provisions applied mutatis mutandis to aerial work operators)
(1) Articles 90, 90-2, and 90-3 shall apply mutatis mutandis to a person or entity that is an aerial work operator performing services prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Articles 93 and 94 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the authorization of regulations for aircraft operation or regulations for aircraft maintenance of a person or entity that is an aerial work operator.

SECTION 3 APPROVAL AND OVERSIGHT OF MAINTENANCE ORGANIZATIONS

Article 97 (Approval and continuing oversight of maintenance organizations)
(1) A person or entity that is an aircraft maintenance service provider or a foreign aircraft maintenance service provider and intends to perform duties prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, including maintenance of an aircraft that has acquired nationality of the Republic of Korea under Article 8 and of any engine, propeller, component, or part used thereon, shall, before commencing such duties, have personnel, facilities, etc. that meet the standards for personnel, facilities, inspection systems, etc. determined and notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as the "Approved Maintenance Organization (AMO) Certification Standards") and shall obtain approval from the Minister (hereinafter referred to as "maintenance organization approval"); provided however, that the holder of a maintenance organization approval issued by a State that has concluded an aviation safety agreement with the Republic of Korea concerning maintenance organization approval shall be deemed to hold a maintenance organization approval issued by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(2) When granting a maintenance organization approval, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue detailed operating specifications prescribing the scope and methods of maintenance, quality-control procedures, and other matters, together with the maintenance organization certificate, to the holder.
(3) Where the holder of a maintenance organization approval performs maintenance of an aircraft, engine, propeller, component, or part, the holder shall adhere to the maintenance methods and procedures prescribed by the manufacturer or approved by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(4) The holder of a maintenance organization approval shall maintain the approved particulars. Where the holder intends to modify any important particular prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, the holder shall undergo an inspection conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, conduct regular or ad hoc inspections to verify whether the holder of a maintenance organization approval continues to comply with the terms of the approval. <Added on May 12, 2026>
(6) Where an inspection under paragraph (5) finds any matter that may endanger the safe operation of an aircraft, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the holder of a maintenance organization approval to take corrective actions, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 12, 2026>

Article 97-2 (Validity and renewal of maintenance organization approvals)
(1) The validity period of a maintenance organization approval shall run from the date of approval for the period specified in each of the following subparagraphs. Where approved particulars are amended under Article 97(4), the validity period shall be the remainder of the previous validity period:
1. The holder of a maintenance organization approval located in the Republic of Korea: Five years;
2. The holder of a maintenance organization approval located outside the Republic of Korea: Two years.
(2) The holder of a maintenance organization approval that intends to continue performing maintenance or other services under Article 97(1) beyond the validity period under paragraph (1) shall renew the approval, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, before its expiration date at each interval specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
(3) Where the holder of a maintenance organization approval fails to renew the approval under paragraph (2), its validity shall be suspended from the day following its expiration date.
(4) The holder of a maintenance organization approval whose validity has been suspended under paragraph (3) shall renew the approval, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in order to restore its validity.
(5) Where the holder of a maintenance organization approval whose validity has been suspended under paragraph (3) fails to renew the approval under paragraph (4) within 12 months from the date of suspension, the approval shall cease to be effective on the day following the expiration of that period.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 97-3 (Prohibition of lending maintenance organization certificates)
(1) The holder of a maintenance organization approval shall not permit another person or entity to perform maintenance activities under the approval using the holder’s name, or lend the maintenance organization certificate to another person or entity.
(2) No person or entity shall perform services under a maintenance organization approval in the name of the holder of that approval, or borrow the maintenance organization certificate.
(3) No person or entity shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (1) or (2).
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 98 (Revocation or suspension of maintenance organization approvals)
(1) Where the holder of a maintenance organization approval falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the approval or order the suspension of its validity for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the approval in cases falling under subparagraph 1, 2-3, 5, or 6: <Amended on May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder has obtained the maintenance organization approval by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder falls under any of the following, in violation of Article 58(2):
a. Where the holder fails to build a safety management system before commencing services;
b. Where the holder operates a safety management system without approval;
c. Where the holder operates a safety management system differently from the details thereof approved;
d. Where the holder changes, without approval, any important matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
2-2. Where the holder fails to comply with an order to take corrective actions under Article 97(6);
2-3. Where the holder permits another person or entity to perform services under the maintenance organization approval using the holder’s name, or lends the maintenance organization certificate to another person or entity, in violation of Article 97-3(1);
3. Where the holder violates the Approved Maintenance Organization (AMO) Certification Standards without justifiable cause;
4. Where the holder causes an aircraft accident by intent or gross negligence, or by neglecting the holder’s duty to exercise due care in the management and supervision of aviation personnel;
5. Where the holder performs services during the period of suspension of the effect under this Article.
6. Where the holder’s registration as an aircraft maintenance business has been revoked under Article 42(1) of the Aviation Business Act.
(2) Standards for making dispositions referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 99 (Penalty surcharges on holders of maintenance organization approvals)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport is required to order the suspension of the validity of a maintenance organization approval because the holder falls under any of subparagraphs 2, 2-2, 3, or 4 of Article 98(1), but such suspension is likely to cause severe inconvenience to users of the relevant services or to harm the public interest, the Minister may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 500 million won in lieu of the suspension. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) Specific standards and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges referred to in paragraph (1), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) Where a person or entity liable to pay a penalty surcharge pursuant to paragraph (1) fails to make payment by the deadline, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected.

CHAPTER VIII Safe operation of FOREIGN AIRCRAFT

Article 100 (Flight of foreign aircraft)
(1) Where the operator of an aircraft of foreign nationality (including a foreign country, a foreign public organization, or a person corresponding thereto) intends to conduct flight falling under any of the following, the operator shall obtain permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, however, that the same shall not apply to an operator that has obtained permission referred to in Articles 54 and 55 of the Aviation Business Act:
1. A flight where an aircraft takes off outside the territorial airspace of the Republic of Korea and lands at an airport in the Republic of Korea;
2. A flight where an aircraft takes off from an airport in the Republic of Korea and lands outside the territorial airspace of the Republic of Korea;
3. A flight where an aircraft takes off outside the territorial airspace of the Republic of Korea, overflies the territorial airspace of the Republic of Korea without landing at an airport in the Republic of Korea, and lands outside the territorial airspace of the Republic of Korea.
(2) In applying paragraph (1), an aircraft used for services of the armed forces, customs or police of a foreign country shall be deemed the aircraft used by the relevant country.
(3) At the request of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, a person who conducts flight falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) shall land the aircraft, without delay, at an aerodrome designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 101 (Cabotage by foreign aircraft)
No aircraft of foreign nationality (excluding an aircraft operated for such transport by the holder of permission under Article 54 or 55 of the Aviation Business Act) shall be operated between places in the Republic of Korea; provided, however, that this shall not apply where permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 102 (Recognition of certificates)
Any certification granted or other act performed by the government of the State of Registry with respect to the airworthiness of any of the following aircraft or the qualifications of its flight crew shall be deemed to have been granted or performed under this Act:
1. Aircraft of foreign nationality conducting a flight falling under any subparagraph of Article 100(1);
2. Aircraft of foreign nationality operated by the holder of permission under Article 54 or 55 of the Aviation Business Act.

Article 103 (Approval of air operator certificates and continuing surveillance of foreign air transport operators)
(1) A person or entity seeking permission to provide international air transport services as a foreign operator under Article 54 of the Aviation Business Act shall, in accordance with the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, obtain approval of the air operator certificate (AOC) and operations specifications specifying operating conditions and limitations, issued by the State of the Operator, from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(2) When granting approval of an air operator certificate under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may issue to the holder, together with the certificate of approval, a document specifying operating conditions and limitations concerning the routes, airports and other operational matters.
(3) The holder of permission to provide international air transport services as a foreign operator under Article 54 of the Aviation Business Act (hereinafter referred to as a "foreign air transport operator") and aviation personnel belonging to the holder shall comply with the operating conditions and limitations specified under paragraph (2).
(4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may modify operating conditions and limitations referred to in paragraph (2) for safe operation of aircraft operated by the holder of permission referred to in paragraph (3), as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) Where the opening of a route in the Republic of Korea or any other reason requires a change to the approval of an air operator certificate (AOC) or to any operating condition or limitation, the holder of the approval shall obtain approval of the change from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(6) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may inspect aircraft operated by the holder of permission referred to in paragraph (3) for the safe operation of aircraft.
(7) Where, during an inspection under paragraph (6), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport finds any matter that may seriously endanger the safe operation of aircraft unless urgent measures are taken, the Minister may suspend the operation of the relevant aircraft or the duties of aviation personnel, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(8) Where the grounds for a suspension under paragraph (7) cease to exist, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall lift or modify the suspension without delay.
(9) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may set the validity period of the approval under paragraph (1) to be the same as that of the air operator certificate issued by the State of the Operator. <Added on May 12, 2026>

Article 104 (Operating requirements and surveillance for foreign air transport operators)
(1) The holder of permission referred to in Article 103(3) shall operate aircraft with the following documents carried on board, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
1. The certificate of approval and the document specifying operating conditions and limitations issued by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 103(2);
2. Copies of the air operator certificate (AOC) and the operations specifications issued by the State of the Operator;
3. Other documents that must be carried on board aircraft in accordance with the Convention on International Civil Aviation and its Annexes.
(2) The holder of permission referred to in Article 103(3) and aviation personnel belonging to the holder shall comply with the operations specifications referred to in subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1).
(3) For the safe operation of aircraft, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may inspect, on a regular or occasional basis and as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, whether the holder of permission referred to in Article 103(3) and aviation personnel belonging to the holder comply with the operations specifications referred to in subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1). <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(4) Where, during a regular or occasional inspection under paragraph (3), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport finds any matter that may seriously endanger the safe operation of an aircraft unless urgent measures are taken, the Minister may suspend the operation of the relevant aircraft or the duties of aviation personnel, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) Where the grounds for a suspension under paragraph (4) cease to exist, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall lift the suspension without delay.

Article 105 (Revocation of foreign operator approval or suspension of aircraft operations)
(1) Where the holder of permission referred to in Article 103(3) falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke approval of the air operator certificate or order suspension of aircraft operations for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke such approval in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 7. <Amended on May 18, 2021; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder has obtained approval of the air operator certificate (AOC) by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder operates an aircraft without obtaining approval of the air operator certificate (AOC), in violation of Article 103(1);
3. Where the holder fails to comply with the operating conditions and limitations referred to in Article 103(2), in violation of Article 103(3);
4. Where the holder operates an aircraft without obtaining approval of a change, in violation of Article 103(5);
5. Where the holder permits a pilot to engage in aviation duties while the pilot is unable to perform such duties normally under the influence of alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 106(1);
6. Where the holder fails to comply with a safety improvement directive for the safety of air transport under any subparagraph of Article 94, as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 106(2);
7. Where the holder operates an aircraft during the period of suspension of the operation of the aircraft under this Article.
8. Where the air operator certificate issued to the holder by the State of the Operator is revoked or its validity is suspended;
(2) Any other requirements, such as detailed standards for making dispositions under paragraph (1), shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 106 (Provisions applied mutatis mutandis to foreign air transport operators)
(1) Article 57 shall apply mutatis mutandis to restrictions on the consumption or use of alcohol, etc. by pilots operating aircraft for the holder of permission referred to in Article 103(3).
(2) Articles 59, 61, 92 and 94 shall apply mutatis mutandis to mandatory and voluntary aviation safety reporting and related matters of the holder of permission referred to in Article 103(3).
[Wholly Amended on May 18, 2021]

Article 107 (Operational safety assessment for commercial air transport by foreign aircraft)
A person or entity seeking permission under Article 55 of the Aviation Business Act to transport persons or property for remuneration using aircraft of foreign nationality shall, in accordance with the standards prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, undergo an operational safety assessment by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport with respect to the air operator certificate (AOC) and operations specifications specifying operating conditions and limitations, issued by the State of the Operator.

CHAPTER IX LIGHT AIRCRAFT

Article 108 (Safety certification and maintenance release for light aircraft)
(1) Except where permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in a case prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a test flight, no person or entity that owns or has the right to use a light aircraft (hereinafter referred to as a "light aircraft owner") shall conduct a flight unless that person or entity has obtained, from the head of an agency or organization prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act and in accordance with the validity period and procedures and methods established by that head, safety certification that the light aircraft complies with the technical standards for flight safety determined and notified by the Minister. In such cases, the head of the agency or organization shall obtain the Minister's approval for the validity period and procedures and methods for safety certification, and the same shall apply to any change thereto.
(2) Where the head of an agency or organization prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act provides safety certification pursuant to paragraph (1), the head shall grant the rating and designate the scope of operation based on the rating, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) A light aircraft owner or a person who intends to fly a light aircraft shall conduct the flight in compliance with the scope of operation based on the rating of safety certification granted pursuant to paragraph (2).
(4) Where a light aircraft owner or a person who intends to fly a light aircraft has performed maintenance on the light aircraft or any component or part thereof, the owner or person shall not fly the aircraft unless the holder of an aircraft maintenance technician licence under subparagraph 8 of Article 35 has confirmed, in the manner prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, that the aircraft may be safely operated; provided, however, that this shall not apply to minor maintenance prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 109 (Licensing of light aircraft pilots)
(1) A person who intends to fly a light aircraft shall obtain a licence from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as a "light aircraft pilot licence"), as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) No person falling under any of the following shall be eligible to obtain a light aircraft pilot licence:
1. A person under the age of 17;
2. A person whose light aircraft pilot licence was revoked under Article 114(1), where two years have not elapsed since the date of revocation.

Article 110 (Scope of privileges of light aircraft pilots)
The holder of a light aircraft pilot licence shall perform only duties involving boarding and piloting a light aircraft (hereinafter referred to as "light aircraft piloting duties"); provided, however, that this restriction shall not apply where the holder has obtained permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, to conduct a test flight or similar flight on board a new category of light aircraft.

Article 111 (Ratings for light aircraft pilots)
(1) When granting a light aircraft pilot licence, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may limit the licence to a category of light aircraft.
(2) The holder of a light aircraft pilot licence with a category rating under paragraph (1) shall not pilot a light aircraft of a category not covered by that rating.
(3) Detailed requirements for the ratings under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 112 (Examinations, assessments and exemptions for licensing of light aircraft pilots)
(1) A person who intends to obtain a light aircraft pilot licence shall pass a written examination and a practical examination conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport on the knowledge and ability necessary to perform light aircraft piloting duties, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants a rating by category of light aircraft under Article 111 for a light aircraft pilot licence (including a light aircraft flight instructor rating under Article 115), the Minister shall assess flight experience on light aircraft and other relevant matters. In such cases, the initial category rating for a light aircraft pilot licence may be assessed by conducting a practical examination.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may fully or partially exempt any of the following persons from examinations or assessments referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2), as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
1. The holder of a licence under any of subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 35 or a light aircraft pilot licence issued by a foreign government;
2. Deleted <May 12, 2026>
3. A person who has experience in the relevant field.
(4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue a light aircraft pilot licence to a person who has passed the written and practical examinations under paragraph (1).

Article 112-2 (Prohibition of lending light aircraft pilot licences)
(1) The holder of a light aircraft pilot licence shall not permit another person to perform light aircraft piloting duties using the holder’s name, or lend the light aircraft pilot licence issued under Article 112(4) to another person.
(2) No person shall perform light aircraft piloting duties using another person's name or borrow another person's light aircraft pilot licence.
(3) No person shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (1) or (2).
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 18, 2021]

Article 113 (Medical Assessments for light aircraft pilots)
(1) The holder of a light aircraft pilot licence who intends to perform light aircraft piloting duties, or a person who conducts light aircraft flight practice under Article 116, shall obtain a Medical Assessment from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(2) Article 40(2) through (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Medical Assessment referred to in paragraph (1). <Amended on January 18, 2022>

Article 114 (Revocation or suspension of light aircraft pilot licences, ratings and Medical Assessments)
(1) Where the holder of a light aircraft pilot licence falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke that licence or the rating on that licence (hereafter in this Article referred to as "licence, etc.") or issue an order to suspend the validity of such licence, etc. for a specified period not exceeding 1 year; provided, however, that where the holder falls under subparagraph 1, 5-2, 5-3, 12, or 17, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the licence or rating: <Amended on May 18, 2021; Dec. 7, 2021; May 12, 2026>
1. Where the holder has obtained a licence or rating by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder is sentenced to a fine or more severe punishment for violating this Act;
3. Where the holder causes casualties or damage to property by causing a light aircraft accident by intent or gross negligence when the holder controls the flight of the light aircraft;
4. Where the holder is engaged in services other than controlling the flight of light aircraft, in violation of the main clause of Article 110;
5. Where the holder pilots a light aircraft of a category not covered by the rating on the holder’s light aircraft pilot licence, in violation of Article 111(2);
5-2. Where the holder permits another person to perform light aircraft piloting duties using the holder’s name, or lends the holder’s light aircraft pilot licence, in violation of Article 112-2(1);
5-3. Where the holder arranges or facilitates any of the following acts, in violation of Article 112-2(3):
a. A licence holder permitting another person to perform light aircraft piloting duties using that licence holder’s name, or lending that licence holder’s light aircraft pilot licence;
b. Performing light aircraft piloting duties using another person's name, or borrowing another person's light aircraft pilot licence;
6. Where the holder controls the flight of light aircraft or undergoes flight practice for light aircraft without obtaining a Medical Assessment, in violation of Article 113 (including cases where it applies mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 116(5));
7. Where the holder conducts flight training without obtaining a flight instructor rating, in violation of Article 115(1);
8. Where the holder fails to receive training designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 115(2);
9. Where the holder takes off or lands a light aircraft at a place other than a takeoff and landing area, or at a takeoff and landing area whose use has been suspended under Article 25(6) of the Airport Facilities Act, in violation of Article 118;
10. Where the holder controls the flight of light aircraft under the condition where the holder is unable to normally control the flight of the light aircraft (including flight practice for a light aircraft under Article 116) under the influence of alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(2);
11. Where the holder consumes or uses alcohol, etc., referred to in Article 57(1) while controlling the flight of light aircraft (including flight practice for light aircraft referred to in Article 116), in violation of Article 57(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(2);
12. Where the holder refuses a request for conducting a breath test to determine whether the holder has consumed or used alcohol, etc., referred to in Article 57(1), in violation of Article 57(3), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(2);
13. Where the holder operates a flight without conforming to flight rules, in violation of Article 67(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(3);
14. Where the holder operates a flight in uncontrolled airspace or warning airspace without adhering to the methods and procedures for flying aircraft determined and announced by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 79(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(4);
15. Where the holder flies in regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces) without obtaining permission or without complying with the flight methods and procedures prescribed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 79(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(4);
16. Where the holder fails to adhere to the order and timing of moving, takeoff and landing, and methods for flying aircraft instructed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate, in violation of Article 84(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(5);
16-2. Where the holder fails to immediately implement a measure referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 67-2(2) (including a measure taken by the Minister of National Defense under Article 10(1) of the Act on the Operation of Military Aircraft), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(6), in violation of Article 67-2(3);
17. Where the holder performs light aircraft piloting duties during a period in which the holder’s licence or rating is suspended under this Article.
(2) Where the holder of a Medical Assessment who performs light aircraft piloting duties falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the Medical Assessment or order the suspension of its validity for a specified period not exceeding one year; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the Medical Assessment where the holder falls under subparagraph 1:
1. Where the holder has obtained a Medical Assessment by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder is deemed unfit for the flight of light aircraft because the holder fails to meet the standards for Medical Assessment for each kind of licence referred to in Article 40(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 113(2);
3. Where the holder falls under any of subparagraphs 10 through 12 of paragraph (1).
(3) Where a person, who applies for an examination for a licence, etc., or undergoes a review, cheats on the examination or the review, or where a person who undergoes an aviation medical examination cheats on such medical examination, that person shall not apply for an examination for a licence, etc. under this Act or undergo a review, or an aviation medical examination under this Act for two years from the date that person cheats on such examination, review or aviation medical examination, respectively.
(4) The criteria and procedures for dispositions under paragraphs (1) and (2), and any other requirements, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 115 (Light aircraft flight instructor rating)
(1) A person who intends to provide light aircraft flight training for persons who undergo the following flight practice shall obtain a flight instructor rating for each category of light aircraft from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
1. Flight practice conducted on board a light aircraft by a person who has not obtained a licence as a light aircraft pilot;
2. Flight practice conducted on board a light aircraft by the holder of a light aircraft pilot licence, where the aircraft is of a category other than that specified in the rating under Article 111.
(2) The flight instructor rating referred to in paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "light aircraft flight instructor rating") shall be granted by issuing a light aircraft flight instructor certificate. The holder of the rating shall receive training as prescribed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(3) Articles 112 and 114(1) and (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to an examination for light aircraft flight instructor rating, the revocation thereof, etc.

Article 116 (Supervised flight training in light aircraft)
(1) A person who intends to undergo flight practice referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 115(1) shall do so after obtaining permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the flight practice and under the supervision of the holder of a light aircraft flight instructor rating, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) A person who intends to undergo flight practice referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 115(1) shall do so under the supervision of the holder of a light aircraft flight instructor rating.
(3) Article 109(1) shall not apply to flight practice referred to in paragraph (1), and Article 111(2) shall not apply to flight practice referred to in paragraph (2).
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives an application for permission for flight practice pursuant to paragraph (1), in which case the Minister deems that an applicant has the ability to undergo light aircraft flight practice, the Minister shall permit the flight practice and issue a light aircraft flight practice permit to the applicant, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) Articles 113 and 114 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Medical Assessment, etc. of the holder of a light aircraft flight practice permit issued under paragraph (4).
(6) When conducting light aircraft flight practice, the holder of a light aircraft flight practice permit issued under paragraph (4) shall carry the permit and a Medical Assessment.

Article 117 (Approval of light aircraft training organizations)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may approve a light aircraft training organization to provide training leading to qualification as a light aircraft pilot, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the holder of a light aircraft training organization approval under paragraph (1) provides training leading to qualification as a light aircraft pilot, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may fully or partially subsidize the expenses incurred in providing that training, within budgetary limits.
(3) The criteria for approval of a light aircraft training organization, including curricula, training methods, personnel, facilities and equipment, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Where the holder of a light aircraft training organization approval falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the approval; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the approval where the holder falls under subparagraph 1:
1. Where the holder has obtained the approval by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder fails to comply with the requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act among the approval criteria referred to in paragraph (3).

Article 118 (Take-off and landing sites for light aircraft)
(1) No person shall take off or land light aircraft at an area other than an aerodrome (excluding a military aerodrome) or an airfield; provided, however, that this shall not apply where unavoidable circumstances exist, such as a safety-related emergency, and permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
(2) Detailed standards and procedures for obtaining permission under the proviso of paragraph (1), and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 119 (Installation and operation of radio equipment on light aircraft)
A person who intends to use a light aircraft prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act for aviation, or the person or entity that owns or has the right to use that aircraft, shall install and operate radio equipment prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as radio communication equipment and an aircraft identification transponder, in the relevant aircraft.

Article 120 (Operating requirements for pilots and accident reporting for light aircraft)
(1) A light aircraft pilot shall comply with the requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to prevent the light aircraft operated by the pilot from causing injury to persons or damage to property.
(2) In the event of a light aircraft accident, a light aircraft pilot shall immediately report the light aircraft accident to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, where the light aircraft pilot is unable to report, the light aircraft owner as defined in Article 108(1) shall report the light aircraft accident to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 121 (Provisions applied mutatis mutandis to light aircraft)
(1) Articles 7 through 18 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the registration, etc. of light aircraft.
(2) Article 57 shall apply mutatis mutandis to restrictions on the consumption or use of alcohol, etc. with regard to light aircraft.
(3) Article 67 shall apply mutatis mutandis to flight rules of light aircraft.
(4) Article 79 shall apply mutatis mutandis to flight restrictions on light aircraft.
(5) Article 84 shall apply mutatis mutandis to compliance with instructions given to light aircraft from air traffic control services.
(6) Article 67-2 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the prohibition, etc. of the use of weapons against light aircraft. <Added on May 12, 2026>

CHAPTER X ULTRA-LIGHT VEHICLES

Article 122 (Notification of ultra-light vehicles)
(1) A person or entity that owns or has the right to use an ultra-light vehicle (hereinafter referred to as an "ultra-light vehicle owner") shall report to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, the category and use of the ultra-light vehicle, the name of its owner, whether the ultra-light vehicle is capable of collecting personal information and personal location information under Article 129(4), and other relevant matters; provided, that this shall not apply to an ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall notify the person or entity that filed the report of whether to accept the relevant report within seven days from the date of receipt of the report under the main clause of paragraph (1). <Added on June 9, 2020>
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport fails to notify the person or entity that filed the report of whether to accept the relevant report or of an extension of the processing period under the statutes and regulations governing processing of civil petitions within the period specified in paragraph (2), the report shall be deemed accepted on the day after the end of such period (referring to the relevant processing period, where the processing period is extended or re-extended under the statutes and regulations governing processing of civil petitions). <Added on June 9, 2020>
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives a report on an ultra-light vehicle pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall issue a report number to the ultra-light vehicle owner. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(5) The ultra-light vehicle owner to which the report number is issued pursuant to paragraph (4) shall indicate the report number on the relevant ultra-light vehicle. <Amended on June 9, 2020>

Article 123 (Notifications of changes and cancellation concerning ultra-light vehicles)
(1) An ultra-light vehicle owner that intends to change a matter prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the use of the ultra-light vehicle or the name of its owner reported under Article 122(1), shall file a report of the change with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall notify the person or entity that filed the report of whether to accept such report within seven days from the date of receipt of the report on changes under paragraph (1). <Added on June 9, 2020>
(3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport fails to notify the person or entity that filed the report of whether to accept the relevant report or of an extension of the processing period under the statutes and regulations governing processing of civil petitions within the period specified in paragraph (2), the report shall be deemed accepted on the day after the end of such period (referring to the relevant processing period, where the processing period is extended or re-extended under the statutes and regulations governing processing of civil petitions). <Added on June 9, 2020>
(4) Where an ultra-light vehicle reported pursuant to Article 122(1) has been destroyed or dismantled (excluding the dismantlement thereof for maintenance, etc., transport, or storage), the ultra-light vehicle owner shall file a report on cancellation with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport within 15 days from the date a reason for cancellation arises. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(5) If a report filed under paragraph (4) has no deficiency in its descriptions and attached documents and meets the format requirements prescribed in statutes, regulations, etc., the reporting shall be deemed completed at the time the report arrives at a receiving agency. <Added on June 9, 2020>
(6) Where an ultra-light vehicle owner fails to file a report on cancellation under paragraph (4), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall require the owner to file the report within a specified period of at least 30 days. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(7) Where the relevant ultra-light vehicle owner fails to file a report on cancellation even after the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has given notification under paragraph (6), the Minister may cancel the report number ex officio, and when the report number has been canceled, the Minister shall notify the relevant ultra-light vehicle owner and other interested parties thereof. <Amended on June 9, 2020>

Article 124 (Safety certification of ultra-light vehicles)
Except where permission has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in a case prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a test flight, no person who intends to fly an ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a powered flying device, shall conduct the flight unless the person has obtained, from the head of an agency or organization prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act and in accordance with the validity period and procedures and methods established by that head, safety certification that the ultra-light vehicle complies with the technical standards for flight safety determined and notified by the Minister. In such cases, the head of the agency or organization shall obtain the Minister's approval for the validity period and procedures and methods for safety certification, and the same shall apply to any change thereto.

Article 125 (Pilot certification for ultra-light vehicles, etc.)
(1) A person who intends to fly an ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a powered flying device, shall obtain, from the head of an agency or organization prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, a certificate to pilot the relevant type of ultra-light vehicle (hereinafter referred to as an "ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate"), in accordance with the qualification standards and examination procedures and methods established by that head for each type of ultra-light vehicle. In such cases, the qualification standards and examination procedures and methods for each type of ultra-light vehicle shall be approved by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, and the same shall apply to any change thereto.
(2) The holder of an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate shall not permit another person to control the flight of an ultra-light vehicle using the holder’s name, or lend the ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate to another person. <Added on May 18, 2021>
(3) No person shall use any other person's name to control the flight of an ultra-light vehicle, or borrow any other person's ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate. <Added on May 18, 2021>
(4) No person shall arrange or facilitate any act prohibited under paragraph (2) or (3).
(5) Where the holder of an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the certificate or order the suspension of its validity for a specified period not exceeding one year; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the certificate where the holder falls under any of subparagraphs 1, 3-2, 3-3, 7, or 8: <Amended on May 18, 2021; December 7, 2021; January 16, 2024>
1. Where the holder has obtained an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder is sentenced to a fine or heavier punishment for violating this Act;
3. Where the holder causes casualties or damage to property by causing an ultra-light vehicle accident by intent or gross negligence in the course of providing services as an ultra-light vehicle pilot;
3-2. Where the holder permits any other person to use the holder’s name to control the flight of an ultra-light vehicle, or lends the holder’s ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate, in violation of paragraph (2);
3-3. Where the holder arranges or facilitates any of the following acts, in violation of paragraph (4):
a. A certificate holder permitting another person to pilot an ultra-light vehicle using that holder’s name, or lending that holder’s ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate;
b. Using any other person's name to control the flight of an ultra-light vehicle or borrowing any other person's ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate;
3-4. Where the holder conducts flight without undergoing safety training, in violation of Article 125-2(1);
4. Where the holder violates the requirements applicable to an ultra-light vehicle pilot referred to in Article 129(1);
5. Where the holder operates the flight using an ultra-light vehicle while being unable to normally operate such flight under the influence of alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1), which is applied mutatis mutandis in Article 131;
6. Where the holder consumes or uses alcohol, etc. referred to in Article 57(1) during the flight of an ultra-light vehicle, in violation of Article 57(2), which is applied mutatis mutandis in Article 131;
7. Where the holder fails to comply with a request to conduct a breath test to determine whether the holder has consumed or used alcohol, etc. referred to in Article 57(1), in violation of Article 57(3), which is applied mutatis mutandis in Article 131;
8. Where the holder operates the flight using an ultra-light vehicle during the period of suspension of the validity of ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate under this Article.
(6) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate, establish, and operate facilities for ultra-light vehicle pilot certification, including practical examination sites and training facilities for ultra-light vehicles. <Added on August 9, 2017; May 18, 2021>
(7) Standards and procedures for making dispositions under paragraph (5) and any other requirements shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on May 18, 2021>

Article 125-2 (Safety training for ultra-light vehicle pilots and instructors)
(1) The holder of an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate for an ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a paraglider, shall undergo safety training.
(2) A person who intends to provide flight training using an ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a paraglider (excluding a person required to undergo safety training under paragraph (1)), shall undergo safety training.
(3) The content, timing and methods of the safety training under paragraphs (1) and (2), and any other requirements, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on January 16, 2024]

Article 126 (Approval of ultra-light vehicle training organizations)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may approve an ultra-light vehicle training organization to provide training leading to qualification as an ultra-light vehicle pilot, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(2) Where the holder of an ultra-light vehicle training organization approval provides training leading to qualification as an ultra-light vehicle pilot, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may fully or partially subsidize the expenses incurred in providing that training, within budgetary limits.
(3) The criteria for approval of an ultra-light vehicle training organization, including curricula, training methods, personnel, facilities and equipment, shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Where the holder of an ultra-light vehicle training organization approval falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the approval; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the approval where the holder falls under subparagraph 1:
1. Where the holder has obtained the approval by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where the holder fails to meet the requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act among the approval criteria under paragraph (3).
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require the holder of an ultra-light vehicle training organization approval to submit reports or documents to verify that the holder meets and continues to comply with the approval criteria under paragraph (3). <Added on August 9, 2017>
(6) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require the relevant public officials to enter the offices or other premises of the holder of an ultra-light vehicle training organization approval and inspect relevant documents, facilities, equipment, etc. to verify that the holder meets and continues to comply with the approval criteria under paragraph (3). In such cases, the inspecting public officials shall carry identification indicating their authority and present it to the persons concerned. <Added on August 9, 2017>
(7) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may implement programmes to develop ultra-light vehicle pilots, including education and training, if necessary for efficiently using such pilots and improving their operational capability. <Added on November 26, 2019>

Article 127 (Ultra-light vehicle flight restrictions and approvals)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary for the safe flight of ultra-light vehicles, the Minister may designate and notify airspace within which the flight of ultra-light vehicles is restricted (hereinafter referred to as "ultra-light vehicle flight restricted airspace").
(2) A person who intends to fly an ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a powered flying device, in ultra-light vehicle flight restricted airspace notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall obtain prior flight approval from the Minister, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, that this shall not apply to flight within a limited area prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, such as the vicinity of an aerodrome or take-off and landing area.
(3) In either of the following cases, approval of flight shall be obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport according to the procedures provided in paragraph (2), even where the approval of flight under the main clause of paragraph (2) is not required: <Added on August 9, 2017; December 7, 2021>
1. Where it is intended to fly at an altitude not lower than the altitude prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act pursuant to subparagraph 1 of Article 68;
2. Where a flight is conducted within a zone prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, such as a control zone, within the controlled airspace, regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces) or warning airspace under Article 78(1).
(4) Where flight approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport is required under paragraphs (2) and (3), if the head of a relevant state agency, etc. intends to operate an unmanned aerial vehicle in accordance with Article 131-2(2) and notifies the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of such fact in advance as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, flight approval shall be deemed to have been granted. <Added on August 27, 2019; December 30, 2025>
(5) No person or entity shall fly an unmanned free balloon that may endanger the safety of air traffic in any portion of regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces) under subparagraph 3 of Article 78(1) where aircraft flight is prohibited for reasons of safety, national defense, or similar grounds (hereinafter referred to as a "no-fly zone"), regardless of the weight of any object suspended from the balloon; provided, however, that this shall not apply in any of the following cases: <Added on December 30, 2025>
1. Where it is used by the State, a local government, or a public institution for purposes such as meteorological observation, national events, testing, or research and development;
2. Where it is used for personal hobbies or leisure activities related to daily life, such as school education, entertainment, or recreation;
3. Where flight approval under paragraphs (2) and (3) and flight permission under the proviso to Article 129(2) has been obtained.
[Title Amended on December 30, 2025]

Article 128 (Rescue-support equipment for ultra-light vehicle flights in restricted airspace)
A person who intends to fly an ultra-light vehicle in ultra-light vehicle flight restricted airspace shall install or carry equipment prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act for safe flight and prompt rescue in the event of an ultra-light vehicle accident; provided, that this shall not apply to an unmanned aircraft or any other ultra-light vehicle prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 129 (Operating requirements, privacy protection and accident reporting for ultra-light vehicles)
(1) A person who intends to fly an ultra-light vehicle (hereinafter in this Article referred to as an "ultra-light vehicle pilot") shall comply with the requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to prevent the ultra-light vehicle from causing injury to persons or damage to property. <Amended on January 16, 2024>
(2) No ultra-light vehicle pilot shall fly an unmanned free balloon; provided, the same shall not apply where the pilot has obtained permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) In the event of an ultra-light vehicle accident, the ultra-light vehicle pilot shall without delay report the occurrence of the ultra-light vehicle accident to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act; provided, where the ultra-light vehicle pilot is unable to report the accident, the ultra-light vehicle owner shall report the ultra-light vehicle accident.
(4) When the pilot of an unmanned aircraft collects or transmits information relating to the public or private life of an individual, including personal information under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Personal Information Protection Act or personal location information under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on the Protection and Use of Location Information, by using the unmanned aircraft, the pilot shall not infringe another person's freedoms or rights, and the details concerning forms, procedures, etc. shall be governed by the relevant statutes. <Amended on August 9, 2017>
(5) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the holder of an approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for nighttime flights or other special flight operations, granted as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to a pilot of an unmanned aircraft that is an ultra-light vehicle, may fly the unmanned aircraft within the scope of the approval. In such cases, the Minister shall inspect whether the unmanned aircraft complies with the safety standards for special flight operations of unmanned aircraft notified by the Minister. <Added on August 9, 2017>
(6) A person or entity that intends to apply for approval under paragraph (5) may concurrently apply for approval of flight under Article 127(2) and (3). <Added on November 26, 2019>

Article 130 (Safety improvement orders for operators of ultra-light vehicle use businesses)
Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary for the safety of an ultra-light vehicle use business, the Minister may issue to a person or entity that operates an ultra-light vehicle use business any of the following safety improvement directives:
1. Improvement of ultra-light vehicles and other facilities;
2. Any other requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to eliminate factors that interfere with the flight safety of ultra-light vehicles.

Article 131 (Provisions applied mutatis mutandis to ultra-light vehicles)
Article 57 shall apply mutatis mutandis to restrictions on the consumption or use of alcohol, etc. by an ultra-light vehicle owner or a person who intends to fly an ultra-light vehicle.

Article 131-2 (Special provisions on certain unmanned aircraft)
(1) This Act shall not apply to unmanned aircraft used for military, police, or customs purposes or to persons engaged in duties relating to the operation of such aircraft.
(2) Article 129(1), (4), and (5) shall not apply where the State, a local government, or a public institution prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act among public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions urgently operates (including for training) an unmanned aircraft owned or leased by that entity for public purposes, such as fire prevention and suppression, search and rescue, disaster and calamity prevention and response activities such as forest patrol, transportation of emergency patients, or any other public purpose prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, provided that safety management measures have been prepared as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on November 26, 2019; December 30, 2025>
(3) For the purpose of applying Article 129(3) to paragraph (2) of this Article, the "Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport" shall be construed as the "head of the competent administrative agency". In such cases, the head of the competent administrative agency shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of the report received under Article 129(3).
[This Article Newly Inserted on August 9, 2017]

CHAPTER XI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 132 (Aviation safety oversight activities)
(1) To ensure aviation safety, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require any person or entity falling under any of the following subparagraphs to submit reports or documents concerning their activities: <Amended on June 9, 2020; January 18, 2022>
1. A person or entity that manufactures or performs maintenance, etc. of aircraft, engine, propeller, components, or parts;
2. A person or entity that constructs or manages aerodromes, airfields, airports, airport facilities, or air navigation facilities;
3. Aviation personnel, light aircraft pilots, or ultra-light vehicle pilots;
4. The holder of an air traffic services certificate;
5. A person or entity that is an air transport operator (including a foreign air transport operator and a person or entity that provides transport for remuneration using aircraft of foreign nationality; hereafter in this Article, the same shall apply), an aerial work operator, an aircraft maintenance service provider, an operator of an ultra-light vehicle use business, an aircraft leasing business operator under subparagraph 22 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act, an aviation leisure sports business operator under subparagraph 27 of Article 2 of that Act, a light aircraft owner, or an ultra-light vehicle owner;
6. A person or entity that establishes or manages an approved training organization under Article 48, a dangerous goods training organization under Article 72, an approved light aircraft training organization under Article 117, or an approved ultra-light vehicle training organization under Article 126;
6-2. A medical examiner;
7. Any other person or entity that continuously uses an aircraft, light aircraft, or ultra-light vehicle.
(2) Where it is particularly necessary for the enforcement of this Act, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have public officials of the Ministry enter any of the following premises or locations of a person or entity falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), inspect aircraft, light aircraft, ultra-light vehicles, air navigation facilities, books, documents and other items, or question the persons concerned. Where the Minister considers it particularly necessary for the efficient conduct of inspections or related duties, the Minister may appoint an aviation safety expert who meets the qualifications prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to provide advice on those duties:
1. An office, factory, or other place of business;
2. An aerodrome, airfield, airport, airport facility, air navigation facility, or construction site for any such facility;
3. A place where aircraft or light aircraft are parked;
4. Aircraft, light aircraft, or ultra-light vehicles.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall regularly conduct safety inspections of airports where an air transport operator provides flight services, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(4) Before conducting an inspection or questioning under paragraph (2) or (3), the public official concerned shall notify the person or entity subject to inspection or the person to be questioned of the plan, including the date, time, reasons and scope of the inspection or questioning, at least seven days in advance; provided, however, that prior notice shall not be required in an emergency or where it is determined that such notice would prevent the purpose of the inspection or questioning from being achieved due to the destruction of evidence or other circumstances.
(5) A public official conducting an inspection or questioning under paragraph (2) or (3) shall carry identification indicating the official’s authority and present it to the persons concerned.
(6) Requirements concerning the identification referred to in paragraph (5) shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(7) Where an inspection or questioning has been conducted under paragraph (2) or (3), the person or entity inspected or the person questioned shall be notified of the results in writing.
(8) Where, during an inspection under paragraph (2) or (3), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport identifies a matter that could pose a serious risk to the safe operation of an aircraft, light aircraft or ultra-light vehicle unless urgent action is taken, the Minister may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, order the temporary suspension of the operation of the aircraft, light aircraft, ultra-light vehicle or air navigation facility, or of the duties of aviation personnel, an ultra-light vehicle pilot, or a person or entity managing an air navigation facility.
(9) Where an inspection under paragraph (2) or (3) identifies any matter that may endanger the safe operation of aircraft, light aircraft or ultra-light vehicles, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the person or entity inspected to take corrective action.

Article 132-2 (Granting and continuing oversight of exemptions and/or exceptions)
(1) Any person or entity that falls under either of the following subparagraphs and intends to use means in lieu of applying standards prescribed by this Act may apply to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for granting of exemptions and/or exceptions that secure an equivalent level of safety (hereinafter referred to as "exemptions and/or exceptions"):
1. Where it is substantially difficult to apply standards prescribed by this Act due to war, disease, a natural disaster, etc., and exemptions are urgently required for aviation safety and industry;
2. Where a person or entity falling under any of the following items has established, as an exception to part of the applicable standards, an alternative method, alternative operating procedure, alternative facility, or other measure capable of ensuring an equivalent level of safety:
a. Any person or entity that intends to obtain type certification: Korea Airworthiness Standards;
b. Any person who intends to obtain a licence: The age below which a licence cannot be obtained, as specified in subparagraph 1 of Article 34(2);
c. Any person or entity that intends to obtain designation as an approved training organization: Standards for Designation as an Approved Training Organization;
d. Any person or entity that intends to transport dangerous goods under Article 70: Procedures and methods for handling dangerous goods determined and notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under paragraph (3) of that Article;
e. Any person or entity that intends to establish and announce instrument flight procedures under Article 78(3): The standards for establishing instrument flight procedures and the procedures for announcing and approving them under paragraph (5) of that Article;
f. Any person or entity that intends to provide air traffic control services in accordance with Article 83: Scope, subject, details, and procedures for the provision of air traffic services under paragraph (5) of that Article;
g. Any person or entity that intends to obtain air traffic services certification: Air Traffic Services Certification Standards;
h. Any person or entity that intends to obtain an air operator certificate: Flight Safety Regulations under Article 77(1).
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives an application for granting of exemptions and/or exceptions under paragraph (1), considers the results of the safety risk assessment submitted by the applicant,  and determines that the means secure a level of aviation safety equivalent to that achieved by applying the standards under this Act, the Minister may grant such exemptions and/or exceptions.
(3) Any person or entity to whom the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has granted an exemption and/or exception pursuant to paragraph (2) shall maintain the exemption and/or exception as granted, including its terms and conditions.
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants an exemption and/or exception under paragraph (2), the Minister shall conduct periodic inspections to verify whether the terms and conditions under which the exemption and/or exception was granted are being maintained, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
(5) Where an inspection under paragraph (4) finds any matter that may endanger the safe operation of an aircraft, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the person or entity whose exemption and/or exception has been granted to take corrective actions.
(6) Where a person or entity whose exemption and/or exception has been granted under paragraph (2) falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the exemption and/or exception or suspend its effect; provided, however, that the Minister shall revoke the exemption and/or exception in cases falling under subparagraph 1:
1. Where the exemption and/or exception has been obtained by fraud or other improper means;
2. Where a person or entity fails to maintain the details and conditions of the exemption and/or exception granted under paragraph (2);
3. Where a person or entity fails to comply with an order to take corrective actions under paragraph (5);
4. Where the granting of exemption and/or exception causes harm to people's lives or safety, or is likely to cause substantial harm thereto.
(7) Details concerning paragraphs (1) through (6), and matters necessary for applications, standards, procedures, methods, etc. for granting of exemption and/or exception shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]

Article 133 (Publication of safety information on air transport operators)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall disclose safety information on air transport operators (including foreign air transport operators; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) including the following so that people may use aircraft in a safe manner, as prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act:
1. Information on aircraft accidents prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act;
2. Results of the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) safety assessment of the State of the Operator [limited to a State identified by ICAO as presenting a high safety risk because it does not meet the applicable safety standards];
3. Any other safety information on air transport operators prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 133-2 (Disclosure of safety investments)
(1) An airport operator or air transport operator falling within the definition in subparagraph 35 of Article 2 of the Aviation Business Act shall publicly disclose each year the details of expenditures or investments that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport recognizes as being directly or indirectly related to aviation safety (hereinafter referred to as "safety investment"), in order to promote aviation safety.
(2) The scope and items of safety investment, the standards and procedures for public disclosure, and any other requirements for the public disclosure of safety investment shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.
[This Article Newly Inserted on November 26, 2019]

Article 134 (Hearings)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall hold a hearing before making any of the following dispositions: <Amended on October 24, 2017; December 26, 2017; May 18, 2021; January 18, 2022; January 16, 2024; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. Revocation of type certification or supplemental type certification under Article 20(9);
2. Revocation of approval of type certification or approval of supplemental type certification referred to in Article 21(7);
3. Revocation of production certification under Article 22(7);
4. Revocation of airworthiness certificate referred to in Article 23(7);
5. Revocation of airworthiness approval referred to in Article 24(3);
6. Revocation of noise certification referred to in Article 25(3);
7. Revocation of Technical Standard Order (TSO) Authorization referred to in Article 27(4);
8. Revocation of parts manufacturer approval referred to in Article 28(5);
8-2. Revocation or suspension of the designation of simulation training devices under Article 39-2(5);
9. Revocation or suspension of a personnel licence or rating, a Medical Assessment, or the passing of an air traffic control aptitude test under Article 43(1), (3) or (4);
10. Revocation of instrument rating or flight instructor rating referred to in Article 43(1) as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 44(4);
11. Revocation of certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency referred to in Article 43(1) as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 45(6);
11-2. Revocation or suspension of permission for practice or a Medical Assessment under Article 47-2;
12. Revocation of a training organization approval under Article 48-4;
13. Revocation or suspension of the designation of a medical examiner under Article 50(1) (excluding cases referred to in subparagraph 8 of that paragraph);
13-2. Revocation or suspension of approval of a safety management system, etc. under Article 58-2(1);
14. Revocation of an operational qualification under Article 63(3);
15. Revocation of designation as an agency responsible for inspecting packaging and receptacles referred to in Article 71(5);
16. Revocation of designation as a training center specializing in dangerous goods referred to in Article 72(5);
17. Revocation of an air traffic services certificate referred to in Article 86(1);
18. Revocation of an air operator certificate (AOC) referred to in Article 91(1) or 95(1);
19. Revocation of a maintenance organization approval referred to in Article 98(1);
20. Revocation of approval of air operator certificate (AOC) referred to in the proviso of Article 105(1);
21. Revocation of a licence or rating under Article 114(1), or of a Medical Assessment under Article 114(2);
22. Revocation of flight instructor rating referred to in Article 114(1) as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 115(3);
23. Revocation of a light aircraft training organization approval referred to in Article 117(4);
24. Revocation or suspension of an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate referred to in Article 125(5);
25. Revocation of an ultra-light vehicle training organization approval referred to in Article 126(4).
26. Revocation or suspension of exemptions and/or exceptions granted under Article 132-2(6);

Article 135 (Delegation and entrustment of authority)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, entrust part of the Minister's authority under this Act to the Minister of National Defense, or delegate it to the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of an agency under the Minister's jurisdiction. <Amended on Mar. 19, 2024>
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate a specialized inspection agency and entrust it with duties concerning certification, approval, or inspections referred to in Articles 20 through 25, 27, 28, and 30, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, entrust the head of a relevant central administrative agency with the authority under Article 30 to approve repairs or modifications of aircraft of State agencies, etc.
(4) Deleted. <Jun. 9, 2020>
(5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the following duties to the Korea Transportation Safety Authority or to aviation-related agencies or organizations, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on August 9, 2017; October 24, 2017; November 26, 2019; June 9, 2020; May 18, 2021; December 7, 2021; January 16, 2024; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. Duties relating to the renewal of licences under Article 34-2(2) through (7), and the establishment and operation of an integrated career management system;
1-2. Duties relating to licence examinations, rating assessments and the issuance of aviation personnel licences under Article 38;
2. Duties relating to instrument rating, flight instructor rating, and the issuance of certificates referred to in Article 44;
3. Duties relating to the issuance of certificates of Aviation English Language Proficiency referred to in Article 45(3);
4. Duties relating to the integrated aviation education and training management systems referred to in Article 48(9) and (10);
5. Duties relating to the receipt, analysis, and dissemination of the voluntary aviation safety reporting referred to in Article 61;
6. Duties relating to examinations for light aircraft pilot licences under Article 112(1);
6-2. Duties relating to rating assessments for light aircraft pilot licences under Article 112(2);
6-3. Duties relating to the issuance of light aircraft pilot licences under Article 112(4);
7. Duties relating to light aircraft flight instructor rating and the issuance of certificates, and training of holders of light aircraft flight instructor ratings referred to in Article 115(1) and (2);
8. Duties relating to acceptance of a report on an ultra-light vehicle and issuance of a report number under Article 122;
9. Duties relating to reports of changes, cancellation reports, notices requiring the submission of cancellation reports, ex officio cancellation, and notification of ex officio cancellation under Article 123;
10. Duties relating to certification of ultra-light vehicle pilot referred to in Article 125(1);
11. Duties relating to the designation, establishment, and operation of facilities, including practical examination sites and training facilities, under Article 125(6);
12. Duties relating to the approval of ultra-light vehicle training organizations and verification that the conditions of approval are met and continue to be met under Article 126(1) and (5);
13. Duties relating to developing qualified pilots, including through education and training under Article 126(7).
13-2. Duties relating to safety improvement directives for ultra-light vehicle use business entities under Article 130;
13-3. Duties relating to aviation safety oversight activities (limited to ultra-light vehicle use business entities) under Article 132(1);
14. Duties relating to public disclosure of safety investment under Article 133-2(1).
(6) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the following duties to an institution or organization specializing in aviation medicine, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act: <Amended on January 18, 2022>
1. Duties relating to Medical Assessment under Article 40;
1-2. Duties relating to receipt of reports on the deterioration of physical and mental state of holders of a Medical Assessment under Article 42(2), verification as to whether standards for Medical Assessment are met and notification of the results under Article 42(3);
2. Duties relating to training of medical examiners under Article 49(3).
(7) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust duties relating to the implementation of examinations for certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency under Article 45(2) to the Korea Transportation Safety Authority or an institution or organization specializing in the evaluation of English proficiency, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on August 9, 2017; October 24, 2017>
(8) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the following duties to the Korea Institute of Aviation Safety Technology under the Korea Institute of Aviation Safety Technology Act or an institution or organization related to aviation, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act: <Added on January 17, 2017; August 9, 2017; August 27, 2019; April 18, 2023; January 16, 2024>
1. Duties relating to research to establish Korea Airworthiness Standards, flight rules, procedures and methods for handling dangerous goods, and Flight Safety Regulations referred to in Articles 19, 67, 70, and 77 in accordance with the Convention on International Civil Aviation and the Standards and Recommended Practices (SARPs) contained in its Annexes;
2. Duties relating to the analysis and dissemination of mandatory aviation safety reporting referred to in Article 59;
2-2. Duties relating to support for the formulation and implementation of a national air navigation plan;
3. Duties relating to inspections under the latter part of Article 129(5);
4. Duties relating to permission for test flight, etc. of light aircraft and safety certification under Article 108;
5. Duties relating to permission for test flights, etc. and safety certification of ultra-light vehicles under Article 124;
6. Any other duties prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act, concerning research and analysis for the safe navigation of aircraft.
(9) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the following duties to the holder of an air traffic services certificate, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act. <Amended on March 19, 2024>
1. Duties relating to the permission for activities referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 3 and 5 of Article 68 under the proviso, with the exception of its subparagraphs, of the same Article;
2. Duties relating to the provision of aeronautical information under Article 89(1);
3. Duties relating to approval of flights of ultra-light vehicles in the ultra-light vehicle flight restricted area under Article 127(2) of the Act;
(10) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust duties relating to safety training for ultra-light vehicles under Article 125-2 to an institution or organization designated by the Minister, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act. <Added on March 19, 2024>

Article 136 (Fees and travel expenses)
(1) Any person or entity falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall pay the fees prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, however, that where the relevant duties have been entrusted pursuant to Article 42-2(3) or Article 135(2), (5) through (8), or (10), the fees shall be paid to the institution entrusted with those duties. <Amended on November 26, 2019; June 9, 2020; January 16, 2024; March 19, 2024; May 27, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. A person or entity that files an application for type certification or restricted type certification or an application for modification thereof pursuant to Article 20(1)
2. A person or entity that applies for supplemental type certification pursuant to Article 20(5);
3. A person or entity that applies for approval of type certification pursuant to Article 21(1);
4. A person or entity that applies for approval of supplemental type certification pursuant to Article 21(5);
5. A person or entity that applies for production certification pursuant to Article 22(1);
6. A person who applies for airworthiness certificate pursuant to Article 23(1);
7. A person or entity that intends to obtain airworthiness approval pursuant to Article 24(1);
8. A person or entity that intends to obtain noise certification pursuant to Article 25(1);
9. A person or entity that intends to obtain Technical Standard Order (TSO) Authorization referred to in Article 27(1);
10. A person or entity that intends to obtain parts manufacturer approval pursuant to Article 28(1);
11. A person or entity that intends to obtain approval of repair or modification pursuant to Article 30(1);
12. A person who applies for a written examination and a practical examination pursuant to Article 38(1);
13. A person who intends to undergo an assessment of flight experience, maintenance experience, and other relevant experience involving aircraft or light aircraft under Article 38(2);
13-2. A person who applies to take an examination under Article 38(3);
14. A person who intends to be exempted, in whole or in part, from the examination for an aviation personnel licence and rating assessments under Article 38(4);
15. A person who intends to be issued a licence under Article 38(5);
16. A person or entity that applies for designation, modification of designation, or extension of the period of validity of a flight simulation training device pursuant to Article 39-2(1) of the Act;
16-2. A person who intends to undergo an air traffic control aptitude test under Article 42-2;
17. A person who intends to obtain an instrument rating or flight instructor rating pursuant to Article 44;
18. A person who intends to apply for an examination for certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency or to be issued a certificate of Aviation English Language Proficiency under Article 45;
19. A person who intends to obtain an aircraft flight practice permit pursuant to Article 46(3);
19-2. A person or entity that intends to obtain approval as a training organization under Article 48(1);
20. A person who intends to obtain operational qualification pursuant to Article 63(1);
21. A person or entity that intends to obtain approval for extended diversion time operations (EDTO) pursuant to Article 74(1);
22. A person or entity that intends to obtain approval under Article 75(1) for operation of an aircraft in reduced vertical separation minimum (RVSM) airspace or other prescribed airspace;
23. A person or entity that intends to obtain an air operator certificate pursuant to Article 90(1);
24. The holder of an air operator certificate that intends to undergo an inspection on any change in the safe operation system pursuant to Article 90(5);
25. A person or entity that intends to obtain a maintenance organization approval referred to in Article 97(1);
26. A person or entity that intends to obtain approval of an air operator certificate (AOC) for a foreign air transport operator under Article 103(1);
27. A person or entity that intends to undergo a flight safety inspection under Article 107;
28. A person or entity that intends to obtain safety certification of light aircraft pursuant to Article 108(1);
29. A person who applies for an examination for a light aircraft pilot licence under Article 112(1);
30. A person who intends to undergo a rating assessment for a light aircraft pilot licence under Article 112(2);
31. A person who intends to be exempted, in whole or in part, from an examination or rating assessment for a light aircraft pilot licence under Article 112(3);
32. A person who intends to be issued a light aircraft pilot licence under Article 112(4);
33. A person who intends to obtain light aircraft flight instructor rating pursuant to Article 115(1);
34. A person who intends to receive safety education of light aircraft flight pursuant to Article 115(2);
35. A person who intends to obtain a light aircraft flight practice permit pursuant to Article 116(4);
36. A person or entity that intends to obtain safety certification of ultra-light vehicle pursuant to Article 124;
37. A person who intends to obtain an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate referred to in Article 125(1);
38. A person who intends to undergo safety training for ultra-light vehicles under Article 125-2;
39. A person who intends to obtain special flight approval for unmanned aircraft pursuant to Article 129(5).
(2) Where travel is required to conduct an inspection or related activities, the applicant shall bear the travel expenses. The criteria for such travel expenses shall be prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act.

Article 136-2 (Duty of confidentiality)
No person who is or was engaged in any of the following duties shall disclose to another person confidential personal information, including medical records, obtained in the course of those duties, or use such information for purposes unrelated to those duties:
1. Duties relating to personnel licensing under Article 34;
2. Duties relating to Medical Assessment under Article 40.
[This Article Newly Inserted on January 18, 2022]

Article 137 (Deemed public officials under the Criminal Act)
Any of the following persons shall be deemed a public official in applying Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act: <Amended on January 17, 2017; June 9, 2020; January 16, 2024; March 19, 2024>
1. A person who is not a public official among inspectors referred to in Article 31(2);
2. Executive officers and employees of a specialized inspection agency, a specialized institution or organization, etc. engaged in duties entrusted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport pursuant to the provisions of Article 135(2) and (5) through (10).

CHAPTER XII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 138 (Endangering aircraft in flight)
(1) A person who crashes, capsizes, or destroys an aircraft, light aircraft, or ultra-light vehicle in flight with people aboard shall be punished by death, life imprisonment, or imprisonment with labor for at least five years.
(2) A person who crashes, capsizes, or destroys an aircraft, light aircraft, or ultra-light vehicle in flight with people aboard by committing an offence under Article 140 shall be punished by death, life imprisonment, or imprisonment with labor for at least five years.

Article 139 (Causing death or injury through an offence under Article 138)
A person who causes death or injury by committing an offence under Article 138 shall be punished by death, life imprisonment, or imprisonment with labor for at least seven years.

Article 140 (Endangering aviation)
A person who endangers aviation by destroying aerodromes, airfields, airport facilities, or air navigation facilities or by other means shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 10 years. <Amended on October 24, 2017>

Article 141 (Attempted offences)
An attempt to commit an offence under Article 138(1) or Article 140 shall be punishable.

Article 142 (Abuse of authority by pilots-in-command or pilots)
(1) A pilot in command or pilot who, by abusing the authority of that position, causes a person on board an aircraft to perform work outside that person's duties or interferes with the exercise of that person's rights shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for at least one year and not more than 10 years.
(2) The pilot in command or a pilot who commits an offence referred to in paragraph (1) by using violence shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for at least three years and not more than 15 years. <Amended on October 24, 2017>

Article 143 (Pilot-in-command leaving aircraft before evacuation)
The pilot in command (including a person who is to perform duties of the pilot in command) who deserts an aircraft, in violation of Article 62(4), shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than five years.

Article 144 (Use of uncertified aircraft, products or parts)
Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won:
1. A person who operates an aircraft for which an airworthiness certificate or noise certification has not been obtained, or for which either has been revoked or suspended, in violation of Article 23 or 25;
2. A person who manufactures or sells Technical Standard Order (TSO) articles without the required TSO Authorization, or uses such articles on an aircraft, engine or propeller, in violation of Article 27(3);
3. A person who manufactures or sells components or parts without the required parts manufacturer approval, or uses such components or parts on an aircraft, engine, propeller or component, in violation of Article 28(3);
4. A person who operates an aircraft, or uses an engine, propeller, component or part on an aircraft, engine or propeller, without the required approval of repair or modification, in violation of Article 30;
5. A person who operates an aircraft, or uses an engine, propeller, component or part on an aircraft, engine or propeller, following maintenance or related work without obtaining the required confirmation of airworthiness, in violation of Article 32(1).

Article 144-2 (Offences relating to approved training organizations)
(1) Any person who uses an aircraft, engine or propeller to provide training leading to qualification as aviation personnel falling under subparagraphs 1 through 4, 7, 7-2, and 8 of Article 35 without obtaining or renewing a training organization approval, in violation of the proviso of Article 48(1) or Article 48-2(2), shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
(2) Any person who violates Article 48-3 and falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
1. A holder of a training organization approval who permits another person to perform the activities of an approved training organization using the approval holder’s name, or lends the training organization approval certificate to another person;
2. A person who performs the activities of an approved training organization using that organization’s name, or borrows a training organization approval certificate;
3. A person who arranges or facilitates any act prescribed in subparagraph 1 or 2.
(3) An instructor at an approved training organization or a person enrolled in a training course who falsely records flight time in an aircraft flight log shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won.
[Wholly Amended on May 12, 2026]

Article 145 (Offences relating to air operator certification and approved maintenance organizations)
(1) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on May 12, 2026>
1. An air transport operator or aerial work operator that has commenced operations without obtaining an air operator certificate under Article 90(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(1));
1-2. An air transport operator or aerial work operator that operates without undergoing a validity inspection, in violation of Article 90-2(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 96(1));
2. An aircraft maintenance business entity or foreign aircraft maintenance business entity that performs maintenance, etc. on aircraft, engines, propellers, equipment or parts without obtaining or renewing a maintenance organization approval, in violation of Article 97(1) or 97-2(2).
(2) Any person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won. <Added on May 12, 2026>
1. A person falling under any of the following items, in violation of Article 90-3 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 96(1)):
a. A holder of an air operator certificate who permits another person to conduct operations under that certificate using the certificate holder’s name, or lends the certificate to another person;
b. A person who conducts operations under an air operator certificate using the name of the certificate holder, or borrows the air operator certificate;
c. A person who arranges or facilitates any activities prescribed in item a or b.
2. A person falling under any of the following items, in violation of Article 97-3:
a. A holder of a maintenance organization approval who permits another person to perform activities under that approval using the approval holder’s name, or lends the maintenance organization approval certificate to another person;
b. A person who performs activities under a maintenance organization approval using the approval holder’s name, or borrows the maintenance organization approval certificate;
c. A person who arranges or facilitates any activities prescribed in item a or b.

Article 146 (Offences involving alcohol and other psychoactive substances)
Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on June 9, 2020; May 18, 2021>
1. Aviation personnel (including a person who undergoes flight practice referred to in Article 46 or undergoes air traffic control practice referred to in Article 47; hereinafter the same shall apply) or cabin crew members who are engaged in duties under the influence of alcohol, etc. and therefore not able to normally perform aviation duties (including flight practice referred to in Article 46 and air traffic control practice referred to in Article 47) or the duties of cabin crew members, in violation of Article 57(1) (including where it is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 106(1));
2. Aviation personnel or cabin crew members who consume or use alcohol, etc. in violation of Article 57(2) (including where it is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 106(1));
3. Aviation personnel or cabin crew members who fail to undergo testing required by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 57(3), in violation of Article 57(3) (including where it is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 106(1)).

Article 147 (Offences relating to air traffic services certification)
(1) Any person who provides air traffic services without obtaining or renewing an air traffic services certificate, in violation of Article 85(1) or Article 85-2(2), shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
(2) Any person who violates Article 85-3 and falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won. <Added on May 12, 2026>
1. A holder of an air traffic services certificate who permits another person to provide air traffic services using the certificate holder’s name, or lends the certificate to another person;
2. A person who provides air traffic services using the name of the holder of an air traffic services certificate, or borrows that holder’s certificate;
3. A person who arranges or facilitates any activities prescribed in subparagraph 1 or 2.
(3) Any person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Amended on May 12, 2026>
1. A holder of an air traffic services certificate who fails to maintain an air traffic services provision system or to comply with the Air Traffic Services Certification Standards, in violation of Article 85(4);
2. A holder of an air traffic services certificate who changes an air traffic services provision system without reporting or obtaining approval, in violation of Article 85(5).

Article 148 (Offences relating to the performance of aviation duties by unqualified persons)
Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won: <Amended on January 17, 2017; May 18, 2021; January 18, 2022>
1. A person who engages in aviation duties without obtaining a licence, in violation of Article 34;
2. A person who engages in duties beyond the scope of services based on the type of licence held, in violation of Article 36(2);
2-2. Any of the following persons who violates Article 39-3:
a. A licence holder who permits another person to use the licence holder’s name to perform aviation duties, or lends the aviation personnel licence to another person;
b. A person who uses another person's name to perform aviation duties or borrows another person's aviation personnel licence;
c. A person who arranges or facilitates any activities specified in item a or b;
3. A person who violates a suspension order under Article 43 or 47-2;
4. A person who engages in duties referred to in Article 45(1) without obtaining certification of Aviation English Language Proficiency, in violation of Article 45.

Article 148-2 (Offences relating to State safety programme)
Any person who takes adverse action against a related person on the grounds of the results of analysis in violation of Article 58(6) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
[This Article Newly Inserted on June 10, 2022]
[Former Article 148-2 moved to Article 148-3 <June 10, 2022>]

Article 148-3 (Offences relating to mandatory safety reporting)
Any person who takes adverse action against a person who submits a mandatory aviation safety report in violation of Article 59(3) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
[This Article Newly Inserted on August 27, 2019]
[Moved from Article 148-2 <June 10, 2022>]

Article 148-4 (Offences relating to voluntary safety reporting)
Any person who takes adverse action against a person who submits a voluntary aviation safety report in violation of Article 61(3) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
[This Article Newly Inserted on June 10, 2022]

Article 149 (Offences relating to negligent endangerment of aviation)
(1) A person who, through negligence, damages an aircraft, light aircraft, ultra-light vehicle, aerodrome, airfield, airport facility or air navigation facility, creates a hazard to aviation by other means, causes an aircraft in flight to crash or capsize, or destroys an aircraft in flight shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Amended on January 17, 2017>
(2) A person who commits an offence under paragraph (1) through negligence in the performance of duties or gross negligence shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won.

Article 150 (Offences relating to aircraft marking)
An owner who operates an aircraft without the markings required under Article 18 or with false markings shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Amended on January 17, 2017>

Article 151 (Assignment of unqualified crew or failure to carry required crew)
An owner who assigns a person who does not hold a personnel licence to serve as a crew member on an aircraft, or fails to assign to an aircraft a crew member required to serve thereon under this Act, shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Amended on January 17, 2017>

Article 152 (Offences relating to instrument flights or flight instruction without required qualifications)
A person who violates Article 44(1) or (2), or Article 55, shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.

Article 153 (Offences relating to non-compliance with aircraft equipment and carriage requirements)
Any person who violates Articles 51 through 54 shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.

Article 153-2 (Offences relating to smoking on board aircraft)
(1) Any person who violates Article 57-2 on an aircraft in flight shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won.
(2) Any person who violates Article 57-2 on board an aircraft while it is parked shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won.
[This Article Newly Inserted on December 8, 2020]

Article 154 (Offences relating to transport of dangerous goods by air without authorization)
Any person who violates Article 70(1) shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.

Article 155 (Offences relating to unauthorized operations in RVSM and other specified airspace)
An owner who operates an aircraft within airspace falling under Article 75(1) without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 75, shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won.

Article 156 (Offences relating to the operations of air transport operators and aerial work operators)
In any of the following cases, an air transport operator or aerial work operator shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won:
1. Where it operates an aeroplane without obtaining approval, in violation of Article 74;
2. Where it operates or maintains an aircraft without complying with the operations manual or maintenance control manual, in violation of the latter part of Article 93(7) (including cases where it is applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(2));
3. Where it fails to comply with a safety improvement directive for the safety of air transport under Article 94 (including where that Article is applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(2)).

Article 157 (Offences relating to services of foreign air transport operators)
In any of the following cases, a foreign air transport operator shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on May 18, 2021>
1. Where it operates an aircraft without carrying documents on board the aircraft, in violation of Article 104(1);
2. Where it violates an order to suspend the operation of an aircraft referred to in Article 105;
3. Where it fails to comply with a safety improvement directive for the safety of air transport under Article 94, as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 106(2).

Article 158 (Offences relating to reporting and flight approval requirements)
Any of the following persons shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won: <Amended on August 27, 2019>
1. A person who fails to report or files a false report on an accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting, in violation of Article 62(5) or (6);
2. A person who causes an aircraft to depart or modifies a flight plan without obtaining approval referred to in Article 65(2).

Article 159 (Offences relating to duties of flight crew members)
(1) A flight crew member, etc. who fails to submit a flight plan or prepares and submits a false flight plan, in violation of subparagraph 4 of Article 67(2), or who flies without complying with the flight plan, shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Added on March 19, 2024>
(2) A flight crew member or any other person falling under any of the following shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won: <Amended on March 19, 2024; May 12, 2026>
1. Any person who violates Article 66, Article 67 (excluding subparagraph 4 of paragraph (2) thereof), the main clause of Article 67-2(3) (including cases where it is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(6)), Article 68, Article 79, or Article 100(1);
2. Any person who fails to comply with instructions referred to in Article 84(1);
3. Any person who fails to comply with a request for landing under Article 100(3).
(3) Where a flight crew member other than the pilot in command commits an offence under paragraph (1) or (2), not only the person who commits the offence but also the pilot in command shall be punished by a fine under the relevant Article; provided, however, that this shall not apply where the pilot in command has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant duties in order to prevent such offence. <Amended on Mar. 19, 2024>

Article 160 (Offences relating to unlawful operation of light aircraft)
(1) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won:
1. Any person who operates a light aircraft while unable to do so normally under the influence of alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(2);
2. Any person who consumes or uses alcohol, etc. while operating a light aircraft, in violation of Article 57(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(2);
3. Any person who fails to comply with a requirement to undergo testing under Article 57(3) imposed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 57(3), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(2).
(2) Any person who performs duties other than controlling the flight of light aircraft in violation of the main clause of Article 110 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
(3) Any person who operates, or permits another person to operate, a light aircraft that has not obtained the safety certification referred to in Article 108(1) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won.
(4) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than six months or by a fine not exceeding five million won: <Amended on June 9, 2020; May 18, 2021>
1. Any person who operates a light aircraft without obtaining a light aircraft pilot licence, in violation of Article 109(1);
2. Any of the following persons who violates Article 112-2:
a. A light aircraft pilot licence holder who permits another person to perform light aircraft piloting duties using the licence holder’s name, or lends the licence to another person;
b. Any person who performs light aircraft piloting duties using another licence holder’s name, or borrows another person's light aircraft pilot licence;
c. Any person who arranges or facilitates any activities specified in item a or b;
3. Any person who operates an unregistered light aircraft, in violation of Article 7(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(1);
4. Any person who operates a light aircraft on which the nationality and registration marks are not displayed or are falsely displayed, in violation of Article 18(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(1).
(5) Any person who conducts flight training without obtaining a light aircraft flight instructor rating, in violation of Article 115(1), shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
(6) Any person who fails to install and operate radio equipment, in violation of Article 119, shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won.
(7) Any of the following persons shall be punished by a fine not exceeding three million won:
1. Any person who performs the takeoff or landing of light aircraft at an area other than a takeoff and landing area or at an airfield of which use is suspended pursuant to Article 25(6) of the Airport Facilities Act, in violation of Article 118;
2. Any person who operates a light aircraft in regulated airspace (Prohibited and restricted airspaces), in violation of Article 79(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(4).

Article 161 (Offences relating to unlawful operation of ultra-light vehicles)
(1) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won:
1. Any person who operates an ultra-light vehicle while unable to do so normally under the influence of any alcohol, etc., in violation of Article 57(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 131;
2. Any person who consumes or uses any alcohol, etc. while operating an ultra-light vehicle in flight, in violation of Article 57(2), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 131;
3. Any person who fails to comply with a requirement imposed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to undergo testing under Article 57(3), in violation of Article 57(3), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 131.
(2) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on December 30, 2025; June 16, 2026>
1. Any person who operates an ultra-light vehicle that has not received safety certification confirming compliance with the technical standards for flight safety under Article 124, without obtaining an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate under Article 125(1);
1-2. Any person who operates an ultra-light vehicle (excluding an unmanned free balloon) in a no-fly zone without approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of subparagraph 2 of Article 127(3);
2. Any person who operates an unmanned free balloon in a no-fly zone, in violation of Article 127(5).
(3) Any person who flies an ultra-light vehicle without filing a report or a report of change under Article 122 or 123 shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than six months or by a fine not exceeding five million won.
(4) Any of the following persons shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won; provided, however, that persons falling under subparagraph 1-2 or 2 of paragraph (2) shall be excluded: <Amended on Dec. 7, 2021; Dec. 30, 2025; Jun. 16, 2026>
1. Any person who flies an ultra-light vehicle in airspace restricted for ultra-light vehicle flights without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 127(2);
2. Any person who, by flying an ultra-light vehicle in a control zone without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, disrupts aerodrome operations, including by delaying aircraft take-offs or landings or causing aircraft to return, in violation of subparagraph 2 of Article 127(3);
3. Any person who operates an unmanned free balloon without obtaining permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 129(2).
(5) Deleted. <Dec. 7, 2021>

Article 162 (Offences relating to failure to comply with safety improvement directives)
An ultra-light vehicle use business entity that fails to comply with a safety improvement directive for the safety of ultra-light vehicle use business under Article 130 shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won.

Article 163 (Obstruction of inspections or entry)
Any person who refuses, obstructs or evades an inspection or entry under Article 132(2) or (3) shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won.

Article 163-2 (Offences relating to exemptions and/or exceptions)
Any person who obtains exemptions and/or exceptions by fraud or other improper means, in violation of Article 132-2, shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
[This Article Newly Inserted on May 12, 2026]
[Former Article 163-2 moved to Article 163-3 <May 12, 2026>]

Article 163-3 (Offences relating to unauthorized disclosure or use of confidential information)
Any person who discloses confidential information obtained in the course of performing duties, or uses such information for purposes unrelated to those duties, in violation of Article 136-2 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
[This Article Newly Inserted on January 18, 2022]
[Moved from Article 163-2 <May 12, 2026>]

Article 164 (Joint penalty provisions)
Where the representative of a corporation, or an agent, employee, or other worker of a corporation or individual commits, in connection with the affairs of the corporation or individual, a violation under Article 144, Article 145, Article 148, any of Articles 150 through 154, Article 156, Article 157, any of Articles 159 through 163, or Article 163-2, the offender shall be punished and the corporation or individual shall also be subject to the fine prescribed in the relevant Article; provided, however, that this shall not apply where the corporation or individual has exercised due care and supervision over the relevant affairs to prevent the violation. <Amended on May 12, 2026>

Article 165 (Special provisions on the application of penalty provisions)
Where the penalty provisions of Articles 144, 156, and 163 apply, prosecution for an act for which a penalty surcharge may be imposed under Article 92 (including where Article 92 is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 106(2)) or Article 95(4) may be instituted only upon an accusation by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; no administrative fine shall be imposed for an act on which a penalty surcharge has been imposed. <Amended on January 17, 2017; May 18, 2021>

Article 166 (Administrative fines)
(1) An administrative fine not exceeding five million won shall be imposed on any person or entity that falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on November 26, 2019; December 8, 2020; January 18, 2022; December 30, 2025; May 12, 2026>
1. Any person or entity that fails to formulate and implement health promotion activity plans for student air traffic controllers, air traffic controllers, or flight crew under their authority, in violation of Article 41-2;
1-2. Any person or entity that fails to manage the fatigue of aircrew or flight operations officers/flight dispatchers under their supervision by at least one of the methods referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 56(1) (excluding an air transport operator or aerial work operator), in violation of Article 56(1);
2. A person or entity that operates a fatigue risk management system or changes important matters without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (excluding an air transport operator or aerial work operator), in violation of Article 56(2);
3. A person or entity that falls under any of the following items (excluding an air transport operator or aerial work operator), in violation of Article 58(2) or (7):
a. Any person or entity that fails to establish a safety management system or an integrated safety management system before commencing business;
b. Any person or entity that operates a safety management system or an integrated safety management system without approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
c. Any person or entity that operates a safety management system or an integrated safety management system in a manner different from that approved;
d. Any person or entity that changes important matters prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
4. Any person or entity (other than an air transport operator) that operates an aircraft without employing a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher, in violation of Article 65(1);
5. Any person or entity (other than an air transport operator) that requires a flight operations officer/flight dispatcher to be engaged in duties without providing education and training necessary to perform the relevant duties, in violation of Article 65(3);
6. Any person or entity that handles dangerous goods without complying with procedures and methods for handling dangerous goods referred to in Article 70(3);
7. Any person or entity that sells packaging and receptacles that have not undergone inspections referred to in Article 71(1);
8. Any person who handles dangerous goods without receiving training necessary to handle dangerous goods, in violation of Article 72(1);
9. Any person who conducts light aircraft flight training without receiving training, as prescribed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 115(2);
10. Any person who flies without obtaining safety certification confirming compliance with technical standards for the flight safety of an ultra-light vehicle, in violation of Article 124 (excluding cases where subparagraph 1 of Article 161(2) applies);
11. Any person who fails to file a report under Article 132(1) or files a false report;
12. Any person who makes a false statement in response to questions referred to in Article 132(2);
13. Any person or entity that fails to comply with an order under Article 132(8) suspending aircraft operations, the operation of air navigation facilities, or the performance of duties;
14. Any person or entity that fails to comply with an order to take corrective actions, etc. referred to in Article 132(9);
15. Any person or entity that fails to make public disclosure under Article 133-2(1) or makes false public disclosure.
(2) An administrative fine not exceeding four million won shall be imposed on a person who flies an ultra-light vehicle without obtaining an ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate, in violation of Article 125(1) (excluding cases where subparagraph 1 of Article 161(2) applies). <Added on December 7, 2021; December 30, 2025>
(3) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding three million won: <Amended on May 18, 2021; December 7, 2021; June 16, 2026>
1. Any person who flies a light aircraft without obtaining confirmation that it may be operated safely in accordance with the method prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 108(4);
2. Any person who flies a light aircraft without complying with the requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 120(1);
3. Deleted; <December 7, 2021>
4. Any of the following persons who violates Article 125(2) through (4):
a. Any ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate holder who permits another person to use the certificate holder’s name to pilot an ultra-light vehicle, or lends the certificate to another person;
b. Any person who uses any other person's name to pilot an ultra-light vehicle, or borrows any other person's ultra-light vehicle pilot certificate;
c. Any person who arranges or facilitates any activities specified in item a or b;
5. Any person who flies an ultra-light vehicle without obtaining approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 127(3) (excluding cases to which subparagraph 1-2 or 2 of Article 161(2), or subparagraph 2 of paragraph (4) of that Article applies);
6. Any person who flies an ultra-light vehicle without complying with the requirements prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 129(1);
7. Any person who flies outside the scope approved by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 129(5).
(4) Any of the following persons or entities shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding two million won: <Amended on August 9, 2017; June 9, 2020; December 7, 2021>
1. Any person or entity that fails to apply for registration of any modification in violation of Article 13 or to apply for de-registration in violation of Article 15(1);
2. Any person or entity that uses an aircraft without attaching the aircraft identification plate, in violation of Article 17(1);
3. Any person or entity that fails to comply with an order to comply with the amended Korea Airworthiness Standards, in violation of Article 26;
4. Any person who is not aviation personnel and who intentionally or through gross negligence causes a hazard referred to in Article 61(1);
5. Any person or entity that fails to comply with instructions given by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the holder of an air traffic services certificate for the safety of air traffic, in violation of Article 84(2) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 121(5));
6. Any person who performs duties relating to aircraft operations or maintenance without complying with the operations manual or maintenance control manual, in violation of the latter part of Article 93(7) (including cases where it is applied mutatis mutandis under Article 96(2));
7. Any person who flies a light aircraft outside the scope of operation permitted by the safety certification rating granted, in violation of Article 108(3);
8. Deleted; <December 7, 2021>
9. Deleted; <December 7, 2021>
10. Deleted. <December 7, 2021>
(5) Any of the following persons or entities shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding one million won: <Amended on August 27, 2019; June 9, 2020; May 18, 2021; December 7, 2021>
1. Any person or entity that fails to file a report under Article 33 or files a false report;
2. Any person or entity that fails to report an accident, serious incident, or incident subject to mandatory reporting or files a false report thereon, in violation of Article 59(1) (including where it is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 106(2));
3. A light aircraft owner who fails to attach the light aircraft identification plate, in violation of Article 17(1), as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(1);
4. An ultra-light vehicle owner who fails to display the report number on the relevant ultra-light vehicle or displays a false report number thereon, in violation of Article 122(5);
5. Any person who flies an ultra-light vehicle without installing or carrying equipment prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act, in violation of Article 128.
(6) Any of the following persons or entities shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 500,000 won: <Amended on December 7, 2021>
1. A light aircraft pilot or the owner of the light aircraft who fails to report a light aircraft accident or files a false report thereon, in violation of Article 120(2);
2. A light aircraft owner who fails to apply for registration of a modification or for de-registration of a light aircraft, in violation of Article 13 or 15, as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 121(1).
(7) Any of the following persons or entities shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 300,000 won: <Amended on June 9, 2020; December 7, 2021; January 16, 2024>
1. An ultra-light vehicle owner who fails to file a report on cancellation of the ultra-light vehicle, in violation of Article 123(4);
1-2. Any person who provides flight training using ultra-light vehicles prescribed by the Enforcement Rule of the Aviation Safety Act without receiving safety training conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 125-2(2);
2. An ultra-light vehicle pilot or the owner of the ultra-light vehicle who fails to report an ultra-light vehicle accident or files a false report thereon, in violation of Article 129(3).

Article 167 (Procedures for imposing and collecting administrative fines)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall impose and collect administrative fines under Article 166, as prescribed by the Enforcement Decree of the Aviation Safety Act.

